// Professional question bank. Loaded by practice.html before the app script.
// Each question is [id, section, question, [correct, wrong, wrong, wrong], explanation, docKey or URL, optionalCode].
// The id ("P001", "P002", …) is what saved progress is attached to, so never change or reuse an id.
// Give a new question the next unused number. Questions can be reordered, edited or removed freely.
const PBANK = [];
PBANK.push(
["P001", 1, "A team's Declarative Automation Bundle has grown large. They want each job and pipeline defined in its own YAML file under a `resources/` folder, while still deploying everything as one bundle. What is the supported approach?",
 ["Keep one `databricks.yml` at the bundle root and list the resource files or globs in its top-level `include` mapping",
  "Add a separate `databricks.yml` inside `resources/` so the CLI merges each folder's configuration at deploy time",
  "List the resource YAML files under the `sync` mapping's `include` key so they are loaded as configuration",
  "Reference each resource file from the matching entry in the `targets` mapping of the root configuration"],
 "A bundle has exactly one `databricks.yml` at its root, and its top-level `include` mapping (paths or globs such as `resources/*.yml`) pulls other configuration files into that single bundle.\n`databricks.yml` inside `resources/`: a second `databricks.yml` would mark a separate bundle; the CLI does not merge folders that way.\n`sync` `include`: controls which files are uploaded to the workspace, not which YAML is read as configuration.\n`targets` mapping: holds per-environment overrides such as workspace or mode; it does not reference resource files.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/settings"],

["P002", 1, "A developer deploys a bundle to a target with `mode: development`. Which behavior should they expect for the deployed resources?",
 ["Schedules and triggers are paused, and resource names get a [dev <short_name>] prefix",
  "The deployment lock is enforced, and concurrent job runs are disabled by default",
  "Deployment fails unless `run_as` is set to a service principal for the target",
  "Cluster overrides through `--cluster-id` are rejected to keep compute consistent"],
 "`mode: development` prefixes resource names with `[dev ${workspace.current_user.short_name}]` and pauses schedules and triggers, so a dev copy never runs on its own. It also allows concurrent runs and disables the deployment lock.\n`deployment lock enforced, concurrency disabled`: the opposite; development mode turns the lock off and enables concurrent runs.\n`run_as` must be a service principal: a production-mode check, not a development one.\nRejecting `--cluster-id` overrides: production mode does this; development mode allows overriding the cluster.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes"],

["P003", 1, "A bundle project uses Poetry with source in `src/my_package` and a `main` entry point. The team wants `databricks bundle deploy` to build the wheel and a classic-compute job task to run that entry point. Which configuration does this?",
 ["An `artifacts` entry with `type: whl` and `build: poetry build`, plus a task with `python_wheel_task` (`package_name`, `entry_point`) and `libraries: - whl: ./dist/*.whl`",
  "A `sync` entry that includes `./dist/*.whl`, plus a task with `spark_python_task` pointing at `src/my_package/main.py` and no `libraries` mapping",
  "An `artifacts` entry with `type: whl` and `path: ./dist`, plus a task with `notebook_task` that runs `%pip install` against the built wheel",
  "A `variables` entry holding the wheel path, plus a task with `python_wheel_task` that sets `package_name` and reads the path from the variable"],
 "The `artifacts` mapping with `type: whl` and a `build` command (`poetry build`) builds the wheel during `bundle deploy`. The task then installs it via `libraries: - whl` and runs it with `python_wheel_task` (`package_name`, `entry_point`).\n`sync` plus `spark_python_task`: `sync` only uploads files, nothing builds the wheel, and running `main.py` directly skips the package install.\n`path: ./dist` plus `notebook_task` with `%pip`: `path` points at the package source, not the output, and a notebook doing `%pip` is not the wheel task the team wants.\n`variables` holding the wheel path: a variable stores a value; it neither builds nor installs the wheel.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/python-wheel"],

["P004", 1, "A bundle defines a job with a `python_wheel_task` that must run on serverless compute. Validation flags the task as incomplete. What does the task need?",
 ["An `environment_key` that points to an entry in the job's `environments` list, whose `spec` lists the dependencies",
  "A `new_cluster` block with `data_security_mode` set so that serverless can install the wheel library",
  "A `job_cluster_key` that points to a shared job cluster with the wheel installed as a cluster library",
  "A `libraries` list with a `whl` entry only, because serverless reads task libraries in place of environments"],
 "On serverless compute, Python script, Python wheel and dbt tasks need an `environment_key` that points to an entry in the job's `environments` list; its `spec` sets `environment_version` and `dependencies` (including the wheel).\n`new_cluster` with `data_security_mode`: a `new_cluster` block defines classic compute, which is not serverless.\n`job_cluster_key`: also points to classic job compute with cluster libraries; serverless has no cluster to attach to.\n`libraries` with only a `whl` entry: serverless tasks get dependencies from the environment spec, so the `environment_key` is still missing.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/examples"],

["P005", 1, "A platform team defines some bundle jobs in YAML and others in Python with the `databricks-bundles` package. They want every job, from either source, to get the same email notifications at deploy time. Which approach fits?",
 ["Write a function decorated with `@job_mutator` and register it under the `python` mapping's `mutators` list",
  "Add a `load_resources` function that rewrites the YAML files on disk before the bundle is validated",
  "Set the notifications in a `presets` block, because Python-defined jobs ignore YAML mutators entirely",
  "Define a complex variable for notifications and reference it only from the Python-defined job classes"],
 "A function decorated with `@job_mutator` and listed under `python.mutators` runs at deploy time over every job, whether defined in YAML or Python, so it can add the same notifications everywhere. If a mutator raises, deployment aborts.\n`load_resources` rewriting YAML: `load_resources` defines resources in Python code; it is not a hook for editing YAML files on disk.\n`presets` block: presets cover things like name prefixes, tags and trigger pause status, not email notifications; and mutators do apply to YAML jobs.\nComplex variable used only in Python jobs: YAML-defined jobs would never get the notifications.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/python/"],

["P006", 1, "A Lakeflow pipeline's Python source runs `%pip install` and then calls `dbutils.library.restartPython()` to load a new package. The pipeline fails. What does Databricks recommend instead?",
 ["Declare the package as a dependency in the pipeline's Environment settings, pinning its version",
  "Move the install into a cluster-scoped init script on the serverless pipeline's compute settings",
  "Install the package as a JVM library on the pipeline so it loads before the Python process starts",
  "Call `dbutils.library.restartPython()` in a separate source file that runs before the others"],
 "Pipelines cannot restart the Python process, so runtime installs plus `dbutils.library.restartPython()` fail. Declare packages, version-pinned, in the pipeline's Environment settings; they apply to every source file and cached libraries can be reused.\nCluster-scoped init script: serverless pipelines do not support init scripts, and Databricks discourages them for dependencies.\nJVM library: pipelines support only SQL and Python, and JVM libraries cannot be used in a pipeline.\n`restartPython()` in a separate file: the restart itself is unsupported, wherever it is called.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/external-dependencies"],

["P007", 1, "A serverless notebook installs a wheel that contains compiled native code. It installs fine on some runs but fails on others with no code changes. What is the most likely cause?",
 ["Serverless compute can run on `aarch64` or `x86_64`, and the architecture can change between runs",
  "Serverless clears the environment cache at every session start, so the wheel is rebuilt each time",
  "Serverless only allows wheels from `/Workspace/` paths, so volume paths fail at random",
  "Serverless applies runtime proxy settings unevenly, so some installs reach the wrong index"],
 "Serverless compute can run on `aarch64` or `x86_64`, and the architecture can change between runs. A wheel with compiled code built for one architecture fails on the other, so pure-Python wheels are recommended.\nCache cleared every session: false; a new session does not clear the whole environment cache, and a rebuild would not cause random failures.\nOnly `/Workspace/` paths allowed: serverless can install wheels from volumes too, and that would fail every time, not intermittently.\nUneven proxy settings: not a documented behavior; the native-code detail points to architecture.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/serverless/dependencies"],

["P008", 1, "An engineer adds a pinned `pyspark` version to a serverless notebook's Environment dependencies to match their local tests. After clicking Apply, the session stops with an error. What should they do?",
 ["Remove `pyspark` and any library that depends on it from the environment dependencies",
  "Pin `pyspark` to the exact version reported by `spark.version` and apply again",
  "Install `pyspark` with `%pip` in the first cell instead of in the Environment pane",
  "Switch the base environment to ML so that a custom `pyspark` version is allowed"],
 "Serverless notebooks provide PySpark themselves and must not install PySpark or any library that depends on it; doing so stops the session with an error. Remove `pyspark` and its dependents from the environment.\nPinning the exact `spark.version`: still installs `pyspark`, which is what is forbidden.\n`%pip` in the first cell: same install through a different route, same failure.\nSwitching base environment to ML: the rule applies to serverless notebooks generally; no base environment permits a custom `pyspark`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/serverless/dependencies"],

["P009", 1, "A job on a classic cluster upgraded to Databricks Runtime 15.4 LTS can no longer install a wheel stored in the DBFS root. What is the recommended fix?",
 ["Move the wheel to a Unity Catalog volume or workspace files and grant read access to the installing identity",
  "Turn on a Spark configuration that re-enables DBFS root libraries for that single job cluster",
  "Move the wheel to a DBFS mount point, because only the DBFS root location is deprecated",
  "Convert the wheel to an egg file, because egg files are still allowed from the DBFS root"],
 "Library files in the DBFS root are deprecated and disabled by default from Databricks Runtime 15.1, and any user can modify them. Move the wheel to a Unity Catalog volume or workspace files and grant the installing identity read access.\nSpark config to re-enable DBFS root: keeps the library in a deprecated, insecure location; Databricks recommends moving it.\nDBFS mount point: mounts are a legacy DBFS pattern; the recommended homes are volumes or workspace files.\nEgg file: eggs only work on 13.3 LTS and below, so they would not install on 15.4 LTS at all.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/libraries/"],

["P010", 1, "On a Unity Catalog cluster in standard access mode, a user cannot install a Maven library that works fine on a dedicated cluster. What is required?",
 ["A user with the `MANAGE ALLOWLIST` privilege must add the Maven coordinates to the allowlist for standard access mode",
  "The user must install the library as a notebook-scoped library with `%pip` instead",
  "The library must be uploaded to workspace files, which standard access mode reads for JARs",
  "The cluster must run Databricks Runtime 12.2 LTS, the last version that supports Maven"],
 "Standard access mode only installs Maven coordinates and JAR paths that are on the allowlist, managed by a user with the `MANAGE ALLOWLIST` privilege. Dedicated mode has no such restriction, which is why it worked there.\nNotebook-scoped `%pip`: `%pip` installs Python packages; JARs and Maven libraries cannot be installed at notebook level.\nWorkspace files for JARs: workspace files are not a supported JAR source in standard or dedicated access mode.\nRuntime 12.2 LTS: Maven is supported on current runtimes; the blocker is the allowlist, not the version.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/libraries/"],

["P011", 1, "A data governance team needs a Python masking function that analysts can call from SQL in any session, that is discoverable in Catalog Explorer, and whose use is controlled by grants. Which option meets these needs?",
 ["Create it with `CREATE FUNCTION catalog.schema.fn(...) RETURNS ... LANGUAGE PYTHON AS $$...$$` and grant `EXECUTE`",
  "Define it with the `@udf` decorator in a shared notebook and call `spark.udf.register` in each session",
  "Define it as a `@pandas_udf` in a Python module stored in a Git folder that all analysts import",
  "Create it with `CREATE TEMPORARY FUNCTION` in a startup notebook that every analyst runs first"],
 "A function created with `CREATE FUNCTION catalog.schema.fn ... LANGUAGE PYTHON` is a Unity Catalog UDF: it persists across sessions, shows in Catalog Explorer, and callers need `EXECUTE`, so access is governed by grants.\n`@udf` plus `spark.udf.register`: session-scoped; it must be re-registered every session and is not governed by Unity Catalog.\n`@pandas_udf` in a Git folder module: still a session-level function; not discoverable in Catalog Explorer or grantable.\n`CREATE TEMPORARY FUNCTION`: lasts only for the session that ran it and has no grants.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/unity-catalog"],

["P012", 1, "A pandas UDF scores rows with a machine learning model. Loading the model is slow, and the team wants to load it once and reuse it for every batch the UDF processes. Which pandas UDF type fits best?",
 ["Iterator of Series to Iterator of Series",
  "Series to Series with a per-call load",
  "Series to scalar used with `groupBy.agg`",
  "A row-at-a-time Python `@udf` function"],
 "Iterator of Series to Iterator of Series receives all batches through one call, so you can load the model once before the loop and apply it to each batch. Output length must still match input length.\nSeries to Series with per-call load: the function is called per batch, so the model reloads every time, which is the cost to avoid.\nSeries to scalar with `groupBy.agg`: an aggregate returning one value per group, not a per-row score.\nRow-at-a-time `@udf`: slowest option, and nothing keeps the model loaded between calls.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/pandas"],

["P013", 1, "A team writes a batch Unity Catalog Python UDF with `PARAMETER STYLE PANDAS` and `HANDLER 'score'`. Which rule must the handler follow?",
 ["It must yield one `pandas.Series` per input batch with the same number of rows as that batch",
  "It must return a single scalar value for each batch, which is then broadcast to every row",
  "It must receive a full pandas DataFrame of the table and return a DataFrame of any length",
  "It must read shared files from the driver to cache state between the batches it processes"],
 "A batch Unity Catalog Python UDF with `PARAMETER STYLE PANDAS` gets an iterator of `pandas.Series` (tuples for several parameters) and must yield, for each batch, a Series with exactly that batch's row count.\nScalar per batch, broadcast: the output must have one value per input row, not one per batch.\nFull table DataFrame, any length: input arrives as batches of Series, and output length must match input.\nShared files on the driver: these UDFs run in an isolated environment with no shared file system access.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/python-batch-udf"],

["P014", 1, "A Unity Catalog Python UDF needs a PyPI package that is not in the default environment. The team adds an `ENVIRONMENT` clause with `dependencies`. What else must the clause contain?",
 ["An `environment_version` value, which is required whenever the `ENVIRONMENT` clause is used",
  "A `cluster_id` value, which tells Unity Catalog where to build and cache the dependency set",
  "A `requirements_path` value, which points to a requirements file stored in workspace files",
  "A `python_version` value, which must match the Python version of the calling SQL warehouse"],
 "Whenever a Unity Catalog Python UDF has an `ENVIRONMENT` clause, `environment_version` is required alongside `dependencies`; use 3 or higher, or `'None'` for the default Python environment.\n`cluster_id`: the clause has no cluster setting; UDF environments are not tied to a cluster.\n`requirements_path`: dependencies are listed inline in `dependencies`, not read from a requirements file.\n`python_version`: not a field of the clause; the version is set through `environment_version`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/unity-catalog"],

["P015", 1, "A query filters with `WHERE s IS NOT NULL AND my_udf(s) > 1`, where `my_udf` is a session Python UDF that fails on null input. It still fails on nulls. What is the correct fix?",
 ["Make the UDF handle nulls itself, or call it inside an `IF` or `CASE WHEN` expression",
  "Swap the predicates so the null check comes first, because Spark evaluates them left to right",
  "Register the UDF with `spark.udf.register` so Spark SQL skips nulls before calling it",
  "Declare the UDF return type as `StringType`, the default type that tolerates null input"],
 "Spark SQL does not guarantee the order subexpressions are evaluated, so a null check in the same `WHERE` clause may not run before the UDF. Make the UDF handle null itself, or guard it with `IF` or `CASE WHEN`.\nSwap the predicates: Spark does not evaluate left to right or short-circuit reliably, so order gives no protection.\n`spark.udf.register`: registering only makes the UDF callable from SQL; it does not skip nulls.\n`StringType` return type: the return type has nothing to do with whether the input can be null.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/python"],

["P016", 1, "A serverless job loads data into a system that is not idempotent, so each task must run at most once. Which configuration change should the engineer make?",
 ["Turn off serverless auto-optimization in the task's Retries settings",
  "Set the job's performance mode to Standard instead of Performance optimized",
  "Set the job's maximum concurrent runs to 1 in the job's advanced settings",
  "Switch the task's serverless environment memory from High to Standard"],
 "Serverless auto-optimization is on by default and may retry failed tasks, so a task could run twice. For non-idempotent work that must run at most once, turn it off in the task's Retries settings.\nPerformance mode Standard: changes startup latency and cost, not retry behavior.\nMaximum concurrent runs = 1: stops overlapping job runs, but a single run can still retry a task.\nMemory High to Standard: changes REPL memory size, unrelated to retries.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-serverless-jobs"],

["P017", 1, "A nightly serverless batch job has no strict deadline, and the team wants to lower its cost. Which setting fits, and what is the trade-off?",
 ["Disable Performance optimized so the job uses standard mode, accepting startup latency of about 4 to 6 minutes",
  "Enable Performance optimized so the job uses a cheaper SKU, accepting a longer queue time on busy days",
  "Disable auto-optimization so failed tasks are not retried, accepting a higher DBU rate for each task",
  "Enable High memory for every task so fewer tasks run in parallel, accepting slower notebook startup"],
 "Turning off Performance optimized puts the serverless job in standard mode, which uses fewer DBUs but can take about 4 to 6 minutes to start. That suits a nightly job with no deadline.\nEnable Performance optimized for a cheaper SKU: both modes use the same SKU; performance optimized is faster and costs more.\nDisable auto-optimization: that controls automatic retries, not the DBU rate.\nHigh memory: it has a higher DBU emission rate, so it raises cost.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-serverless-jobs"],

["P018", 1, "A serverless notebook task runs out of memory while building a large pandas DataFrame on the driver. Which statement about the High memory setting is accurate?",
 ["It raises total memory from 16 GB to 32 GB for the REPL, not the Spark session, and applies only to notebook tasks",
  "It raises Spark executor memory to 32 GB for every task type, including Python script and wheel tasks",
  "It doubles Spark session memory at the same DBU emission rate, and it is set in the job's compute settings",
  "It raises driver and executor memory together, and it is set with a Spark configuration in the notebook"],
 "High memory gives 32 GB instead of the Standard 16 GB, adds the memory to the REPL (where pandas runs on the driver) rather than the Spark session, has a higher DBU emission rate, and is available only for notebook tasks.\nExecutor memory for every task type: it is notebook-only and does not raise Spark memory.\nDoubles Spark session memory at the same rate: wrong on both counts; REPL memory, higher DBU rate.\nSpark configuration for driver and executor: it is an environment setting, not a Spark conf, and does not touch executors.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/serverless/dependencies"],

["P019", 1, "A serverless notebook keeps using an older cached version of a dependency even after the user starts a new session. How can they force a clean reinstall?",
 ["Click the arrow next to Apply in the Environment pane and choose Reset to defaults",
  "Detach and reattach the notebook, because a new session clears the environment cache",
  "Run `dbutils.library.restartPython()` twice so that the cached environment is rebuilt",
  "Change the base environment to ML and back, which rebuilds only the changed packages"],
 "The notebook's virtual environment is cached and a new session does not clear the whole cache. Choosing Reset to defaults from the arrow next to Apply in the Environment pane reinstalls the dependencies cleanly.\nDetach and reattach: that only starts a new session, which is exactly what failed.\n`restartPython()` twice: restarts the Python process but does not rebuild the cached environment.\nSwitching to ML and back: not the documented way to clear the cache; Reset to defaults is.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/serverless/dependencies"],

["P020", 1, "A unit test calls `assertDataFrameEqual(actual, expected)` with default arguments. Both DataFrames hold the same rows and schema, but in a different row order. What happens?",
 ["The assertion passes, because `checkRowOrder` defaults to `False`",
  "The assertion fails, because `checkRowOrder` defaults to `True`",
  "The assertion passes only if `ignoreColumnOrder=True` is also set",
  "The assertion fails unless both DataFrames are sorted with `orderBy`"],
 "`assertDataFrameEqual` defaults to `checkRowOrder=False`, so rows are compared regardless of order. Set it to `True` only when order matters.\n`checkRowOrder` defaults to `True`: it defaults to `False`.\nNeeds `ignoreColumnOrder=True`: that flag is about column order; the columns here already match.\nMust sort with `orderBy`: unnecessary, because row order is already ignored by default.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.testing.assertDataFrameEqual.html"],

["P021", 1, "A test compares two schemas with `assertSchemaEqual(actual, expected)`. The schemas differ only in the nullability of one field, and the team wants that difference to fail the test. What should they change?",
 ["Pass `ignoreNullable=False`, since nullability is ignored by default",
  "Pass `ignoreColumnName=False`, since names and nullability share a flag",
  "Pass `ignoreColumnOrder=True`, since that flag also checks nullability",
  "Nothing, since `assertSchemaEqual` always compares field nullability"],
 "`assertSchemaEqual` defaults to `ignoreNullable=True`, so a field that differs only in nullability passes. Pass `ignoreNullable=False` to make that difference fail.\n`ignoreColumnName=False`: controls whether field names are compared; it is already `False` and is separate from nullability.\n`ignoreColumnOrder=True`: relaxes column order checks; it does not check nullability.\nNothing needed: nullability is ignored by default, so the test currently passes.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.testing.assertSchemaEqual.html"],

["P022", 1, "An engineer refactors a pipeline into small functions such as `add_n(df, n)` so each can be unit tested, and wants to chain them in production code. Which call is valid in PySpark 3.3 and later?",
 ["`df.transform(add_n, 1).transform(add_n, n=10)`",
  "`df.transform(add_n(1)).transform(add_n(n=10))`",
  "`df.transform([add_n, 1], [add_n, 10])`",
  "`df.apply(add_n, 1).apply(add_n, n=10)`"],
 "`DataFrame.transform(func, *args, **kwargs)` passes the DataFrame plus any extra positional and keyword arguments to `func`; `*args` and `**kwargs` were added in Spark 3.3.0.\n`transform(add_n(1))`: calls `add_n` immediately without a DataFrame, so it errors before `transform` runs.\n`transform([add_n, 1], ...)`: `transform` expects a function, not a list.\n`df.apply`: PySpark DataFrames have no `apply` method for chaining.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.transform.html"],

["P023", 1, "A team stores tests in workspace files and runs `pytest` from a Databricks notebook. Which setup matches the Databricks guidance?",
 ["Name files `test_*.py`, set `sys.dont_write_bytecode = True`, and call `pytest.main` with `-p no:cacheprovider`",
  "Name files `*_spec.py`, set `PYTHONPATH` to the volume root, and call `pytest.main` with `--cache-clear`",
  "Name files `test_*.ipynb`, enable bytecode caching, and call `pytest.main` with `--import-mode=append`",
  "Name files `tests.py`, keep the default cache plugin, and call `unittest.main` with `exit=True`"],
 "pytest collects `.py` files named `test_*.py` (or `*_test.py`) by default. The Databricks example sets `sys.dont_write_bytecode = True` and passes `-p no:cacheprovider` so pytest does not try to write `.pyc` or cache files.\n`*_spec.py` with `--cache-clear`: pytest does not collect `_spec` files by default, and clearing the cache still uses the cache plugin.\n`test_*.ipynb` with bytecode caching: pytest does not collect notebooks, and the guidance turns bytecode writing off.\n`tests.py` with `unittest.main`: not the pytest setup, and keeps the cache plugin the guidance disables.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/notebooks/testing"]
);

PBANK.push(
["P024", 1, "A Structured Streaming query uses `foreachBatch` to append each micro-batch to a Delta table. After a driver failure, the restarted query replayed the last micro-batch and the table now contains duplicate rows. What should the engineer change so a replayed batch is not written twice?",
 ["Set the `txnAppId` and `txnVersion` write options, binding `txnVersion` to the `batchId`", "Call `dropDuplicates()` on the micro-batch DataFrame before writing it", "Switch the query to the continuous trigger so batches are never replayed", "Write with `mode('overwrite')` so each replay replaces the previous output"],
 "`foreachBatch` is at-least-once, so a batch can be replayed after failure. Setting `txnAppId` and `txnVersion` (bound to `batchId`) makes the Delta write idempotent: Delta skips a version it has already committed for that app ID.\n`dropDuplicates()`: dedupes within the batch only; it cannot see the rows already written by the first attempt.\nContinuous trigger: `foreachBatch` does not support continuous processing mode, and it would not make writes idempotent.\n`mode('overwrite')`: each batch would wipe the whole table, losing earlier batches.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/foreach"],
["P025", 1, "A streaming aggregation must upsert per-customer running totals into an existing Delta table, updating rows that already exist. Which approach does Databricks support for this?",
 ["Use `foreachBatch` and run a `MERGE INTO` against the Delta table for each micro-batch", "Write directly to the Delta sink with `outputMode('update')`", "Write directly to the Delta sink with `outputMode('complete')` and `mergeSchema` enabled", "Write with `outputMode('append')` and set `delta.enableChangeDataFeed` on the target"],
 "Delta sinks accept only append and complete output modes, so updating existing rows needs `foreachBatch` running a `MERGE INTO` for each micro-batch.\n`outputMode('update')` to Delta: not supported by the Delta sink.\n`outputMode('complete')` with `mergeSchema`: rewrites the whole result each batch; `mergeSchema` handles schema changes, not row upserts.\nAppend with change data feed: append only inserts rows; CDF records changes on a table, it does not perform upserts.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/foreach"],
["P026", 1, "A query counts events in 5-minute tumbling windows with `withWatermark('event_time', '10 minutes')` and writes to a Delta table in append mode. Analysts say each window's count appears well after the window closes. What explains this?",
 ["In append mode a window's row is written only after the watermark passes it, so output is delayed by the lateness threshold", "Append mode recomputes every open window each trigger and writes them all once the checkpoint is compacted", "The default trigger interval matches the watermark delay, so micro-batches run only every 10 minutes", "Delta sinks buffer aggregated rows until the state store is snapshotted to the checkpoint"],
 "In append mode an aggregated window is emitted only once the watermark passes the window end, because only then can no more late data change it. So each count appears roughly the 10-minute lateness threshold after the window closes.\nRecomputes and writes after checkpoint compaction: output timing is not tied to checkpoint compaction.\nTrigger interval matches the watermark: the default trigger starts the next micro-batch as soon as the previous one finishes.\nDelta buffers until state store snapshot: the Delta sink writes what the query emits; the delay comes from append mode semantics.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/watermarks"],
["P027", 1, "A query reads two streaming sources, each with its own `withWatermark`. One source regularly lags hours behind the other, and state keeps growing. What is the default global watermark, and what is the trade-off of changing it?",
 ["The minimum of the watermarks; setting `spark.sql.streaming.multipleWatermarkPolicy` to `max` advances faster but drops data from the slowest stream", "The maximum of the watermarks; setting `spark.sql.streaming.multipleWatermarkPolicy` to `min` keeps late data but grows state", "The watermark of the first source defined; reordering the sources changes which one controls state cleanup", "The average of the watermarks; setting a single watermark on the joined output removes the lag"],
 "With several watermarks, Structured Streaming uses the minimum as the global watermark so no stream's data is dropped as late; a lagging stream holds state back. Setting `spark.sql.streaming.multipleWatermarkPolicy` to `max` advances faster but drops the slower stream's late data.\nMaximum by default: reversed; `min` is the default, `max` is the opt-in.\nFirst source defined: source order does not decide the global watermark.\nAverage: there is no averaging policy.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/watermarks"],
["P028", 1, "To save storage, an engineer points two streaming queries that write to different Delta tables at the same `checkpointLocation`. What is the correct guidance?",
 ["Give each query its own checkpoint location; multiple queries should never share one", "Sharing is fine as long as the two queries write to different target tables", "Sharing is fine if both queries use the same trigger and output mode", "Sharing is fine if the queries run on separate clusters with Dedicated access mode"],
 "A checkpoint stores one query's offsets, commits, state and unique query ID, so every streaming query needs its own `checkpointLocation`. Sharing one corrupts progress tracking.\nDifferent target tables: the conflict is in the checkpoint metadata, regardless of where the data goes.\nSame trigger and output mode: irrelevant; the queries still overwrite each other's offsets.\nSeparate dedicated clusters: compute placement does not change the shared checkpoint files.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/checkpoints"],
["P029", 1, "A production stateful streaming query must be modified and restarted from its existing checkpoint without losing progress. Which change is generally safe to make?",
 ["Changing the trigger interval or the rate limits on the source", "Adding a new streaming aggregation to the query", "Changing the output sink from a Delta table to Kafka", "Changing the input path that Auto Loader reads from"],
 "Trigger intervals and source rate limits (such as `maxFilesPerTrigger`) only change how much is processed when, not what the state or offsets mean, so they are generally safe with an existing checkpoint.\nNew streaming aggregation: changes stateful operations, so the old state no longer fits; generally needs a new checkpoint.\nDelta sink to Kafka: changing the sink type generally needs a new checkpoint.\nAuto Loader input path: the checkpoint tracks files from the old path; changing it generally needs a new checkpoint.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/checkpoints"],
["P030", 1, "A CDC flow is defined with `dp.create_auto_cdc_flow(target='customers', source='cdc_feed', keys=['id'], sequence_by='ts')` and no other options. The business expected to see every past address of each customer. What does the target contain?",
 ["One row per key with only the latest values, because the default is SCD Type 1", "Every version of each row, because the default is SCD Type 2", "Nothing, because the flow fails validation without `stored_as_scd_type`", "Every version of each row, but only for columns listed in `keys`"],
 "`stored_as_scd_type` defaults to 1, which updates each key in place and keeps only the latest values. To keep every past address, set `stored_as_scd_type=2`.\nDefault SCD Type 2: no, Type 2 must be set explicitly.\nFails validation: `stored_as_scd_type` is optional, so the flow runs.\nHistory only for `keys` columns: `keys` identifies rows; it does not select history columns, and Type 1 keeps no history.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-python-ref-apply-changes"],
["P031", 1, "An engineer adds an `apply_as_truncates` condition to an AUTO CDC flow with `stored_as_scd_type=2`. What is the issue?",
 ["`apply_as_truncates` is supported only for SCD Type 1 targets", "`apply_as_truncates` requires `apply_as_deletes` to be set in the same flow", "Truncate events must be routed to a separate `append_flow` on the target", "`apply_as_truncates` is valid only when the flow is defined with `once=True`"],
 "AUTO CDC supports truncates only for SCD Type 1 targets. A Type 2 target keeps history, so `apply_as_truncates` cannot be used with `stored_as_scd_type=2`.\nRequires `apply_as_deletes`: the options are independent; the blocker is the SCD type.\nSeparate `append_flow`: an append flow does not apply truncate semantics.\n`once=True`: not a condition for truncates; Type 2 never supports them.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-python-ref-apply-changes"],
["P032", 1, "In a CDC feed, several change events for the same key can share the same `event_ts`. A second column, `event_seq`, breaks the tie. How should the AUTO CDC flow be sequenced?",
 ["`sequence_by=struct('event_ts', 'event_seq')`", "`sequence_by=['event_ts', 'event_seq']`", "`keys=['id', 'event_seq']` with `sequence_by='event_ts'`", "`sequence_by='event_ts'` with `ignore_null_updates=True`"],
 "`sequence_by` takes one sortable value; to order by several columns pass `struct('event_ts', 'event_seq')`, which orders by the first field and uses the next to break ties.\nList of columns: `sequence_by` does not accept a list; use a struct.\nAdding `event_seq` to `keys`: changes what counts as one record, so events for the same `id` stop updating the same row.\n`ignore_null_updates=True`: controls NULL handling in updates, not ordering ties.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-python-ref-apply-changes"],
["P033", 1, "A source system sends CDC updates that contain only the changed columns; every unchanged column arrives as NULL. With default settings the AUTO CDC target loses data. What fixes this?",
 ["Set `ignore_null_updates=True` so existing target values are kept when the incoming value is NULL", "Set `stored_as_scd_type=2` so NULL updates create a new version instead of overwriting", "Set `except_column_list` to the columns that may arrive as NULL", "Set `apply_as_deletes` to treat rows with NULL columns as deletes"],
 "By default `ignore_null_updates` is `False`, so a NULL in an update overwrites the target value. Setting it to `True` keeps the existing target value whenever the incoming column is NULL, which fits partial-column updates.\n`stored_as_scd_type=2`: each new version would still carry the NULLs.\n`except_column_list`: drops those columns from the target entirely rather than preserving their values.\n`apply_as_deletes`: would delete the rows, the opposite of a partial update.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-python-ref-apply-changes"],
["P034", 1, "A dashboard reads an SCD Type 2 table maintained by an AUTO CDC flow and must show only the currently active version of each record. Which filter is correct?",
 ["`WHERE __END_AT IS NULL`", "`WHERE __START_AT IS NULL`", "`WHERE __END_AT = current_timestamp()`", "`WHERE __IS_CURRENT = true`"],
 "AUTO CDC SCD Type 2 targets get `__START_AT` and `__END_AT`; the active version is the one not yet closed, so its `__END_AT` is NULL.\n`__START_AT IS NULL`: every version has a start value.\n`__END_AT = current_timestamp()`: closed rows hold the sequence value of the change that ended them, and the current row has NULL.\n`__IS_CURRENT = true`: AUTO CDC does not create an `__IS_CURRENT` column.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/cdc"],
["P035", 1, "A team prototypes pipelines on open-source Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines and plans to use the same code for an SCD Type 2 CDC flow with `create_auto_cdc_flow`. What should they know?",
 ["AUTO CDC is not supported by Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines; it needs Lakeflow pipelines on serverless or the Pro or Advanced edition", "AUTO CDC works the same in both, but SCD Type 2 requires the Advanced edition on Databricks", "AUTO CDC is supported in both, but open-source pipelines must use the older APPLY CHANGES syntax", "AUTO CDC is supported in both, but only from SQL; the Python API is Databricks-only"],
 "The AUTO CDC APIs, including `create_auto_cdc_flow`, are not part of open-source Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines. On Databricks they need serverless pipelines or the Pro or Advanced edition.\nWorks the same, SCD2 needs Advanced: it does not work in open source at all, and Pro also supports AUTO CDC.\nOlder APPLY CHANGES syntax in open source: APPLY CHANGES is the earlier Databricks name for the same feature, also not in open source.\nSQL only in open source: neither SQL nor Python AUTO CDC is in open source.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/cdc"],
["P036", 1, "A Lakeflow pipeline already writes events to an external Kafka topic through a sink created with `dp.create_sink()`. The team now wants to send the output of an AUTO CDC flow to that same sink. What is the constraint?",
 ["Only `append_flow` and `update_flow` can write to sinks; `create_auto_cdc_flow` cannot target a sink", "Sinks accept AUTO CDC flows only when `stored_as_scd_type` is 1", "Sinks accept AUTO CDC flows only when defined in SQL rather than Python", "Sinks accept AUTO CDC flows only if the pipeline runs in continuous mode"],
 "Only append flows (`append_flow`) and update flows (`update_flow`) can write to a sink created with `dp.create_sink()`. Other flow types, including `create_auto_cdc_flow`, are not supported.\nSCD Type 1 only: no SCD type makes AUTO CDC work with sinks.\nSQL rather than Python: the restriction is on the flow type, not the language.\nContinuous mode: pipeline mode does not change which flows can write to sinks.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/ldp-sinks"],
["P037", 1, "A streaming table joins a stream of orders to a product dimension table. When a product's category is corrected, finance requires that all historical orders reflect the new category. What should the engineer do?",
 ["Define the joined result as a materialized view so it stays consistent with the current source data", "Keep the streaming table and enable change data feed on the product table", "Keep the streaming table and switch the pipeline to continuous mode", "Keep the streaming table and add a watermark on the orders stream"],
 "A streaming table join processes each order once against the dimension as it was then; later dimension changes do not recompute old rows. A materialized view keeps results correct against current source data at each update.\nChange data feed on the product table: CDF records changes but does not make the streaming table recompute past joins.\nContinuous mode: makes updates more frequent but still never revisits already processed orders.\nWatermark on orders: limits late-data state; it does not reprocess history.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/streaming-tables"],
["P038", 1, "A fraud team needs newly arriving card transactions processed incrementally with the lowest possible latency and high throughput. Which dataset type best fits, and why?",
 ["A streaming table, because materialized view refresh latency is in seconds or minutes and not meant for low-latency use", "A materialized view, because incremental refresh processes only new rows with millisecond latency", "A materialized view, because it is always correct and so needs no streaming source", "A view, because it is computed on read and adds no refresh latency"],
 "Streaming tables process new rows incrementally with high throughput and low latency. Materialized views refresh with latency in seconds or minutes and are not designed for low-latency use.\nMaterialized view with millisecond latency: refresh latency is seconds to minutes.\nMaterialized view always correct: correctness against changing sources is not the need here; latency is.\nView computed on read: a view recomputes its query each time it is read and does not process arrivals incrementally.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/materialized-views"],
["P039", 1, "An engineer fixes a bug in the transformation logic of a streaming table and runs a normal pipeline update. Rows ingested last month still show the old, wrong values. Why, and what is the fix?",
 ["Rows already appended are not re-queried after code changes; run a full refresh of the table", "The pipeline cached the old plan; restart the pipeline compute and run another update", "The checkpoint is corrupt; delete the checkpoint folder and run a normal update", "Streaming tables apply code changes only to partitions newer than the change; run `OPTIMIZE`"],
 "A streaming table processes each input row once; rows already appended are not re-queried when the code changes. A full refresh truncates the table and reprocesses all input with the fixed logic.\nRestart compute: the plan is not the issue; old rows are simply never reprocessed.\nCorrupt checkpoint: nothing is corrupt, and a normal update would still skip old input.\n`OPTIMIZE`: compacts files and does not recompute values; code changes are not partition-based.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/streaming-tables"],
["P040", 1, "An Auto Loader stream reading JSON has `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` set and no explicit schema. A new field appears in the source files and the query stops. How does Auto Loader behave in this default mode?",
 ["It adds the column to the schema, fails with `UnknownFieldException`, and resumes with the new column after a restart", "It ignores the new column and continues, storing nothing about it", "It writes the new field to `_rescued_data` and continues without failing", "It fails and will not restart until the schema is changed or the file is removed"],
 "With no schema provided, `cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode` defaults to `addNewColumns`: Auto Loader adds the new column to the schema location, fails with `UnknownFieldException`, and picks it up on restart. Run it in a Lakeflow Job that restarts it.\nIgnore and continue: that is `none` mode.\nWrite to `_rescued_data` and keep going: that is `rescue` mode.\nFails until schema edited: that is `failOnNewColumns`; `addNewColumns` succeeds on restart.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/schema"],
["P041", 1, "An Auto Loader stream ingests CSV files with schema inference. Downstream users complain that numeric and date columns arrive as strings. Without supplying a full schema, what should the engineer set?",
 ["`cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes` to `true`", "`cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode` to `addNewColumnsWithTypeWidening`", "`cloudFiles.schemaLocation` to the same path as the checkpoint", "`rescuedDataColumn` to a new column name"],
 "For JSON, CSV and XML, Auto Loader infers every column as string by default. Setting `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes` to `true` makes it infer numeric, date and other types.\n`addNewColumnsWithTypeWidening`: governs how the schema evolves when new data arrives; it does not change the initial string inference.\n`schemaLocation` equal to checkpoint path: only where the inferred schema is stored, not how types are inferred.\n`rescuedDataColumn`: captures data that does not match the schema; columns stay strings.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/schema"],
["P042", 1, "A Lakeflow pipeline only needs its tables refreshed once an hour, and the priority is to keep compute cost low. How should it be configured?",
 ["Triggered pipeline mode, scheduled hourly by a Lakeflow Job", "Continuous pipeline mode with `pipelines.trigger.interval` set to 1 hour", "Continuous pipeline mode with autoscaling set to a minimum of zero workers", "Triggered pipeline mode with every table changed to a streaming table"],
 "A triggered pipeline processes available data, then stops its compute; scheduling it hourly with a Lakeflow Job means you pay only during each update. Continuous pipelines keep compute running all the time.\nContinuous with `pipelines.trigger.interval` of 1 hour: changes how often it processes, but the cluster still runs between updates.\nContinuous with autoscaling to zero workers: continuous mode still keeps compute running.\nTriggered with all streaming tables: triggered mode is right, but the saving comes from stopping compute; converting dataset types is unnecessary.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/pipeline-mode"],
["P043", 1, "A For each task receives a JSON array of objects, each with a `table` key (for example sales and orders). The nested notebook task needs the table name for the current iteration. Which parameter value is correct, and how many iterations run at once by default?",
 ["`{{input.table}}`; concurrency defaults to 1", "`{{input.table}}`; concurrency defaults to the number of inputs", "`{{tasks.input.values.table}}`; concurrency defaults to 1", "`{{job.parameters.table}}`; concurrency defaults to 10"],
 "Inside a For each nested task, `{{input}}` is the current element and `{{input.<key>}}` reads a field of an object element, so `{{input.table}}`. Concurrency defaults to 1, so iterations run one at a time unless you raise it.\n`{{input.table}}` with concurrency equal to inputs: the reference is right but the default concurrency is 1.\n`{{tasks.input.values.table}}`: that pattern is for task values set by an upstream task, not the current iteration.\n`{{job.parameters.table}}`: a job parameter is fixed for the run, not per iteration, and the default is not 10.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/for-each"],
["P044", 1, "An If/else condition task compares the task value `{{tasks.check.values.version}}`, which resolves to `12.0`, with the literal `12` using the `==` operator. The false branch runs. Why?",
 ["`==` performs a string comparison, so `12.0` and `12` are not equal", "Task values are always passed as booleans, so the comparison is false", "`==` compares numerically but rounds the left operand down first", "Condition tasks cannot read task values, so the operand resolves to empty"],
 "In an If/else condition task, `==` and `!=` compare their operands as strings, so `12.0` and `12` are different strings and the false branch runs. Only `>`, `>=`, `<` and `<=` compare numerically.\nTask values always booleans: task values keep their type; here it resolved to `12.0`.\nNumeric comparison with rounding: `==` is a string comparison with no rounding.\nCannot read task values: condition tasks can reference `{{tasks.<name>.values.<key>}}`; it resolved fine.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/if-else"],
["P045", 1, "A CI script needs to run a one-time workload from a JSON definition without creating a saved job that appears in the Jobs UI. Which Databricks CLI command fits?",
 ["`databricks jobs submit`", "`databricks jobs run-now`", "`databricks jobs create --json @job.json`", "`databricks bundle deploy`"],
 "`databricks jobs submit` runs a one-time workload from a definition without saving a job, so nothing appears in the Jobs UI.\n`jobs run-now`: triggers a job that already exists.\n`jobs create`: saves a job that shows in the UI, the opposite of what is wanted.\n`bundle deploy`: deploys saved jobs and pipelines to the workspace; it does not run a one-off workload.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/automate"],
["P046", 1, "A Lakeflow Job has three parallel ingestion tasks followed by a cleanup task that must release resources whether the upstream tasks succeed or fail. Which Run if setting should the cleanup task use?",
 ["All done", "None failed", "At least one succeeded", "All succeeded"],
 "`All done` runs the task once every dependency has finished, whatever their status, so cleanup always runs. `All succeeded` is the default.\n`None failed`: skips the task if any upstream failed, which is when cleanup matters most.\n`At least one succeeded`: skips cleanup when all three fail.\n`All succeeded`: the default; any failure skips cleanup.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if"]
);

PBANK.push(
["P047", 2, "A streaming job must read every Kafka topic whose name starts with `orders-`, including topics created after the stream starts. Along with `kafka.bootstrap.servers`, which option should be set?", ["`subscribePattern` set to the Java regex `orders-.*`", "`subscribe` set to the wildcard string `orders-*`", "`assign` set to a JSON map of `orders-` partitions", "`topicPrefix` set to the string `orders-`"], "Kafka reads need exactly one of `subscribe`, `subscribePattern` or `assign`. Only `subscribePattern` takes a Java regex, so `orders-.*` matches existing and newly created topics.\n`subscribe` with `orders-*`: `subscribe` takes a comma-separated list of literal names; no wildcards.\n`assign`: a fixed JSON of specific topic partitions, so new topics are never picked up.\n`topicPrefix`: not a Kafka source option.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/kafka"],
["P048", 2, "A team reads JSON events from Kafka with `spark.readStream.format('kafka')` and then calls `from_json(col('value'), schema)`. The call fails on type. What explains this?", ["The `value` column is binary and must be cast to string before JSON parsing", "The `value` column is a struct and must be flattened before JSON parsing", "The `value` column is a map and must be exploded before JSON parsing", "The `value` column is null until the `kafka.deserializer` option is set"], "The Kafka source always delivers `key` and `value` as binary byte arrays. Cast `value` to string (or use `from_avro` for Avro) before calling `from_json`.\nStruct to flatten: `value` is binary, not a struct.\nMap to explode: `value` is binary, not a map.\n`kafka.deserializer` option: Spark does not let you set Kafka deserializers; values are always bytes and you convert them with DataFrame operations.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/kafka"],
["P049", 2, "An engineer configures a Kinesis streaming read and sets both `streamName` and `streamARN` for the same stream to be safe. What is the correct guidance?", ["Specify either `streamName` or `streamARN`, but not both", "Specify both, because `streamARN` is required for cross-account reads", "Specify only `streamName`, because `streamARN` is a sink option", "Specify both, because `streamName` only sets the checkpoint label"], "The Kinesis source identifies the stream by `streamName` or `streamARN`, and you must specify exactly one, not both, in all Databricks Runtime versions.\nBoth for cross-account reads: the rule is one or the other, never both.\n`streamARN` is a sink option: it is a valid source option for identifying the stream.\n`streamName` sets a checkpoint label: it names the stream to read.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/kinesis"],
["P050", 2, "A pipeline reads from Google Pub/Sub using the `pubsub` format. Which statement about delivery should shape the downstream design?", ["Rows are processed exactly once from the subscriber, but Pub/Sub can publish duplicates and out-of-order rows", "Rows are processed at most once, so a replay job must restore any messages lost on restart", "Rows are deduplicated and ordered by `messageId`, so no downstream handling is needed", "Rows are processed at least once, and ordering is guaranteed only within one subscription"], "The Pub/Sub connector processes rows from the subscriber exactly once, but Pub/Sub itself may publish duplicates and deliver out of order. Downstream code must handle deduplication and ordering.\nAt most once with lost messages: the connector gives exactly-once processing from the subscriber.\nDeduplicated and ordered by `messageId`: the connector does not dedupe or order for you.\nAt least once, ordered per subscription: it is exactly-once processing, and ordering is not guaranteed.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/connect/streaming/pub-sub"],
["P051", 2, "A team needs to load only `.jpg` files from a partitioned storage path as raw bytes, keeping partition discovery. Which approach and resulting columns are correct?", ["`binaryFile` format with `pathGlobFilter`; columns `path`, `modificationTime`, `length`, `content`", "`binaryFile` format with `recursiveFileLookup`; columns `path`, `size`, `bytes`, `partition`", "`image` format with `pathGlobFilter`; columns `origin`, `height`, `width`, `data`", "`text` format with `wholetext`; columns `path`, `modificationTime`, `value`, `length`"], "The `binaryFile` source reads each file as one row with `path`, `modificationTime`, `length` and `content`. `pathGlobFilter` (such as `*.jpg`) filters file names while keeping partition discovery.\n`recursiveFileLookup`: disables partition discovery, and the column names listed are wrong.\n`image` format: decodes images into an image struct rather than raw bytes, and does not give the binary file columns.\n`text` with `wholetext`: reads files as text, not raw bytes.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/query/formats/binary"],
["P052", 2, "In a Lakeflow pipeline, an engineer writes `CREATE FLOW f AS AUTO CDC INTO silver.customers FROM STREAM(bronze.cdc) KEYS (id) SEQUENCE BY seq;` with no `STORED AS` clause. How is the target maintained?", ["As SCD type 1, overwriting rows in place with no history columns", "As SCD type 2, adding `__START_AT` and `__END_AT` columns", "As an append-only table that keeps every change event", "As BITEMPORAL, keeping both system and valid time"], "In `AUTO CDC INTO`, `STORED AS` is optional and defaults to SCD type 1: each key is updated in place, with no history and no `__START_AT` or `__END_AT` columns.\nSCD type 2 with history columns: only when `STORED AS SCD TYPE 2` is written.\nAppend-only of every event: AUTO CDC applies changes by key; it does not just append events.\nBITEMPORAL: not the default storage for AUTO CDC.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-sql-ref-apply-changes-into"],
["P053", 2, "A CDC feed has ties on `event_ts`, which are broken by `log_offset`. How should ordering be declared in an `AUTO CDC INTO` flow?", ["`SEQUENCE BY STRUCT(event_ts, log_offset)`", "`SEQUENCE BY event_ts, log_offset`", "`SEQUENCE BY event_ts TIEBREAK log_offset`", "`KEYS (event_ts, log_offset)` with no `SEQUENCE BY`"], "`SEQUENCE BY` takes one sortable column. To order by several, wrap them in `STRUCT(event_ts, log_offset)`, which orders by the first field and then the next on ties.\n`SEQUENCE BY event_ts, log_offset`: a column list is not accepted; it must be a single expression.\n`TIEBREAK`: no such keyword.\n`KEYS (event_ts, log_offset)` without `SEQUENCE BY`: `KEYS` identifies records, and `SEQUENCE BY` is required.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-sql-ref-apply-changes-into"],
["P054", 2, "A source system occasionally emits a TRUNCATE event. The team wants to use `APPLY AS TRUNCATE WHEN` in an AUTO CDC flow that keeps full history. What happens?", ["It is not possible, because truncate is supported only for SCD type 1", "It works, and closes every open row by setting `__END_AT`", "It works, but only when `IGNORE NULL UPDATES` is also set", "It is not possible, because truncate needs `APPLY AS DELETE WHEN` first"], "`APPLY AS TRUNCATE WHEN` is supported only for SCD type 1. Keeping full history means SCD type 2, which does not support truncate.\nWorks by closing rows with `__END_AT`: Type 2 has no truncate operation at all.\nWorks with `IGNORE NULL UPDATES`: that clause is about NULL values and does not enable truncate.\nNeeds `APPLY AS DELETE WHEN`: it is unsupported because of the SCD type, not because of a missing delete clause.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-sql-ref-apply-changes-into"],
["P055", 2, "An SCD type 2 target built with `dp.create_auto_cdc_flow()` grows quickly because `last_login` changes constantly. The team wants new versions only when other columns change. What should they set?", ["`track_history_except_column_list=['last_login']`", "`except_column_list=['last_login']`", "`ignore_null_updates=True`", "`stored_as_scd_type='1'` for `last_login` only"], "In SCD type 2 any column change creates a new version. `track_history_except_column_list=['last_login']` (`TRACK HISTORY ON * EXCEPT` in SQL) keeps `last_login` in the table but stops its changes from creating versions.\n`except_column_list`: removes the column from the target table altogether.\n`ignore_null_updates=True`: only keeps old values when incoming ones are NULL; real changes still create versions.\n`stored_as_scd_type` per column: the SCD type applies to the whole target, not one column.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-python-ref-apply-changes"],
["P056", 2, "A team wants external Iceberg v3 clients to read streaming tables produced by a Lakeflow pipeline. Which statement is correct?", ["Set Iceberg properties such as `delta.universalFormat.enabledFormats = 'iceberg'` and enable external metadata; external writes are not supported", "Recreate the streaming tables `USING ICEBERG`; external clients can then read and write them", "Enable change data feed on each table; Iceberg clients read the feed as snapshots", "Publish the tables through a share; Iceberg clients cannot read pipeline datasets otherwise"], "To let external Iceberg clients read pipeline datasets, set table properties including column mapping, `delta.universalFormat.enabledFormats = 'iceberg'` and `delta.enableIcebergCompatV3`, plus `pipelines.externalMetadata.enabled`. External writes to pipeline datasets are not supported.\n`USING ICEBERG` with read and write: external clients cannot write to pipeline datasets.\nChange data feed: CDF records row changes; it does not expose Iceberg metadata to clients.\nShare required: Iceberg reads come from the UniForm properties, not Delta Sharing.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/external-access/external-for-pipelines"],
["P057", 2, "A SQL Server source has some tables with primary keys and some without. Lakeflow Connect will ingest all of them with minimal source load. Which change tracking setup matches Databricks guidance?", ["Change tracking for tables with primary keys, and CDC for tables without", "CDC for all tables, because change tracking cannot be used with Lakeflow Connect", "Change tracking for all tables, because it adds keys when none exist", "CDC for tables with primary keys, and change tracking for tables without"], "Change tracking is lightweight on the source but needs a primary key; CDC works without one but adds more load. So use change tracking wherever a primary key exists and CDC for the rest.\nCDC for all: change tracking is supported, and CDC everywhere adds unnecessary load.\nChange tracking for all: it does not add keys, so tables without one cannot use it.\nCDC with keys, change tracking without: reversed; change tracking is the one that requires a key.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/sql-server-source-setup"],
["P058", 2, "A PostgreSQL table has no primary key and must be ingested with the Lakeflow Connect PostgreSQL connector. Which source setting is required for it?", ["`REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` on the table", "`REPLICA IDENTITY DEFAULT` on the table", "`wal_level = replica` on the server", "A replication slot using the `wal2json` plugin"], "Without a primary key, PostgreSQL cannot identify changed rows in the WAL unless the table uses `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL`, which logs the whole old row. FULL is also needed for TOASTable columns.\n`REPLICA IDENTITY DEFAULT`: uses the primary key, which this table lacks.\n`wal_level = replica`: the connector needs `logical`.\n`wal2json` slot: only the `pgoutput` plugin is supported.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/postgresql-source-setup"],
["P059", 2, "A DBA prepares a MySQL instance for the Lakeflow Connect MySQL connector. Which binary log configuration is required?", ["Binary logging on, format `ROW`, row image `FULL`", "Binary logging on, format `MIXED`, row image `MINIMAL`", "Binary logging on, format `STATEMENT`, row image `FULL`", "Binary logging on, format `ROW`, value options `PARTIAL_JSON`"], "The MySQL connector reads row changes from the binary log, so binlog must be on with format `ROW` and row image `FULL`, and `binlog_row_value_options` left empty.\n`MIXED` with `MINIMAL`: mixed format may log statements, and minimal images omit columns.\n`STATEMENT`: logs SQL statements, not row changes.\n`PARTIAL_JSON`: not supported; the value options must be empty.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/mysql-source-setup"],
["P060", 2, "A team reviews costs for a Lakeflow Connect database connector (standard gateway setup). Which statement about compute is correct?", ["The ingestion gateway runs continuously on classic compute; the ingestion pipeline runs on serverless", "The ingestion gateway and ingestion pipeline both run on serverless compute", "The ingestion gateway runs on serverless; the ingestion pipeline runs on classic compute", "The ingestion gateway runs only when the ingestion pipeline is triggered"], "The ingestion gateway runs continuously on classic compute to capture changes before source logs are truncated, so you pay for it even when the pipeline is idle. The ingestion pipeline runs on serverless.\nBoth serverless: in the standard setup the gateway is classic compute.\nGateway serverless, pipeline classic: reversed.\nGateway runs only when triggered: it runs continuously so it does not miss changes.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/cdc-overview"],
["P061", 2, "A Lakeflow Connect pipeline must keep prior versions of changed source rows. What is the default, and how is history enabled?", ["Default is SCD type 1; set `scd_type: SCD_TYPE_2` in `table_configuration`", "Default is SCD type 2; set `scd_type: SCD_TYPE_1` to turn history off", "Default is SCD type 1; add an AUTO CDC flow on top of the destination", "Default is append-only; set `history: true` on the ingestion gateway"], "Lakeflow Connect destinations default to SCD type 1 (history off). Set `scd_type: SCD_TYPE_2` in `table_configuration`, or turn on History tracking in the UI, to keep prior versions.\nDefault SCD type 2: the default is type 1.\nExtra AUTO CDC flow on top: the connector tracks history natively; another pipeline is unnecessary.\nAppend-only with `history: true` on the gateway: the default is SCD type 1, and history is set per table in `table_configuration`, not on the gateway.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/scd"],
["P062", 2, "A provider shares data with a partner that uses Databricks with Unity Catalog. Which SQL creates the recipient?", ["`CREATE RECIPIENT partner USING ID 'aws:us-west-2:<metastore-uuid>';`", "`CREATE RECIPIENT partner USING TOKEN 'aws:us-west-2:<metastore-uuid>';`", "`CREATE RECIPIENT partner USING ID '<workspace-id>';`", "`CREATE RECIPIENT partner USING SHARE '<cloud>.<region>.<account-id>';`"], "For a Databricks-to-Databricks recipient, create it with the recipient metastore's sharing identifier, `<cloud>:<region>:<uuid>`, via `USING ID`.\n`USING TOKEN`: token recipients are for open sharing with non-Databricks users.\nWorkspace ID: the identifier is the metastore sharing identifier, not a workspace ID.\n`USING SHARE` with account ID: not valid syntax, and the identifier format is wrong.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/opensharing/create-recipient"],
["P063", 2, "A provider creates an open (bearer token) recipient for a partner who does not use Databricks. Which statement is correct?", ["The credential file can be downloaded only once, and the token is valid for at most one year", "The credential file can be downloaded many times, and the token never expires by default", "The partner signs in with a Databricks account, and no token is issued", "The partner receives a sharing identifier and mounts the share as a catalog"], "Open sharing gives the recipient a credential file with a bearer token. It can be downloaded only once, and tokens are valid for at most one year; rotation issues a new token and activation link.\nDownload many times, never expires: one download only, and lifetime is capped at one year.\nSign in with a Databricks account, no token: that is Databricks-to-Databricks sharing.\nSharing identifier mounted as a catalog: also Databricks-to-Databricks; this partner does not use Databricks.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/opensharing/create-recipient-token"],
["P064", 2, "A Databricks recipient wants to query the change data feed of a shared table with `table_changes()`. What must the provider do?", ["Enable change data feed on the table, then add it to the share `WITH HISTORY`", "Add the table to the share `WITHOUT HISTORY` and grant `MODIFY` to the recipient", "Add the table with a `PARTITION` clause and grant `SELECT` on the table", "Enable change data feed on the share, then grant `USE SHARE` to the recipient"], "`table_changes()` reads a table's change data feed, so CDF must be enabled on the table and the table added to the share `WITH HISTORY`. History sharing also enables time travel and streaming reads.\n`WITHOUT HISTORY` plus `MODIFY`: no history means no CDF access, and recipients get read access, not `MODIFY`.\n`PARTITION` clause: limits which rows are shared; it does not share history.\nCDF on the share: CDF is a table property, not a share setting.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/opensharing/create-share"],
["P065", 2, "In a clean room, collaborator A uploads a notebook and names collaborator B as the runner. Collaborators B and C have not reviewed it. What must happen before it runs?", ["B and C must approve it, then only B can run it", "Only A must approve it, then any collaborator can run it", "B must approve it, then A or B can run it", "A, B and C must approve it, then only A can run it"], "Every collaborator except the uploader must approve a clean room notebook, so B and C must approve. Only the runner the uploader designated, B, can run it, and a runner who did not upload it must approve first.\nOnly A approves, anyone runs: the uploader's approval is not needed, others' is, and only the runner can run.\nOnly B approves: C must also approve, and A is not the runner.\nA, B and C approve, A runs: the uploader does not approve, and B is the designated runner.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/clean-rooms/clean-room-notebook"],
["P066", 2, "A clean room notebook writes output tables. Which statement about them is correct?", ["They are read-only, stored for 30 days, and readable by default only by the runner", "They are writable, stored for 90 days, and readable by all collaborators", "They are read-only, stored until the clean room is deleted, and readable by all", "They are writable, stored for 7 days, and readable by metastore admins only"], "Clean room output tables are temporary, read-only tables in the runner's metastore, kept for 30 days. By default only the principal that ran the notebook can read them; copy them to keep them.\nWritable, 90 days, all collaborators: they are read-only, 30 days, and runner-only by default.\nUntil clean room deleted, readable by all: they expire after 30 days and are runner-only by default.\nWritable, 7 days, metastore admins: wrong on writability, retention and default access.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/clean-rooms/output-tables"],
["P067", 2, "A user must create a foreign catalog on an existing PostgreSQL connection owned by another team. Which privileges are needed?", ["`CREATE CATALOG` on the metastore, plus `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` on the connection", "`CREATE CONNECTION` on the metastore, plus `USE CONNECTION` on the connection", "`CREATE CATALOG` on the metastore, plus `USE CONNECTION` on the connection", "`CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` on the metastore, plus `SELECT` on the connection"], "A foreign catalog is a catalog, so you need `CREATE CATALOG` on the metastore. Because the connection belongs to another team, you also need `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` on it (or ownership); credentials come from that connection.\n`CREATE CONNECTION` plus `USE CONNECTION`: `CREATE CONNECTION` is for making new connections, and `USE CONNECTION` does not allow creating catalogs on one.\n`CREATE CATALOG` plus `USE CONNECTION`: the metastore half is right, but the connection privilege must be `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG`.\n`CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` on metastore, `SELECT` on connection: privileges are on the wrong objects; `SELECT` is not a connection privilege.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/query-federation/database-federation"],
["P068", 2, "Finance runs daily dashboards over about 2 TB of data in a production SQL Server database and occasionally needs ad hoc lookups against the live source. Which approach follows Databricks guidance for the dashboards?", ["Ingest the data with a Lakeflow Connect managed connector, and keep query federation for the ad hoc lookups", "Query a foreign catalog through Lakehouse Federation, since filters are pushed down to SQL Server", "Query a foreign catalog and enable the disk cache so repeated dashboard reads come from worker SSDs", "Create a foreign catalog and write the dashboard aggregates back to SQL Server through it each night"], "At this volume with daily dashboards, Databricks recommends ingesting with a Lakeflow Connect managed connector for lower query latency, and keeping federation for ad hoc or proof-of-concept queries against the live source.\n`Query a foreign catalog ... pushed down`: pushdown helps, but every dashboard query still hits the production database, which is what the guidance steers you away from at high volume.\n`enable the disk cache`: the disk cache and result cache don't apply to federated queries.\n`write the dashboard aggregates back`: federated queries are read-only, so you can't write to SQL Server through a foreign catalog.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/query-federation/database-federation"],
["P069", 2, "An admin creates a PostgreSQL connection for Lakehouse Federation and wants to avoid plaintext credentials and limit what users can do. Which statement is correct?", ["Use `secret()` for `user` and `password` in the connection; `MODIFY` cannot be granted on foreign tables", "Store `user` and `password` on each foreign catalog; `MODIFY` lets users write back to PostgreSQL", "Pass credentials per query with `remote_query`; foreign tables accept `MODIFY` grants", "Store credentials in a storage credential; foreign tables inherit `MODIFY` from the catalog"], "Credentials live on the connection, and Databricks recommends `secret('<scope>','<key>')` for `user` and `password` so nothing is stored in plaintext. Foreign tables are read-only, so `MODIFY` can't be granted on them.\n`on each foreign catalog`: the foreign catalog points at a connection; it doesn't hold credentials. And `MODIFY` can't enable writes back.\n`remote_query`: it runs native SQL through an existing connection and its stored credentials, not credentials passed per query, and foreign tables still reject `MODIFY`.\n`storage credential`: that authenticates to cloud object storage, not to a PostgreSQL database.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/query-federation/postgresql"],
["P070", 2, "Iceberg tables are registered in AWS Glue, and the team wants to query them from Unity Catalog without copying data. Which statement is correct?", ["Use catalog federation to create a foreign catalog; the Iceberg tables are read-only", "Use catalog federation to create a foreign catalog; the Iceberg tables are fully writable", "Use query federation over JDBC; Glue pushes queries down to its own engine", "Use Auto Loader with `cloudFiles.format` set to `iceberg` to mirror them"], "Catalog federation registers an external catalog such as AWS Glue as a foreign catalog in Unity Catalog, so you query the Iceberg tables in place on Databricks compute. These foreign Iceberg tables are read-only.\n`fully writable`: catalog federation is read-only; writes go through the system that owns the catalog.\n`query federation over JDBC`: Glue is a metadata catalog, not a query engine, so there is nothing to push queries down to.\n`Auto Loader ... iceberg`: Auto Loader ingests files into new tables (copying data), and `iceberg` isn't a supported `cloudFiles.format`.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/query-federation/"],
["P071", 7, "A data steward is not the owner of table `sales.core.orders`. Which privileges let them add a tag to it?", ["`APPLY TAG` on the table, `USE SCHEMA` on `core`, `USE CATALOG` on `sales`", "`MODIFY` on the table, `USE SCHEMA` on `core`, `USE CATALOG` on `sales`", "`APPLY TAG` on the table only, without any parent privileges", "`MANAGE` on the metastore, which inherits to all tables"], "A non-owner needs `APPLY TAG` on the object to tag it, plus the usual path privileges: `USE SCHEMA` on the schema and `USE CATALOG` on the catalog. (A governed tag would also need `ASSIGN` on the tag.)\n`MODIFY on the table`: that allows data changes, not tagging; `APPLY TAG` is the privilege tagging checks.\n`APPLY TAG ... only`: without `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` the user can't reach the table at all.\n`MANAGE on the metastore`: metastore-level grants don't inherit to child objects (only `READ METADATA` does).", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/database-objects/tags"],
["P072", 7, "A user with `APPLY TAG`, `USE SCHEMA` and `USE CATALOG` can set ordinary tags but fails when assigning the governed tag `data_class`. What is missing?", ["The `ASSIGN` permission on the governed tag", "The `MODIFY` privilege on the table", "Ownership of the parent schema", "The `MANAGE` privilege on the catalog"], "Governed tags are controlled by a tag policy on the tag itself. Besides the normal tagging privileges, the user needs `ASSIGN` on that governed tag, and the value must match the policy.\n`MODIFY on the table`: it controls data writes, not tags, and the user can already tag the table.\n`Ownership of the parent schema`: not required; ordinary tags already work with the privileges they have.\n`MANAGE on the catalog`: it controls privileges on the catalog's objects; it isn't the permission on the governed tag.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/database-objects/tags"],
["P073", 7, "An engineer tries to tag three columns with `pii` in one `ALTER TABLE` statement. What happens?", ["It is not allowed; each column must be tagged with a separate statement", "It works if the columns are listed in one `ALTER COLUMN` clause", "It works only for governed tags, not ordinary tags", "It works, but the tag is stored at table level, not column level"], "Column tags must be set one column per `ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... SET TAGS` statement. Unity Catalog doesn't let you tag several columns in a single `ALTER TABLE` command.\n`listed in one ALTER COLUMN clause`: the clause takes a single column for tagging.\n`only for governed tags`: the restriction applies to all tags, governed or not.\n`stored at table level`: column tags are stored per column; the issue is that the multi-column statement isn't accepted, not where the tag lands.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/database-objects/tags"],
["P074", 7, "An auditor needs a SQL list of every column tagged `pii` in a catalog. Where should they look?", ["The `COLUMN_TAGS` view in the catalog's `INFORMATION_SCHEMA`", "The `TABLE_TAGS` view in the catalog's `INFORMATION_SCHEMA`", "The output of `DESCRIBE HISTORY` on each table", "The `system.access.audit` table filtered on tag events"], "Each catalog's `INFORMATION_SCHEMA` has a tag view per object level, and `COLUMN_TAGS` lists column-level tags with the table and column names, so filtering on the `pii` tag gives the list in one query.\n`TABLE_TAGS`: holds tags set on tables themselves, not on their columns.\n`DESCRIBE HISTORY`: shows Delta commit history for one table at a time, not current tags.\n`system.access.audit`: records tag change events over time, not the current set of tagged columns.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/database-objects/tags"],
["P075", 7, "A user with `MODIFY` on a table wants to remove its existing comment. Which statement does this?", ["`COMMENT ON TABLE my_table IS NULL;`", "`COMMENT ON TABLE my_table DROP;`", "`ALTER TABLE my_table UNSET COMMENT;`", "`COMMENT ON TABLE my_table REMOVE;`"], "`COMMENT ON TABLE ... IS NULL` removes an existing comment. On a table, `MODIFY` is enough (views need ownership).\n`COMMENT ON ... DROP`: not valid syntax; `COMMENT ON` takes `IS 'text'` or `IS NULL`.\n`ALTER TABLE ... UNSET COMMENT`: not valid syntax; `UNSET` works with `TBLPROPERTIES`, not comments.\n`COMMENT ON ... REMOVE`: not valid syntax either.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-ddl-comment"],
["P076", 7, "A team uses AI-generated comments in Catalog Explorer to document tables. Which statement is correct?", ["Suggestions must be reviewed and accepted, and saving one runs an `ALTER` command", "Suggestions are saved automatically once enabled at the catalog level", "Suggestions can be used to classify columns as PII", "Suggestions can be saved by any user with `BROWSE` on the catalog"], "AI-generated comments are suggestions: someone must review and accept them, and saving one runs an `ALTER` command, so the user needs ownership or `MODIFY` (ownership for views).\n`saved automatically`: suggestions are never applied without review.\n`classify columns as PII`: they generate descriptions; sensitive data classification is a separate feature.\n`any user with BROWSE`: `BROWSE` only lets users discover and view metadata; saving a comment needs `MODIFY` or ownership.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/comments/ai-comments"],
["P077", 7, "An admin wants one grant that lets an auditor see metadata for every object in the metastore, now and in future. Which metastore-level privilege inherits that way?", ["`READ METADATA`, the exception to the rule that metastore grants do not inherit", "`BROWSE`, because all metastore-level privileges inherit to every catalog", "`USE CATALOG`, because metastore grants inherit only to catalogs", "`CREATE CATALOG`, because creation rights imply read rights on children"], "Grants on a metastore normally don't inherit to child objects. `READ METADATA` is the documented exception: granted on the metastore, it inherits to every current and future object, with no usage privileges needed.\n`BROWSE ... all metastore-level privileges inherit`: false on both counts; `BROWSE` is granted at catalog level and metastore grants don't inherit.\n`USE CATALOG ... inherit only to catalogs`: metastore grants don't inherit to catalogs either.\n`CREATE CATALOG`: it only allows creating catalogs; creation rights never imply read rights on other objects.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges/privileges"],
["P078", 7, "A platform lead holds `MANAGE` on catalog `finance` but does not own it, and gets an error running `SELECT` on a table inside it. Why?", ["`MANAGE` covers privileges and ownership, but data access such as `SELECT` must be granted explicitly", "`MANAGE` on a catalog does not reach child tables, so it must be granted per table", "`MANAGE` includes `SELECT`, so the error means `USE SCHEMA` was revoked", "`MANAGE` is part of `ALL PRIVILEGES`, which must also be granted on the catalog"], "`MANAGE` lets a principal manage grants, transfer ownership and drop objects, and it applies to children. It doesn't include data access: the holder must grant themselves `SELECT` first.\n`does not reach child tables`: it does apply to children, so per-table `MANAGE` isn't needed.\n`MANAGE includes SELECT`: it doesn't, so the error isn't evidence that `USE SCHEMA` was revoked.\n`part of ALL PRIVILEGES`: `ALL PRIVILEGES` explicitly excludes `MANAGE`, and granting it isn't a prerequisite.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts"],
["P079", 7, "Analysts should be able to find tables and view their metadata across all catalogs, without reading data or being granted `USE CATALOG`. What does Databricks recommend?", ["Grant `BROWSE` on the catalogs to the `All account users` group", "Grant `READ METADATA` on each table to the analysts group", "Grant `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` without `SELECT`", "Grant `SELECT` on the `information_schema` of each catalog"], "`BROWSE` lets users discover objects and see their names, descriptions and tags without `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA` or data access. Databricks recommends granting it on catalogs to `All account users`.\n`READ METADATA on each table`: granted below catalog level it still needs usage privileges on the parents, and per-table grants don't cover new tables.\n`USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA without SELECT`: the requirement was to avoid granting `USE CATALOG`.\n`SELECT on information_schema`: users only see rows for objects they already have privileges on, so this exposes nothing new.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts"],
["P080", 7, "An admin runs `GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA sales.reporting TO analysts;`. Analysts still cannot query tables in that schema. What else is needed?", ["`USE CATALOG` on `sales` and `USE SCHEMA` on `sales.reporting`", "`SELECT` on each table, because schema grants do not inherit", "`BROWSE` on `sales`, which implies `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA`", "`READ METADATA` on the metastore, which implies `USE SCHEMA`"], "Reading a table needs three things: `USE CATALOG` on the catalog, `USE SCHEMA` on the schema, and `SELECT` on the table. `SELECT` on the schema already covers current and future tables, so only the two usage privileges are missing.\n`SELECT on each table`: wrong reason; schema grants do inherit to tables.\n`BROWSE`: it shows metadata only and doesn't imply any usage privilege.\n`READ METADATA on the metastore`: it inherits metadata visibility, not `USE SCHEMA` or data access.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts"]
);

PBANK.push(
// ---------- Section 3: advanced transformations ----------
["P081", 3, "A silver table can contain several versions of each customer. You must keep exactly one row per `customer_id`, the one with the latest `updated_at`, and two versions can share the same `updated_at`. Which filter guarantees one row per customer?",
 ["`QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC) = 1`",
  "`QUALIFY RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC) = 1`",
  "`QUALIFY DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC) = 1`",
  "`QUALIFY CUME_DIST() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC) = 1`"],
 "`ROW_NUMBER` gives every row in a partition a distinct number, even when the ordering values tie, so `= 1` keeps exactly one row per customer. Which tied row wins is arbitrary unless the `ORDER BY` is unique.\n`RANK`: tied rows share rank 1, so two versions with the same `updated_at` both survive.\n`DENSE_RANK`: same tie problem as `RANK`; it only differs in leaving no gaps after ties.\n`CUME_DIST`: it returns a fraction, and with `DESC` the value 1 lands on the oldest rows, plus any ties.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/row_number"],
["P082", 3, "A PySpark job computes `F.sum('amount').over(Window.partitionBy('acct').orderBy('txn_date'))`. Several transactions in an account share the same `txn_date`. What does the running total show for those rows?",
 ["The same cumulative value for each of them, because the default frame with ordering is a range frame from unbounded preceding to the current row",
  "A different cumulative value for each of them, because the default frame with ordering is a row frame from unbounded preceding to the current row",
  "The full account total for each of them, because the default frame covers the whole partition whenever no frame is specified",
  "No value, because the job fails when orderBy is used without an explicit rowsBetween or rangeBetween frame"],
 "With `orderBy` and no explicit frame, Spark uses a range frame from unbounded preceding to the current row. A range frame includes all peers with the same `txn_date`, so tied rows show the same running total.\n`a row frame`: that's what `rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)` gives; it isn't the default.\n`full account total`: the whole-partition frame is the default only when there is no `orderBy`.\n`job fails`: an explicit frame is optional; the default frame applies.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Window.html"],
["P083", 3, "You need a 7-row moving average of `daily_sales` per store in PySpark: the current row plus the six rows before it, ordered by `sale_date`. Which window specification is correct?",
 ["`Window.partitionBy('store_id').orderBy('sale_date').rowsBetween(-6, 0)`",
  "`Window.partitionBy('store_id').orderBy('sale_date').rowsBetween(-7, 0)`",
  "`Window.partitionBy('store_id').orderBy('sale_date').rowsBetween(-6, -1)`",
  "`Window.partitionBy('store_id').orderBy('sale_date').rowsBetween(0, 6)`"],
 "`rowsBetween` bounds are inclusive and relative to the current row (0). `-6` to `0` is the six previous rows plus the current one: seven rows.\n`(-7, 0)`: eight rows, one too many.\n`(-6, -1)`: six rows and excludes the current row.\n`(0, 6)`: looks forward at the next six rows instead of back.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Window.rowsBetween.html"],
["P084", 3, "A data quality check must list the rows in the `orders` DataFrame whose `customer_id` has no match in `customers`. The output should contain only the columns of `orders`. Which PySpark call does this?",
 ["`orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')`",
  "`orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_semi')`",
  "`orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_outer')`",
  "`orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'full_outer')`"],
 "A `left_anti` join returns left-side rows with no match on the right, and only the left side's columns: exactly the orphaned orders.\n`left_semi`: the opposite; it keeps orders that do have a matching customer.\n`left_outer`: returns every order plus customer columns (NULL when unmatched), so you'd still need a filter and to drop columns.\n`full_outer`: also adds customers with no orders and all columns from both sides.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],
["P085", 3, "A sales report needs totals for each (region, product) pair, a subtotal for each region, and a grand total. It must not include product-only subtotals. Which GROUP BY clause produces exactly these grouping sets?",
 ["`GROUP BY ROLLUP (region, product)`",
  "`GROUP BY CUBE (region, product)`",
  "`GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((region, product), (product), ())`",
  "`GROUP BY ALL`"],
 "`ROLLUP (region, product)` builds N+1 hierarchical grouping sets: `(region, product)`, `(region)` and `()`, which is exactly the detail, region subtotal and grand total.\n`CUBE`: produces all 2^N combinations, adding the unwanted `(product)` subtotal.\n`GROUPING SETS ((region, product), (product), ())`: lists a product subtotal instead of the region subtotal.\n`GROUP BY ALL`: just groups by every non-aggregated column, giving only the `(region, product)` level.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-groupby"],
["P086", 3, "A query joins a fact table `f` to a dimension table `d` and carries the hint `/*+ SHUFFLE_HASH(f), BROADCAST(d) */`. How does Databricks treat these conflicting join strategy hints?",
 ["It gives BROADCAST priority over SHUFFLE_HASH, but it does not guarantee that the hinted strategy is used",
  "It gives SHUFFLE_HASH priority because it is applied to the larger side, and it always uses that strategy",
  "It ignores both hints because conflicting strategy hints cancel each other out and fall back to a sort-merge join",
  "It raises an analysis error because only one join strategy hint may appear in a single query"],
 "Conflicting strategy hints on the two sides are resolved by priority: `BROADCAST`, then `MERGE`, then `SHUFFLE_HASH`, then `SHUFFLE_REPLICATE_NL`. Hints are suggestions, so even the winning strategy isn't guaranteed.\n`SHUFFLE_HASH priority ... always uses`: it ranks below `BROADCAST`, and no hint forces a strategy.\n`ignores both ... sort-merge`: conflicts are resolved by priority, not cancelled.\n`raises an analysis error`: multiple strategy hints are allowed; the query runs.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-hints"],

// ---------- Section 3: VARIANT ----------
["P087", 3, "A bronze table stores webhook payloads as JSON strings in `raw`. A small number of payloads are malformed. The silver load must convert `raw` to VARIANT and keep loading, writing NULL for the malformed rows. Which expression should it use?",
 ["`try_parse_json(raw)`",
  "`parse_json(raw)`",
  "`schema_of_variant(parse_json(raw))`",
  "`is_variant_null(parse_json(raw))`"],
 "`try_parse_json` converts valid JSON to VARIANT and returns NULL for malformed input, so the load keeps going.\n`parse_json(raw)`: raises `MALFORMED_RECORD_IN_PARSING` on invalid JSON and fails the load.\n`schema_of_variant(parse_json(raw))`: still calls `parse_json`, so it fails the same way, and returns a schema string, not the data.\n`is_variant_null(parse_json(raw))`: also fails on bad JSON, and returns a boolean testing for a JSON null.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/parse_json"],
["P088", 3, "The VARIANT column `payload` holds JSON such as `{\"customerId\": 42, ...}`. The query `SELECT payload:CustomerId FROM events` returns NULL for every row. What is the most likely cause?",
 ["VARIANT path elements are matched case-sensitively, so `CustomerId` does not match `customerId`",
  "The colon syntax only works on VARIANT arrays, so the field must be read with `variant_get` instead",
  "VARIANT fields must be cast with `::` before they can be selected, otherwise NULL is returned",
  "VARIANT stores all field names in lower case, so the path must be written as `customerid`"],
 "VARIANT path elements are matched case-sensitively, so `payload:CustomerId` doesn't find the field `customerId` and returns NULL. (Paths into JSON strings are case-insensitive, which is a common source of this surprise.)\n`only works on VARIANT arrays`: colon path syntax works on VARIANT objects and arrays.\n`must be cast with ::`: casting is optional; without it you get a VARIANT value, not NULL.\n`stores all field names in lower case`: VARIANT keeps field names as written.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/semi-structured/variant-json-diff"],
["P089", 3, "In the VARIANT column `v`, the field `qty` is usually a number but sometimes contains the text 'N/A'. You need an INT column, with NULL where the value cannot be converted, and the query must not fail. Which expression should you use?",
 ["`try_variant_get(v, '$.qty', 'int')`",
  "`variant_get(v, '$.qty', 'int')`",
  "`variant_get(v, '$.qty', 'variant')`",
  "`schema_of_variant(v:qty)`"],
 "`try_variant_get` extracts the path and casts it to the requested type, returning NULL when the path is missing or the cast fails, so 'N/A' becomes NULL.\n`variant_get(..., 'int')`: same extraction, but it raises an error when the cast fails, so the query breaks on 'N/A'.\n`variant_get(..., 'variant')`: never fails, but returns a VARIANT, not the required INT.\n`schema_of_variant`: returns the value's type as a string, not the value.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/try_variant_get"],
["P090", 3, "Each order row has a VARIANT column `v` whose `items` field is an array. You need one output row per item, and orders with an empty or missing `items` array must still appear once with NULL item values. Which FROM clause meets this requirement?",
 ["`FROM orders o, LATERAL variant_explode_outer(o.v:items) AS e`",
  "`FROM orders o, LATERAL variant_explode(o.v:items) AS e`",
  "`FROM orders o, LATERAL variant_get(o.v, '$.items', 'array') AS e`",
  "`FROM orders o, LATERAL schema_of_variant(o.v:items) AS e`"],
 "`variant_explode_outer` is the outer form of the generator: it yields one row per array element, and for NULL, missing or empty input it yields one row of NULLs, so every order appears.\n`variant_explode`: produces no rows for NULL, non-container or empty input, so those orders vanish.\n`variant_get(..., 'array')`: a scalar extraction function, not a table-valued generator, so it can't produce one row per item.\n`schema_of_variant`: returns a schema string, not rows.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/variant_explode"],
["P091", 3, "An `events` Delta table stores everything in a VARIANT column `payload`. Most queries filter on `payload:event_type`, and the team wants to improve data skipping for those filters. Which design works?",
 ["Extract `event_type` into its own STRING column during ingestion and use that column as a liquid clustering key",
  "Add `CLUSTER BY (payload)` so liquid clustering can organise the data by the fields inside the VARIANT",
  "Partition the table by `payload` so each distinct event type is written to its own directory",
  "Run `OPTIMIZE events ZORDER BY (payload)` on a schedule to co-locate rows with the same event type"],
 "VARIANT columns can't be clustering keys, partition columns or Z-order keys. Extracting `event_type` into a typed column at ingestion gives you a normal column that liquid clustering can use for data skipping.\n`CLUSTER BY (payload)`: VARIANT columns aren't allowed as clustering keys.\n`Partition ... by payload`: VARIANT can't be a partition column either.\n`ZORDER BY (payload)`: Z-order keys can't be VARIANT.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/semi-structured/variant"],
["P092", 3, "You are profiling a VARIANT column whose structure differs between rows, and you want a single schema that combines the structure of all rows in the table. Which function should you use?",
 ["`schema_of_variant_agg`",
  "`schema_of_variant`",
  "`variant_explode`",
  "`is_variant_null`"],
 "`schema_of_variant_agg` is an aggregate: it merges the structure of all VARIANT values in a group into one combined schema.\n`schema_of_variant`: same idea but per value, so you get one schema per row, not a merged one.\n`variant_explode`: turns array or object elements into rows; it doesn't describe a schema.\n`is_variant_null`: only tests whether a value is a VARIANT null.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/semi-structured/variant"],

// ---------- Section 3: AI functions ----------
["P093", 3, "A Lakeflow pipeline enriches product reviews with `ai_query`. A single endpoint error on one row currently fails the whole update. The team wants the update to finish and to send rows that failed inference to a review table. What should they do?",
 ["Call `ai_query` with `failOnError => false` and route rows whose `errorMessage` field is not NULL to the review table",
  "Wrap the `ai_query` call in `try_cast` and route rows where the result is NULL to the review table",
  "Pass a `modelParameters` struct that sets a retry count and route rows that still fail to the review table",
  "Set `responseFormat` to `text` so endpoint errors are returned as strings that can be filtered later"],
 "With `failOnError => false`, `ai_query` returns a STRUCT of `response` and `errorMessage` instead of raising. Failed rows have a non-NULL `errorMessage`, so you can route them to a review table while the update finishes.\n`try_cast`: it handles type conversion errors, not model endpoint errors, which still fail the query.\n`modelParameters ... retry count`: it passes model settings such as `temperature`; it isn't a retry control.\n`responseFormat text`: it shapes the model's output format; errors still raise.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_query"],
["P094", 3, "An engineer classifies 20 million support emails by looping in Python over 1,000-row chunks and calling `ai_query` on a provisioned throughput endpoint for each chunk. Which change follows the Databricks recommendation for batch inference?",
 ["Submit the whole dataset in a single query against a Databricks-hosted foundation model endpoint whose name starts with `databricks-`",
  "Reduce the chunk size to 100 rows so each call to the provisioned throughput endpoint finishes faster",
  "Keep the 1,000-row chunks but use a Python thread pool to send several chunks to the endpoint at once",
  "Write each chunk to its own table first and run a separate scheduled job for each table"],
 "AI Functions handle parallelism, retries and scaling themselves, so Databricks recommends sending the whole dataset in one query, and for `ai_query` prefers Databricks-hosted foundation models (names starting `databricks-`) over provisioned throughput.\n`Reduce the chunk size`: more, smaller calls keeps the manual loop the guidance tells you to drop.\n`Python thread pool`: hand-rolled parallelism duplicates what the function already manages.\n`Write each chunk to its own table`: adds jobs and tables without using the built-in scaling.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/large-language-models/ai-functions"],
["P095", 3, "A pipeline uses `ai_query` with a chat foundation model to pull fields out of invoices. Downstream code needs the output to be JSON that matches a defined schema. Which `ai_query` argument should you set?",
 ["`responseFormat`",
  "`returnType`",
  "`modelParameters`",
  "`failOnError`"],
 "`responseFormat` makes a chat foundation model return output in a structure you define, as a DDL string or a JSON spec of type `text`, `json_object` or `json_schema`.\n`returnType`: declares the expected return type for custom model serving endpoints, not chat models.\n`modelParameters`: passes settings like `temperature` and `max_tokens`; it doesn't enforce a schema.\n`failOnError`: controls whether errors raise or come back in an `errorMessage` field.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_query"],
["P096", 3, "Support tickets must be tagged as `billing`, `outage` or `feature_request` inside a pipeline. The team wants to supply the labels and avoid choosing a model or writing a prompt. Which function fits best?",
 ["`ai_classify`",
  "`ai_extract`",
  "`ai_analyze_sentiment`",
  "`ai_similarity`"],
 "`ai_classify` is the task-specific function for this: you pass the text and the label list, and Databricks picks the model and prompt.\n`ai_extract`: pulls named entities out of text into a structure; it doesn't choose one label.\n`ai_analyze_sentiment`: only returns sentiment such as positive or negative, not your own labels.\n`ai_similarity`: returns a similarity score between two strings.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/large-language-models/ai-functions"],
["P097", 3, "An engineer wants to set `temperature` in `ai_query` from a column so that each row can use a different value. Which statement about the `modelParameters` argument is correct?",
 ["Its values must be constant rather than data-dependent, and `temperature` defaults to 0.0",
  "Its values can reference columns for each row, and `temperature` defaults to 1.0",
  "It only applies to custom model endpoints, and `temperature` is fixed at 0.0",
  "It only applies when `failOnError` is false, and `temperature` defaults to 0.5"],
 "`modelParameters` values must be constant, not data-dependent, so a per-row `temperature` isn't possible; the documented default for `temperature` is 0.0.\n`can reference columns ... 1.0`: columns aren't allowed and the default isn't 1.0.\n`only applies to custom model endpoints ... fixed`: it is used with foundation models, and `temperature` is configurable.\n`only applies when failOnError is false`: the two arguments are independent.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_query"],
["P098", 3, "Before call transcripts are written to a shared silver table, personal details such as email addresses and phone numbers must be hidden while the rest of the text stays readable. Which task-specific AI function is designed for this?",
 ["`ai_mask`",
  "`ai_summarize`",
  "`ai_fix_grammar`",
  "`ai_extract`"],
 "`ai_mask` replaces specified entities, such as emails and phone numbers, with masks in place, leaving the rest of the text readable.\n`ai_summarize`: shortens the text, and may still include personal details.\n`ai_fix_grammar`: corrects grammar and leaves personal details untouched.\n`ai_extract`: pulls entities out into a structure instead of hiding them in the original text.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/large-language-models/ai-functions"],

// ---------- Section 3: Lakeflow expectations ----------
["P099", 3, "In a Lakeflow pipeline SQL definition, rows with a NULL `order_id` must be removed before they are written to the streaming table, and the update must continue. Which constraint is correct?",
 ["`CONSTRAINT valid_order EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW`",
  "`CONSTRAINT valid_order EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE`",
  "`CONSTRAINT valid_order EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION SKIP RECORD`",
  "`CONSTRAINT valid_order EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION QUARANTINE`"],
 "Pipeline expectations have three behaviours: warn (the default, no `ON VIOLATION`), `DROP ROW` and `FAIL UPDATE`. `ON VIOLATION DROP ROW` removes invalid rows before the write, records the count, and lets the update continue.\n`FAIL UPDATE`: stops the update on the first violation instead of continuing.\n`SKIP RECORD`: not a valid action.\n`QUARANTINE`: not a valid action; quarantining is a pattern you build yourself.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations"],
["P100", 3, "A Python pipeline table has `@dp.expect('valid_amount', 'amount > 0')` and no other expectations. A batch arrives in which 2% of rows have a negative amount. What happens?",
 ["All rows, including the invalid ones, are written to the target, and the violations are reported in the dataset's data quality metrics",
  "The invalid rows are dropped before the write, and the number of dropped rows is reported in the data quality metrics",
  "The update fails and the transaction is rolled back, so no rows from the batch are written to the target",
  "The invalid rows are moved to an automatically created quarantine table next to the target"],
 "`@dp.expect` with no action suffix is the warn behaviour: all rows, valid or not, are written, and the violation counts appear in the data quality metrics.\n`dropped before the write`: that needs `@dp.expect_or_drop`.\n`update fails and rolls back`: that needs `@dp.expect_or_fail`.\n`quarantine table`: none is created automatically; quarantine is a pattern you build yourself.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations"],
["P101", 3, "A triggered Lakeflow pipeline has two independent flows running in parallel. Flow A uses `@dp.expect_or_fail` and receives a record that violates the rule. What is the result?",
 ["The update to flow A's table is rolled back atomically, and flow B is not failed by flow A's failure",
  "Flow A writes the valid rows and skips the invalid one, and flow B continues without changes",
  "Both flows are rolled back, because any failed expectation fails every flow in a triggered pipeline",
  "Flow A retries automatically with the invalid record dropped, and flow B waits for the retry to finish"],
 "A fail expectation stops the flow and atomically rolls back that table's update. In a triggered pipeline, other flows running in parallel aren't failed by it; you fix the cause manually and reprocess.\n`writes the valid rows and skips the invalid one`: that's drop behaviour, not fail.\n`Both flows are rolled back`: the failure is scoped to the flow whose expectation failed.\n`retries automatically with the record dropped`: fail expectations need manual intervention; nothing is dropped and retried for you.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations"],
["P102", 3, "Invalid trip records must be kept for review rather than dropped, valid records must flow downstream, and data quality metrics must still be recorded. Which design follows the Databricks quarantine pattern?",
 ["A temporary table applies `@dp.expect_all(rules)` and adds an `is_quarantined` column set to `NOT` of all rules combined, and two views filter on that column",
  "The target table applies `@dp.expect_all_or_drop(rules)`, and a second pipeline reads the event log to rebuild the dropped rows for review",
  "The target table applies `@dp.expect_all_or_fail(rules)`, and the failed update's error message is used to copy the bad rows into a review table",
  "Two tables read the same source, one with `@dp.expect_all_or_drop(rules)` and one with no expectations that keeps every row for review"],
 "The documented quarantine pattern puts `expect_all(rules)` on a temporary table, which records metrics without dropping, adds an `is_quarantined` flag from the inverted combined rules, and builds separate views for valid and invalid rows.\n`expect_all_or_drop ... event log`: the event log only has counts, so dropped rows can't be rebuilt from it.\n`expect_all_or_fail`: a failure stops the update, so valid records don't flow downstream either.\n`Two tables read the same source`: the review table keeps every row, valid ones included, not just the invalid records, and it isn't the documented pattern.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectation-patterns"],
["P103", 3, "A team keeps twelve data quality rules in a Python dictionary that maps rule names to SQL constraints. Any row that breaks at least one rule must be dropped. Which decorator should the table function use?",
 ["`@dp.expect_all_or_drop(rules)`",
  "`@dp.expect_or_drop(rules)`",
  "`@dp.expect_all(rules)`",
  "`@dp.expect_all_or_fail(rules)`"],
 "The `expect_all*` decorators take a dictionary of rule names to SQL constraints and apply one action to the group; `expect_all_or_drop` drops any row that fails at least one rule.\n`expect_or_drop(rules)`: takes a single name and constraint, not a dictionary.\n`expect_all(rules)`: warn behaviour, so invalid rows are kept.\n`expect_all_or_fail(rules)`: fails the update instead of dropping rows.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations"],
["P104", 3, "Which proposed expectation constraint can be used in a Lakeflow pipeline?",
 ["`CASE WHEN discount IS NOT NULL THEN discount BETWEEN 0 AND 1 ELSE TRUE END`",
  "`customer_id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM main.crm.customers)`",
  "`is_valid_email(email)`, where `is_valid_email` is a Python function defined in the pipeline",
  "`http_post('https://validator.example.com', to_json(struct(*))) = 200`"],
 "An expectation must be a SQL boolean expression evaluated on each record on its own. The `CASE` expression is plain SQL returning true or false per row, so it is valid.\n`IN (SELECT ... FROM main.crm.customers)`: subqueries that reference other tables aren't allowed.\n`is_valid_email(email)` Python function: custom Python functions can't be used in constraints.\n`http_post(...)`: calls to external services aren't allowed.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations"],

// ---------- Section 9: table layout ----------
["P105", 9, "A new Unity Catalog managed table will hold about 600 GB of order data. A team member proposes partitioning it by `order_date` because most queries filter on that column. What does Databricks recommend?",
 ["Do not partition it, and use liquid clustering on `order_date` instead",
  "Partition it by `order_date` and Z-order it by the other filter columns",
  "Partition it by `order_date` and add liquid clustering on the same column",
  "Partition it by a generated year-month column to keep partitions under 1 GB"],
 "Databricks advises against partitioning tables under 1 TB, and recommends liquid clustering for new managed tables. Clustering on `order_date` gives data skipping without small partition directories.\n`Partition ... and Z-order`: 600 GB is below the 1 TB threshold, so partitioning is still advised against.\n`Partition and add liquid clustering on the same column`: clustering isn't compatible with partitioning.\n`generated year-month column`: still partitioning a table under 1 TB, and the aim is partitions of at least 1 GB, not under 1 GB.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/partitions"],
["P106", 9, "A 400 TB clickstream table is the rare case where testing shows partitioning helps. Candidate columns are `event_ts` (timestamp), `user_id`, `session_id` and `event_date`, and each day holds several GB. Which partition column fits the Databricks guidance?",
 ["`event_date`, since it has low, known cardinality and each partition would hold at least 1 GB",
  "`event_ts`, since it gives the finest grain and lets queries prune down to individual events",
  "`user_id`, since most queries look up one user and each user would get a separate directory",
  "`session_id`, since sessions are spread evenly and would avoid skew between partitions"],
 "Partition columns should have low, known cardinality, and each partition should hold at least 1 GB. `event_date` meets both: one partition per day with several GB each.\n`event_ts`: a timestamp has very high cardinality, creating huge numbers of tiny partitions.\n`user_id`: high cardinality, so each partition would be far below 1 GB.\n`session_id`: even higher cardinality; even distribution doesn't fix tiny partitions.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/partitions"],
["P107", 9, "A fact table is mostly queried with filters on `order_date` and `store_id`. It also has `order_ts`, which is highly correlated with `order_date`, and a unique `order_id`. Which clustering choice follows the key selection guidelines?",
 ["`CLUSTER BY (order_date, store_id)`",
  "`CLUSTER BY (order_ts, order_date, store_id, order_id)`",
  "`PARTITIONED BY (order_date) CLUSTER BY (store_id)`",
  "`CLUSTER BY (order_id)` with `ZORDER BY (order_date, store_id)`"],
 "Pick clustering keys from the columns most often used in filters, and include only one of two highly correlated columns. `order_date` and `store_id` are the filter columns, and `order_ts` adds nothing beyond `order_date`.\n`(order_ts, order_date, store_id, order_id)`: includes correlated columns and an unfiltered unique key, diluting clustering.\n`PARTITIONED BY ... CLUSTER BY`: liquid clustering can't be combined with partitioning.\n`CLUSTER BY (order_id)` with `ZORDER BY`: liquid clustering can't be combined with Z-order, and `order_id` isn't a filter column.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P108", 9, "Query patterns on a large liquid-clustered table have changed, so you run `ALTER TABLE sales CLUSTER BY (region, product_id)`. What must you do so that the existing data is organised by the new keys?",
 ["Run `OPTIMIZE sales FULL`, because changing keys does not rewrite existing data",
  "Nothing, because `ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY` rewrites all existing files at once",
  "Run `OPTIMIZE sales ZORDER BY (region, product_id)` to apply the new keys",
  "Run `VACUUM sales` so that files using the old keys are reclustered"],
 "Changing clustering keys only affects how data is clustered from then on; existing files aren't rewritten. `OPTIMIZE ... FULL` reclusters all existing data with the new keys (it can take hours on a large table).\n`Nothing`: `ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY` updates metadata only.\n`ZORDER BY`: Z-order isn't compatible with liquid clustered tables.\n`VACUUM`: it deletes unreferenced old files; it doesn't recluster anything.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P109", 9, "A Unity Catalog managed Delta table is 1.5 TB and has no `delta.targetFileSize` set. Which target file size does Databricks autotune for it?",
 ["256 MB",
  "128 MB",
  "1 GB",
  "64 MB"],
 "With no `delta.targetFileSize` set, autotuning uses table size: below 2.56 TB the target is 256 MB, between 2.56 TB and 10 TB it grows linearly to 1 GB, and above 10 TB it is 1 GB. A 1.5 TB table gets 256 MB.\n`128 MB`: the fixed target used by auto compaction and optimized writes when set to `true`, not the autotuned size here.\n`1 GB`: only for tables over 10 TB.\n`64 MB`: not one of the autotuned targets.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/tune-file-size"],

// ---------- Section 9: dimensional models, MVs and metric views ----------
["P110", 9, "Analysts keep five standard views over the same sales star schema, each defining revenue and margin at a different grain. The definitions have drifted apart. Which Databricks feature lets you define each metric once and let users group by any available field?",
 ["A Unity Catalog metric view with the fact and dimension joins and the measures defined in YAML",
  "A materialized view per grain, each with its own aggregation and refresh schedule",
  "A single standard view that aggregates at the finest grain and is grouped again downstream",
  "A gold Delta table with one row per grain and a column that names the grain"],
 "A Unity Catalog metric view defines measures once in YAML, separately from the fields, so users can group any measure by any available field and definitions can't drift between views.\n`A materialized view per grain`: still one definition per grain, so the drift problem stays.\n`A single standard view ... grouped again`: re-aggregating downstream works for sums but breaks for ratios like margin; views lock in their aggregation.\n`A gold Delta table with one row per grain`: precomputes fixed grains, so users can't group by arbitrary fields.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/uc-semantics/metric-views/"],
["P111", 9, "The metric view `sales_metrics` defines a field `region` and a measure `total_revenue`. Which query is valid?",
 ["`SELECT region, MEASURE(total_revenue) FROM sales_metrics GROUP BY region`",
  "`SELECT region, SUM(total_revenue) FROM sales_metrics GROUP BY region`",
  "`SELECT region, total_revenue FROM sales_metrics GROUP BY region`",
  "`SELECT * FROM sales_metrics WHERE region = 'EMEA'`"],
 "In a metric view query, every measure must be wrapped in the `MEASURE()` aggregate, and fields are grouped normally.\n`SUM(total_revenue)`: measures can't be aggregated with ordinary functions; `MEASURE` evaluates the defined aggregation.\n`total_revenue` bare: a measure referenced without `MEASURE` is not allowed.\n`SELECT *`: not supported on metric views; columns must be listed explicitly.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/uc-semantics/metric-views/query"],
["P112", 9, "An analyst needs monthly revenue from the metric view `revenue_mv` alongside monthly targets stored in the table `targets`. How should the query be written?",
 ["Query the metric view with MEASURE in a CTE, then join the CTE result to `targets`",
  "Join `revenue_mv` directly to `targets` in the FROM clause and wrap the measure in MEASURE",
  "Join `revenue_mv` directly to `targets` and use SUM instead of MEASURE on the measure",
  "Add `targets` to the query with a LATERAL join so the measure is evaluated for each target row"],
 "Metric views can't be joined to other tables at query time. Query the metric view with `MEASURE` inside a CTE, then join that result set to `targets`.\n`Join revenue_mv directly ... MEASURE`: the direct join isn't allowed, even with `MEASURE`.\n`Join directly ... SUM`: also a direct join, and measures must use `MEASURE`.\n`LATERAL join`: still a join against the metric view, which isn't supported.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/uc-semantics/metric-views/query"],
["P113", 9, "A metric view has an aggregated materialization at (`order_date`, `store_id`) with the measures `SUM(revenue)` and `COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id)`. A dashboard queries both measures grouped by month only. How is the query served?",
 ["Revenue can be rolled up from the materialization, but the distinct customer count cannot, because DISTINCT aggregates are not additive",
  "Both measures are rolled up from the materialization, because month is coarser than the stored order_date grain",
  "Neither measure can use the materialization, because rollup matching only works when GROUP BY lists every stored dimension",
  "Both measures are served by exact match, because the optimizer converts month to order_date automatically"],
 "Rollup matching re-aggregates a materialization to a coarser grain, but only for additive measures like `SUM`, `COUNT`, `MIN` and `MAX`. Monthly revenue can be summed from daily rows; distinct customers can't, because the same customer appears on several days.\n`Both measures are rolled up`: wrong for `COUNT(DISTINCT)`, which isn't additive.\n`Neither measure`: rollup exists precisely for grouping by a subset of stored dimensions or a coarser grain.\n`exact match`: the query's grain (month) differs from the stored grain, so it isn't an exact match.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/uc-semantics/metric-views/materialization"],
["P114", 9, "You create a standalone materialized view that pre-aggregates daily sales from two Delta source tables for a dashboard. You want refreshes to process only new or changed data where possible. What is required on the source tables?",
 ["Row tracking must be enabled on the source Delta tables",
  "Change data feed must be enabled on the materialized view",
  "The source tables must be liquid clustered on the date column",
  "The source tables must be partitioned by the refresh date"],
 "Incremental refresh lets a materialized view process only new or changed source data, and it requires row tracking on the source Delta tables. Without it, refreshes fall back to full recompute.\n`Change data feed ... on the materialized view`: the requirement is on the sources, and it is row tracking.\n`liquid clustered on the date column`: layout doesn't decide whether refresh can be incremental.\n`partitioned by the refresh date`: also not a requirement.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/mv-st/materialized"]
);

PBANK.push(
["P115", 4, "A FinOps engineer sums `usage_quantity` from `system.billing.usage` but keeps only rows where `record_type = 'ORIGINAL'`. After Databricks issues a billing correction, the totals are too high. What should the query do instead?", ["Include all record types and sum `usage_quantity` by the grouping keys", "Keep only RESTATEMENT rows, which replace every ORIGINAL row for that day", "Keep only rows where `ingestion_date` equals `usage_date` to drop corrections", "Exclude rows with a negative `usage_quantity`, which are refunds billed separately"], "Billing corrections add rows instead of editing old ones: a `RETRACTION` row has negative `usage_quantity` that cancels the original, and a `RESTATEMENT` row holds the corrected value. Summing all record types by key gives the corrected total.\n`Keep only RESTATEMENT rows`: drops every original row that was never corrected.\n`ingestion_date equals usage_date`: corrections land later, so this keeps the wrong originals and drops their fixes.\n`Exclude negative usage_quantity`: those negatives are the retractions, so excluding them keeps the over-count.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/billing"],
["P116", 4, "A scheduled job runs its tasks on an existing all-purpose cluster. A cost report that groups `system.billing.usage` by `usage_metadata.job_id` shows that cluster's usage under a NULL job ID. What explains this?", ["`usage_metadata.job_id` is populated for job compute and serverless jobs, not all-purpose compute", "`usage_metadata.job_id` is populated only after a RESTATEMENT record is written for the run", "`usage_metadata.job_id` is populated only for jobs whose `run_as` is a service principal", "`usage_metadata.job_id` is moved into `custom_tags` for every job that runs on a schedule"], "`usage_metadata.job_id` is populated for job compute and serverless jobs. On an all-purpose cluster the usage belongs to the cluster, not to any job, so it shows as NULL.\n`only after a RESTATEMENT record`: restatements correct quantities; they don't drive `job_id` attribution.\n`run_as is a service principal`: the run-as identity doesn't affect this field.\n`moved into custom_tags`: `custom_tags` holds compute tags; `job_id` isn't moved there.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/billing"],
["P117", 4, "You need the current, non-deleted list of jobs from `system.lakeflow.jobs`, which is a slowly changing dimension (SCD2) table. Which approach returns the correct result?", ["Take the latest row per `workspace_id` and `job_id` by `change_time`, then drop rows with `delete_time` set", "Filter to `delete_time IS NULL` first, then take the latest row per `job_id` by `change_time`", "Take the row with the earliest `create_time` per `job_id` and ignore `delete_time` entirely", "Keep rows whose `change_time` falls in the last 395 days and whose `paused` value is false"], "`system.lakeflow.jobs` is SCD2: every change adds a row. Take the latest row per `workspace_id` and `job_id` by `change_time` first, then drop those whose `delete_time` is set.\n`Filter to delete_time IS NULL first`: a deleted job's earlier rows have no `delete_time`, so its last pre-delete state survives and it wrongly shows as current.\n`earliest create_time ... ignore delete_time`: returns the original definition and includes deleted jobs.\n`last 395 days ... paused`: retention and paused state have nothing to do with being current or deleted.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/jobs"],
["P118", 4, "An engineer counts rows in `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` grouped by `job_id` to report how many runs each job had. Jobs with long runs show inflated counts. Why?", ["Each row covers at most one hour, so long runs are split into several rows with the same `run_id`", "Each task in a multi-task run writes its own row into `job_run_timeline` with the parent `run_id`", "The table is SCD2, so every status change of a run emits a new row with a new `change_time`", "Queue time and execution time are written as two separate rows for every run of the job"], "`job_run_timeline` slices runs by time: each row covers at most one hour, so a long run has several rows with the same `run_id`, and `result_state` only on the final slice. Count distinct `run_id` instead.\n`Each task ... writes its own row`: task runs go in `job_task_run_timeline`, a separate table.\n`The table is SCD2`: that describes definition tables like `jobs`, not the run timeline.\n`Queue time and execution time ... two rows`: they aren't split into separate rows.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/jobs"],
["P119", 4, "An on-call engineer needs every job run in the workspace that failed or timed out in the last seven days, with the reason it ended. Which query approach is correct?", ["Filter `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` on `result_state` `FAILED`, `ERROR` or `TIMED_OUT`, and read `termination_code`", "Filter `system.lakeflow.jobs` on rows where `delete_time` is set, and read the last `change_time`", "Filter `system.billing.usage` on rows where `usage_quantity` is 0, and group by `usage_metadata.job_id`", "Filter `system.lakeflow.job_tasks` on `result_state` `FAILED`, and read the task's `notebook_path`"], "Run outcomes are in `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline`: `result_state` says how the run ended (`FAILED` or `ERROR` for errors, `TIMED_OUT` for timeouts) and `termination_code` gives the reason, such as `RUN_EXECUTION_ERROR`.\n`system.lakeflow.jobs ... delete_time`: that table holds job definitions; `delete_time` marks deleted jobs, not failed runs.\n`system.billing.usage ... 0`: billing records cost, not run outcomes.\n`system.lakeflow.job_tasks`: holds task definitions, so it has no run `result_state`.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/jobs"],
["P120", 4, "A pipeline's event log must be readable by a data quality team who are not the pipeline's run-as user. What does Databricks recommend?", ["Publish the event log to a Unity Catalog table, create a view over it, and grant access on the view", "Grant SELECT directly on the hidden event log table after locating it in Catalog Explorer", "Change the pipeline's owner to the team's group so `event_log()` runs with their identity", "Grant SELECT on `system.information_schema.tables` so the team can read the event log"], "By default only the pipeline's run-as user can query the event log. Databricks recommends publishing it to a Unity Catalog table, creating a view over it, and granting access on the view, because granting on the table directly can expose schema metadata.\n`Grant SELECT directly on the hidden event log table`: the approach the guidance warns against.\n`Change the pipeline's owner`: changes who controls the pipeline just to share a log, and isn't the recommended route.\n`system.information_schema.tables`: lists table metadata; it doesn't contain the event log.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/monitor-event-logs"],
["P121", 4, "Which event log filter and field give per-expectation passed and failed record counts for a Lakeflow pipeline?", ["`event_type = 'flow_progress'` with `details:flow_progress.data_quality.expectations`", "`event_type = 'update_progress'` with `details:update_progress.data_quality.expectations`", "`event_type = 'flow_definition'` with `details:flow_definition.data_quality.expectations`", "`event_type = 'user_action'` with `details:user_action.data_quality.expectations`"], "Expectation results are reported per flow: `flow_progress` events carry `details:flow_progress.data_quality.expectations` with each expectation's `name`, `dataset`, `passed_records` and `failed_records`.\n`update_progress`: tracks the state of the whole update, not per-expectation counts.\n`flow_definition`: describes a flow's inputs, outputs and schema (lineage), not its data quality results.\n`user_action`: records actions such as starting or stopping the pipeline.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/monitor-event-logs"],
["P122", 4, "You want to rebuild a pipeline's dataset lineage (which inputs feed each output) from its event log. Which `event_type` should you query?", ["`flow_definition`", "`flow_progress`", "`operation_progress`", "`create_update`"], "`flow_definition` events describe each flow, and `details:flow_definition` contains `output_dataset` and `input_datasets`: the lineage you need.\n`flow_progress`: reports a flow's run status, metrics and data quality, not its inputs.\n`operation_progress`: reports progress of operations within an update, not dataset lineage.\n`create_update`: records that an update started and what triggered it.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/monitor-event-logs"],
["P123", 4, "A Lakeflow pipeline on classic compute is slow and you suspect it is under-provisioned. Which event log data best shows whether tasks are waiting for executor slots?", ["`cluster_resources` events, using `avg_num_queued_tasks` and `avg_task_slot_utilization`", "`runtime_details` events, using the Databricks Runtime version recorded for the update", "`flow_progress` events, using `data_quality.dropped_records` for each of the flows", "`user_action` events, using the START and STOP actions recorded for each update"], "`cluster_resources` events report compute metrics for classic pipelines, including `avg_num_queued_tasks` and `avg_task_slot_utilization`. High queued tasks with full slot use means tasks are waiting for executor slots.\n`runtime_details`: only the Databricks Runtime version, which says nothing about capacity.\n`flow_progress ... dropped_records`: a data quality count, unrelated to compute.\n`user_action`: start and stop actions, not resource usage.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/monitor-event-logs"],
["P124", 4, "A job run has been repaired twice. A monitoring script needs the run details together with its repair history. Which CLI command returns this?", ["`databricks jobs get-run <run-id> --include-history`", "`databricks jobs list-runs --job-id <job-id> --completed-only`", "`databricks jobs get-run-output <run-id> --include-history`", "`databricks jobs export-run <run-id> --views-to-export ALL`"], "`databricks jobs get-run <run-id> --include-history` returns the run's details plus its repair history.\n`list-runs --completed-only`: lists a job's finished runs; it doesn't return repair history.\n`get-run-output`: returns the output of a single task run, such as a notebook's exit value, not repair history.\n`export-run`: exports the run's views, such as notebook HTML, not repair history.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/reference/jobs-commands"],
["P125", 4, "A monitoring script calls `databricks jobs get-run-output` for a notebook task run that prints a very large amount of text. What should the team expect?", ["Only the first 5 MB of the output is returned", "The full output is returned across several pages", "The output is redirected to the cluster log path", "The call fails unless the run is still in progress"], "The `get-run-output` API returns only the first 5 MB of output for a single task run, so a very large output is truncated.\n`full output ... several pages`: the API doesn't page through the output; it stops at 5 MB.\n`redirected to the cluster log path`: the output isn't redirected; cluster log delivery is a separate setting.\n`fails unless the run is still in progress`: output can be read after the run finishes.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/reference/jobs-commands"],
["P126", 4, "Which CLI command retrieves events for a Lakeflow pipeline and supports SQL-like filtering of the results?", ["`databricks pipelines list-pipeline-events <pipeline-id> --filter ...`", "`databricks pipelines list-updates <pipeline-id> --filter ...`", "`databricks pipelines get-update <pipeline-id> <update-id> --events`", "`databricks jobs list-runs --pipeline-id <pipeline-id> --filter ...`"], "`databricks pipelines list-pipeline-events` retrieves a pipeline's event log entries, and its `--filter` flag takes SQL-like criteria.\n`list-updates`: lists a pipeline's updates (runs), not their events.\n`get-update ... --events`: `get-update` returns one update's details; it isn't the event retrieval command.\n`jobs list-runs --pipeline-id`: the jobs command group lists job runs, not pipeline events.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/reference/pipelines-commands"],
["P127", 4, "A Databricks SQL alert query returns one row per region with a `failed_orders` column. The alert must trigger when the total across all regions exceeds 500. How do you configure the condition?", ["Use the SUM aggregation on `failed_orders`, operator >, static value 500", "Use the first value of `failed_orders`, operator >, static value 500", "Use the COUNT aggregation on `failed_orders`, operator >, static value 500", "Use the first value of `failed_orders`, operator >, static value 500 per row"], "An alert condition can test the first value of a column or an aggregate over all rows. Choosing `SUM` on `failed_orders` with `>` 500 totals every region, as required; the alert wraps the query in a CTE and aggregates its result.\n`first value`: only checks the first region's row, not the total.\n`COUNT aggregation`: counts rows, which here is the number of regions, not failed orders.\n`first value ... per row`: alerts evaluate a single value for the condition, not each row separately.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/alerts/create"],
["P128", 4, "A Lakeflow job includes a SQL alert task. In a run, the alert query completes and the alert condition is met. What status does the SQL alert task get?", ["Succeeded", "Failed", "Excluded", "Skipped"], "A SQL alert task succeeds when the alert is evaluated successfully, whether or not the condition is met. It only fails on an evaluation error, such as a query error or warehouse connectivity problem.\n`Failed`: a met condition is a normal result, not an error.\n`Excluded`: the status for tasks on an If/else branch that wasn't taken; this task ran.\n`Skipped`: for tasks that didn't run; this one did.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/alert"],
["P130", 4, "A workspace has hundreds of Databricks SQL alerts all scheduled at the top of the hour. Some scheduled evaluations never run, although a manual Run now succeeds. What is the most likely cause?", ["The default limit of 250 simultaneous active alert runs was reached, so those runs were skipped", "Alerts whose last state was OK are not re-evaluated until the condition is edited", "Each SQL warehouse can evaluate at most 25 alerts per hour before it queues the rest", "Scheduled alerts only run when the SQL warehouse is already running at that time"], "Each workspace allows 250 simultaneous active alert runs by default. When hundreds fire at the same moment and all slots are busy, extra scheduled runs are skipped; a manual run works later because slots are free. Stagger the schedules.\n`last state was OK`: alerts are evaluated on every schedule whatever their last state.\n`25 alerts per hour per warehouse`: no such per-warehouse limit applies; the limit is concurrent runs per workspace.\n`warehouse is already running`: a scheduled alert starts the warehouse if needed.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/alerts/create"],
["P131", 4, "A security alert queries `system.access.audit` for account-level events, filtering on the current workspace's `workspace_id`. It never returns account-level rows. Why?", ["Account-level audit events record `workspace_id` as 0", "Account-level audit events are only in `system.access.outbound_network`", "Account-level audit events have a NULL `event_date` value", "Account-level audit events are kept for only 14 days"], "Account-level audit events aren't tied to a workspace, so they record `workspace_id` as `0`. Filtering on the current workspace's ID excludes all of them.\n`only in system.access.outbound_network`: that table logs denied outbound network access, not account audit events.\n`NULL event_date`: account-level events have normal dates, and the filter in question is on `workspace_id`.\n`kept for only 14 days`: they share the audit table's retention; a retention limit wouldn't make them never appear anyway.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/audit-logs"],
["P132", 4, "An alert must flag SQL warehouse queries that spent a long time queued because the warehouse was at capacity. Which `system.query.history` column measures this?", ["`waiting_at_capacity_duration_ms`", "`waiting_for_compute_duration_ms`", "`total_duration_ms`", "`spilled_local_bytes`"], "`waiting_at_capacity_duration_ms` is the time a query sat in the queue because the warehouse had no available capacity: the exact condition to alert on.\n`waiting_for_compute_duration_ms`: time waiting for compute to be provisioned (for example a warehouse starting), not queuing at capacity.\n`total_duration_ms`: the whole query time, mixing queueing with execution.\n`spilled_local_bytes`: data spilled to disk during execution, not time spent queued.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/query-history"],
["P133", 4, "A manager wants a run-success trend for each job over the last six months. The Runs tab in the Jobs UI does not go back that far. What should you use?", ["Query `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline`, which retains 395 days", "Export the job's matrix view, which retains 180 days of runs", "Call `databricks jobs list-runs`, which returns all runs ever made", "Raise the job's run history retention in the job settings"], "The Jobs UI keeps run history for 60 days. `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` keeps 395 days, so six months of runs can be queried and trended with SQL.\n`matrix view ... 180 days`: the matrix view shows the same UI history; it doesn't hold 180 days.\n`databricks jobs list-runs ... all runs ever`: the Jobs API is limited to the same 60-day window.\n`Raise the job's run history retention`: there is no per-job setting to extend it.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/monitor"],
["P134", 4, "A streaming task in a job has a streaming backlog notification threshold set. When are notifications sent?", ["When the average backlog over 10 minutes exceeds the threshold, then every 30 minutes while it stays high", "When any single micro-batch exceeds the threshold, then once for every micro-batch over it", "When the backlog stays over the threshold for one hour, then once per day while it stays high", "When the backlog exceeds the threshold at task start, then once more when the task completes"], "Streaming backlog notifications fire when the average backlog over 10 minutes exceeds the threshold. Databricks then waits 30 minutes before sending another while it stays high. (Pipeline tasks don't support them.)\n`any single micro-batch ... every micro-batch`: the check uses a 10-minute average, not individual batches.\n`one hour ... once per day`: wrong window and wrong resend interval.\n`at task start ... when the task completes`: notifications track the backlog during the run, not at start and end.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/notifications"],
["P135", 8, "In the Spark UI stage page, Summary Metrics show a task duration 75th percentile of 40 seconds and a Max of 4 minutes. According to Databricks guidance, what does this suggest and what is a valid fix?", ["Likely skew; salt the skewed key or tune the AQE skew join thresholds", "Likely spill; set shuffle partitions to a fixed value of 10,000", "Likely small files; run VACUUM on the source table before the job", "Likely an overloaded driver; move to a larger driver node type"], "Databricks' rule of thumb: if the Max task duration is 50% or more above the 75th percentile, a few tasks are doing far more work than the rest, which points to skew. Salting the hot key, AQE skew join settings, liquid clustering or the `REBALANCE` hint spread that work out.\n`spill`: spill shows up in the stage's spill metrics, and a fixed 10,000 shuffle partitions isn't the guidance for it.\n`small files`: VACUUM deletes unreferenced old files; it doesn't compact anything or even out task times.\n`overloaded driver`: driver trouble shows as gaps with no Spark jobs running, not one slow task inside a stage.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/long-spark-stage-page"],
["P136", 8, "The Spark UI jobs timeline shows gaps of several minutes in the middle of a pipeline with no Spark jobs running. Cluster Metrics show a red block on the driver in Server load distribution while workers are blue. What is the likely cause?", ["Driver-side work outside Spark, or an overloaded driver", "Data skew concentrated in the pipeline's longest stage", "Executors spilling shuffle data to local disk", "Autoscaling removing idle executors from the cluster"], "When no Spark jobs run, the time is being spent on the driver: plain Python or other non-Spark code, or planning a complex query. A red driver block with blue workers in Server load distribution confirms the driver is busy while executors sit idle.\n`Data skew`: skew makes a stage run long with a few slow tasks; Spark jobs are still running, so it doesn't create gaps.\n`spilling shuffle data`: spill happens inside running tasks, again visible as slow stages, not as idle time between jobs.\n`Autoscaling removing idle executors`: that is a consequence of idle time, not its cause, and it wouldn't make the driver show red.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/spark-job-gaps"],
["P137", 8, "The Spark UI timeline for a failed job shows several removed executors in red. What should you check first?", ["The compute event log for resizing or lost spot instance messages", "The query profile's Top operators tab for the most expensive operator", "The `system.billing.usage` table for RETRACTION records on the cluster", "The driver's stdout for print statements from the notebook code"], "Removed executors often mean the cluster lost nodes, so start with the compute Event log, which records resizing and lost spot instances. If that explains nothing, read the executor logs from the Executors tab; out-of-memory is the likeliest remaining cause.\n`Top operators`: that tab is in the SQL query profile and ranks expensive operators; it says nothing about why executors were removed.\n`system.billing.usage` RETRACTION records: those are billing corrections, not a diagnostic for lost executors.\n`driver's stdout`: print output from your code rarely explains executor loss and isn't where Databricks says to look first.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/failing-spark-jobs"],
["P138", 8, "A data engineer must analyze the query profile of a slow query that a colleague ran on a SQL warehouse. Besides being the query owner, what access allows them to view it?", ["CAN MONITOR on the SQL warehouse that ran the query", "CAN USE on the SQL warehouse that ran the query", "SELECT on the `system.query.history` system table", "CAN VIEW on the colleague's workspace home folder"], "Query profiles are protected by warehouse permissions: you see a profile if you own the query or hold at least CAN MONITOR on the SQL warehouse that ran it. CAN MONITOR is the level meant for watching other users' activity on a warehouse.\n`CAN USE`: lets you run your own queries on the warehouse, but not view other users' query profiles.\n`SELECT on system.query.history`: gives you query history records, not permission to open another user's query profile.\n`CAN VIEW on the home folder`: workspace folder permissions cover files and notebooks, not warehouse query profiles.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/queries/query-profile"],
["P139", 8, "You configure compute log delivery to a Unity Catalog volume for a job cluster so you can debug failures after the cluster terminates. How are the logs delivered?", ["Every five minutes, archived hourly, in a subfolder named after the cluster ID", "Once at termination, in a subfolder named after the job ID", "Every minute, in a subfolder named after the run ID", "Hourly, in each user's home folder under a Logs folder"], "Compute log delivery copies driver, worker and event logs to the destination every five minutes and archives them hourly. Databricks creates a subfolder named after the compute's `cluster_id`, and Unity Catalog volumes are the recommended destination.\n`Once at termination, job ID`: delivery is periodic during the cluster's life, and the folder is keyed by cluster ID, not job ID.\n`Every minute, run ID`: wrong interval and wrong folder key; the docs give five minutes and `cluster_id`.\n`Hourly, each user's home folder`: logs go to the configured destination path, not to user home folders.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/configure"],
["P140", 8, "A job has tasks A -> B -> C and A -> D. A and D succeeded, B failed, and C did not run because B failed. Which tasks does Repair run re-run?", ["B and C", "A, B and C", "B only", "A, B, C and D"], "Repair run re-runs every task that didn't succeed plus anything that depends on them, and skips tasks that already succeeded. B failed and C was skipped because of B, so B and C run; A and D succeeded and are left alone.\n`A, B and C`: A succeeded, and repair doesn't re-run successful upstream tasks.\n`B only`: C never ran because it depends on B, so it is unsuccessful and is included.\n`A, B, C and D`: that is a full new run (Run now), not a repair.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures"],
["P141", 8, "Task B failed because of a bug. You fix its notebook and increase the job cluster size in the job settings, then click Repair run. Which settings does the repair use?", ["The current job and task settings, including the fix and the new cluster size", "The settings captured when the failed run started, ignoring later edits", "The fixed notebook, but the cluster size captured when the run started", "The original notebook, unless the job is started again with Run now"], "A repair re-runs the unsuccessful tasks with the job and task settings as they are now, not as they were when the run started. So both the notebook fix and the larger cluster apply, which is why you can fix and then repair.\n`settings captured when the run started`: that would make repair useless for fixes; Databricks uses current settings.\n`fixed notebook but old cluster size`: there's no split; cluster changes in the job settings also apply.\n`original notebook unless Run now`: repair already uses the current notebook; Run now starts a brand new run of every task.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures"],
["P142", 8, "In a first repair you overrode the job parameter `load_date`. On a second repair of the same run you want the parameter's original value back. What do you do in the Repair run dialog?", ["Clear the key and value of the overridden parameter", "Set the parameter's value to the literal `null`", "Nothing; each repair reverts overrides automatically", "Delete the first repair from the run's history"], "Values entered in the Repair run dialog override the job's parameters for that repair. On a later repair, clearing both the key and the value of the override is how you go back to the original value.\n`literal null`: that would pass the value `null` as the parameter, not restore the original.\n`each repair reverts overrides automatically`: it doesn't; you must clear the override yourself.\n`Delete the first repair`: repair history isn't something you edit to change parameters, and that isn't how overrides are removed.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures"],
["P143", 8, "A task appended part of its rows to a Delta table and then failed. Before repairing the run, what should the engineer consider?", ["The task re-runs from the start and may duplicate rows; use MERGE or overwrite", "The task resumes from its last commit, so no rows can be written twice", "Repair rolls back the partial write automatically using Delta time travel", "Repair skips tasks with partial writes, so they must be run with Run now"], "Repair re-runs each unsuccessful task from the beginning, and Lakeflow Jobs does not make tasks idempotent. An append that already wrote some rows will write them again, so use MERGE or overwrite where duplicates matter.\n`resumes from its last commit`: tasks aren't checkpointed by repair; the whole task runs again.\n`rolls back the partial write automatically`: repair does no Delta rollback; the committed rows stay in the table.\n`skips tasks with partial writes`: a failed task is exactly what repair re-runs.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures"],
["P144", 8, "From the CLI, you want to repair a finished, failed job run by re-running all of its failed tasks. Which command is correct?", ["`databricks jobs repair-run <run-id> --rerun-all-failed-tasks`", "`databricks jobs run-now <job-id> --rerun-all-failed-tasks`", "`databricks jobs repair-run <job-id> --only-failed-tasks`", "`databricks jobs get-run <run-id> --rerun-all-failed-tasks`"], "`databricks jobs repair-run` takes the run ID of a run that is not in progress, and `--rerun-all-failed-tasks` re-runs every failed task in it. Repair always works on an existing run, so it needs the run ID.\n`run-now <job-id>`: starts a brand new run of the whole job; it doesn't repair an old one.\n`repair-run <job-id> --only-failed-tasks`: repair needs the run ID, not the job ID, and the flag is `--rerun-all-failed-tasks`.\n`get-run`: only fetches details about a run; it changes nothing.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/reference/jobs-commands"],
["P145", 8, "A bundle variable `catalog` has a top-level default of `dev`, the `prod` target sets it to `prod_cat`, and the CI runner exports `BUNDLE_VAR_catalog=hotfix_cat`. The deploy command also passes `--var='catalog=ci_cat'`. Which value is used?", ["`ci_cat`", "`hotfix_cat`", "`prod_cat`", "`dev`"], "The CLI takes the first value it finds in this order: `--var` on the command line, `BUNDLE_VAR_` environment variables, `variable-overrides.json`, the target's `variables`, then the top-level default. `--var` was passed, so `ci_cat` wins.\n`hotfix_cat`: environment variables come second, below the explicit `--var` flag.\n`prod_cat`: the target value is only used when no flag, environment variable or overrides file sets the variable.\n`dev`: the top-level default is the last fallback.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/variables"],
["P146", 8, "A developer deploys a Declarative Automation Bundle to a target with `mode: development`. The bundle defines a job with a daily schedule. How is the job deployed?", ["With a `[dev <short_name>]` name prefix and its schedule paused", "With its original name and its schedule left active", "With its original name and its schedule paused", "With a `[dev <short_name>]` prefix and run once at deploy"], "`mode: development` keeps dev deployments from clashing with production: resource names get a `[dev <short_name>]` prefix, a `dev` tag is added, and all schedules and triggers are paused so the copy doesn't run on its own.\n`original name, schedule active`: that describes a deployment without development mode.\n`original name, schedule paused`: development mode also adds the name prefix.\n`run once at deploy`: deploying never runs a job; you start it with `bundle run`.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes"],
["P147", 8, "A `prod` target with `mode: production` specifies the Git branch `main`. A release engineer runs `databricks bundle deploy` for that target from branch `hotfix-12`. What happens?", ["Branch validation fails the deploy unless `--force` is used", "The deploy succeeds and checks out `main` before uploading", "The deploy succeeds but pauses every job in the bundle", "Branch validation fails the deploy and cannot be overridden"], "`mode: production` adds safety checks, including that the current Git branch matches the branch set on the target. Deploying from `hotfix-12` fails, and `--force` overrides this and other blocking validations.\n`checks out main`: the CLI never switches your branch; it validates and stops.\n`succeeds but pauses every job`: pausing schedules is development-mode behavior, not production.\n`cannot be overridden`: `--force` exists for exactly this case.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes"],
["P148", 8, "A production job was created by hand in the UI. The team now defines the same job in a bundle and wants the bundle to manage the existing job instead of creating a duplicate. Which command should they run?", ["`databricks bundle deployment bind <job-key> <job-id>`", "`databricks bundle sync --full --watch <job-id>`", "`databricks bundle validate --output json <job-id>`", "`databricks bundle open <job-key> --force-pull`"], "`databricks bundle deployment bind` links a resource key in the bundle to an existing workspace resource ID. The next deploy then updates that job instead of creating a second copy.\n`bundle sync`: uploads local files to the workspace; it doesn't link job definitions.\n`bundle validate`: only checks the bundle configuration and prints it; it changes nothing in the workspace.\n`bundle open`: opens a bundle resource in the browser; it doesn't bind anything.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands"],
["P149", 8, "A bundle job passes `${var.run_date}` as a task parameter. An engineer wants a different date on each run without redeploying. What should they do?", ["Define a job parameter and pass it at run time with `bundle run --params`", "Export a new `BUNDLE_VAR_run_date` value before each `bundle run`", "Edit `variable-overrides.json` before each `bundle run` of the job", "Pass `--var='run_date=...'` to `bundle run` for each new run"], "Bundle variables like `${var.run_date}` are substituted when the bundle is deployed, so the deployed job holds a fixed value. A value that changes per run must be a job parameter, which `databricks bundle run --params` sets as k=v pairs at run time.\n`BUNDLE_VAR_run_date`: still a bundle variable, resolved at deploy, so it only changes after a redeploy.\n`variable-overrides.json`: same problem; it feeds bundle variables at deploy time.\n`--var`: also sets a bundle variable, which needs a redeploy to reach the deployed job.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/variables"],
["P150", 8, "In the Databricks-recommended CI/CD flow with Git folders, how is the production Git folder updated after a pull request is merged?", ["Automation calls the Repos API to pull changes into the production folder", "Each developer pulls into the production folder after their PR merges", "The production folder syncs itself after every push to any branch", "An admin deletes and re-clones the production folder for each release"], "In the recommended flow, production Git folders are never updated by hand. When a pull request merges, automation such as GitHub Actions or a scheduled job calls the Repos API to pull the new commits into the production folder.\n`Each developer pulls`: manual updates are what the guidance avoids; they are error prone and need broad permissions.\n`syncs itself after every push`: Git folders don't sync automatically, and any branch reaching production would be unsafe.\n`deletes and re-clones`: unnecessary; a pull through the API updates the folder in place.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/ci-cd-techniques-with-repos"],
["P151", 8, "Which setup matches Databricks guidance for a production Git folder that automation keeps up to date?", ["A service principal with a Git credential runs the update; most users get run-only access", "An admin's personal access token runs the update; all project users get Can Edit", "Each developer's own Git credential runs the update; project users get Can Manage", "A shared user account runs the update; all workspace users get Can Manage"], "Automation should run as a service principal with its own Git credential, so updates don't depend on a person. Most users get run-only access to production folders, and only admins and service principals can edit.\n`admin's personal access token`: ties production to one person, and Can Edit for all users breaks the restriction.\n`developer's own credential, Can Manage`: personal credentials and broad rights are what the guidance avoids.\n`shared user account, all users Can Manage`: a shared account isn't a service principal, and Can Manage for everyone is far too broad.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/ci-cd-techniques-with-repos"],
["P152", 8, "Databricks recommends that production jobs reference a remote Git repository rather than a Git folder path. How does a remote Git source behave at run time?", ["It snapshots the given branch, tag or commit at run start, and all tasks use one commit", "Each task pulls the newest commit when it starts, so tasks can use different commits", "Code is copied into the workspace when the job is created and is never refreshed", "The job runs from the job owner's Git folder on whichever branch is checked out"], "A job with a remote Git source snapshots the given branch, tag or commit when the run starts, and every task in the run uses that same commit. Code can't change between tasks part way through a run.\n`Each task pulls the newest commit`: that would let tasks run different code; all tasks share one commit.\n`copied when the job is created`: the snapshot is taken at each run start, so new commits are picked up on the next run.\n`job owner's Git folder`: that is the Git folder path approach the guidance moves away from.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/git"],
["P153", 8, "A notebook task uses a remote Git source and fails when it writes a temporary file into the repository's workspace path. Where should it write data instead?", ["Driver ephemeral storage for temporary data, or a volume or table for persistent data", "The job owner's Git folder, which is writable by tasks using a remote Git source", "A workspace file next to the notebook, after enabling sparse checkout on the source", "The repository root, after switching the Git reference from a branch to a tag"], "Tasks that run code from a remote Git repository can't write to workspace files in that checkout. Write temporary data to the driver's ephemeral storage and anything that must persist to a Unity Catalog volume or table.\n`job owner's Git folder`: a remote Git source doesn't run from a Git folder, and writing there isn't the recommended fix.\n`sparse checkout`: that limits which files a Git folder checks out; it doesn't make the repo path writable.\n`switch branch to tag`: the type of Git reference doesn't affect whether the task can write.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/git"],
["P154", 8, "After pulling a teammate's changes into your Git folder, a notebook's cell outputs and widgets are gone. What explains this?", ["Git operations that pull upstream changes clear the notebook state", "A pull in a Git folder runs `git reset --hard` on the working branch", "Notebook outputs are removed from Git folders after 30 days on disk", "Widgets are stored only on the default branch of the Git folder"], "Git operations that change a notebook's source, such as pulling upstream changes, clear the notebook's state, including cell outputs and widgets. The code arrives but the run results don't.\n`git reset --hard`: a pull merges upstream changes; it isn't documented as a hard reset.\n`removed after 30 days`: there's no such time-based clean-up; the pull itself cleared the state.\n`widgets stored only on the default branch`: widgets aren't tied to a branch; the state was cleared by the pull.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/git-operations-with-repos"]
);

PBANK.push(
// ---------- Section 5: Cost & Performance Optimization ----------
// Objective: managed tables, predictive optimization, liquid clustering reduce overhead
["P155", 5, "A platform team enables predictive optimization at the catalog level. The catalog holds Unity Catalog managed Delta tables and several external Delta tables registered on S3 paths. Which tables will predictive optimization maintain?",
 ["Only the Unity Catalog managed tables", "All managed and external tables in the catalog", "Only the external tables registered on S3 paths", "Only tables that already use liquid clustering"],
 "Predictive optimization only runs on Unity Catalog managed tables, because Databricks needs to manage the storage layout to run OPTIMIZE and VACUUM safely. Enabling it on a catalog skips external tables and tables loaded as sharing recipients.\n`All managed and external tables`: external tables are excluded however the setting is inherited.\n`Only the external tables`: the reverse of the rule.\n`Only liquid clustered tables`: liquid clustering isn't required; any UC managed table qualifies.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization"],
["P156", 5, "A data owner wants predictive optimization for every table in schema `sales.core`, while leaving the rest of the `sales` catalog at its current setting. Which statement does this?",
 ["`ALTER SCHEMA sales.core ENABLE PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION`", "`ALTER SCHEMA sales.core SET DBPROPERTIES ('predictiveOptimization' = 'true')`", "`ALTER CATALOG sales INHERIT PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION FOR SCHEMA core`", "`SET spark.databricks.predictiveOptimization.enabled = true` in each job"],
 "Predictive optimization is set on a catalog, schema or table with `ALTER CATALOG | SCHEMA | TABLE ... ENABLE | DISABLE | INHERIT PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION`. Enabling it on `sales.core` covers that schema's tables and leaves the catalog setting alone.\n`SET DBPROPERTIES`: schema properties aren't how predictive optimization is turned on.\n`ALTER CATALOG ... FOR SCHEMA`: not valid syntax; `INHERIT` is set on the object itself and takes the parent's setting.\n`spark.databricks.predictiveOptimization.enabled`: predictive optimization is a property of UC objects, not a per-job Spark setting.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization"],
["P157", 5, "A nightly Lakeflow Job runs `OPTIMIZE` on a liquid clustered Unity Catalog managed table. Predictive optimization has just been enabled for that table. What does Databricks recommend doing with the nightly job?",
 ["Disable the scheduled OPTIMIZE job", "Change it to run `OPTIMIZE FULL` every night", "Keep it and add `ZORDER BY` on the clustering keys", "Move it to run every hour instead of nightly"],
 "Once predictive optimization is on, Databricks runs OPTIMIZE on the table automatically when it judges it worthwhile. A scheduled OPTIMIZE job then duplicates work and cost, so Databricks recommends disabling it.\n`OPTIMIZE FULL` every night: FULL reclusters all data, which is expensive and meant for occasions like changing keys.\n`ZORDER BY`: Z-order can't be used with liquid clustering.\n`run every hour`: running more often makes the duplication worse.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P158", 5, "A managed table was maintained for years with `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY (customer_id)`. Predictive optimization is now enabled on it. How does predictive optimization treat the Z-ordering?",
 ["It runs OPTIMIZE without ZORDER and ignores Z-ordered files", "It reruns ZORDER BY using the last Z-order columns", "It converts the Z-order columns into clustering keys", "It skips the table until ZORDER is removed manually"],
 "Predictive optimization's OPTIMIZE runs plain compaction without ZORDER, so it ignores files that were Z-ordered. Liquid clustering is the layout predictive optimization maintains, so move to it for an automatically kept layout.\n`reruns ZORDER BY`: it never runs Z-order.\n`converts Z-order columns into clustering keys`: no automatic conversion happens; you change the table yourself.\n`skips the table`: Z-ordered tables aren't skipped; they are optimized without Z-order.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization"],
["P159", 5, "A FinOps analyst wants to see which maintenance operations predictive optimization ran on each table, plus their cost and impact. Which source should they query?",
 ["`system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history`", "`system.billing.predictive_optimization_usage`", "`system.compute.maintenance_operations`", "`system.access.table_maintenance_log`"],
 "`system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history` records each operation predictive optimization ran, per table, with its cost and impact. Predictive optimization runs on serverless jobs compute and is billed under a serverless jobs SKU.\n`system.billing.predictive_optimization_usage`: not a system table; billing data lives in `system.billing.usage`, without per-operation detail.\n`system.compute.maintenance_operations`: not a system table.\n`system.access.table_maintenance_log`: not a system table; the `access` schema holds audit and lineage data.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization"],

// Objective: choose deletion vectors, liquid clustering, CLUSTER BY AUTO
["P160", 5, "A Unity Catalog managed table is queried by many teams, and their filter columns change every few months. Predictive optimization is enabled. The engineers don't want to choose or revisit clustering keys. What should they do?",
 ["Run `ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY AUTO`", "Run `ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY NONE` and rely on ingestion order", "Partition the table by the most common filter column", "Schedule `OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY` on all filter columns"],
 "`CLUSTER BY AUTO` turns on automatic liquid clustering: predictive optimization analyzes the query workload, picks clustering keys and changes them as filters change. Nobody has to choose or revisit keys.\n`CLUSTER BY NONE`: removes clustering keys entirely, so nothing adapts to the filters.\n`Partition by the most common filter`: a fixed layout that has to be redone when filters change, and not recommended for new tables.\n`ZORDER BY on all filter columns`: manual, fixed keys, and Z-order loses effectiveness as columns are added.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P161", 5, "Which requirement must a Delta table meet to use automatic liquid clustering (`CLUSTER BY AUTO`)?",
 ["It is a Unity Catalog managed table with predictive optimization enabled", "It is an external table with change data feed enabled", "It is a partitioned table with deletion vectors turned off", "It is a table read only through SQL warehouses"],
 "Automatic liquid clustering needs a Unity Catalog managed table with predictive optimization enabled, because predictive optimization is what selects the keys and runs the clustering. Delta tables also need Databricks Runtime 15.4 LTS or above.\n`external table with change data feed`: external tables can't use predictive optimization, and CDF is unrelated.\n`partitioned, deletion vectors off`: liquid clustering can't be combined with partitioning.\n`read only through SQL warehouses`: how the table is queried isn't a requirement.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P162", 5, "A table uses `CLUSTER BY AUTO`. An engineer recreates it with `CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE t (...)` and no CLUSTER BY clause. What happens to automatic clustering?",
 ["It is turned off and previously selected keys are dropped", "It stays on and the previously selected keys are reused", "The statement fails until `CLUSTER BY NONE` is run first", "The keys are kept but OPTIMIZE no longer runs on the table"],
 "`CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE` defines the table afresh, and without `CLUSTER BY AUTO` the new definition has no automatic clustering. The keys predictive optimization picked are dropped. Include `CLUSTER BY AUTO` in the statement to keep it.\n`stays on, keys reused`: the replace statement resets clustering to what it declares.\n`fails until CLUSTER BY NONE`: the statement succeeds; no prior step is needed.\n`keys kept but OPTIMIZE stops`: the keys are dropped, not kept.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P163", 5, "A Delta table has deletion vectors enabled. A job runs `DELETE FROM events WHERE device_id = 42`, touching a few rows in many large Parquet files. What does the DELETE do to those files?",
 ["Records the removed rows in deletion vectors and leaves the files unchanged for now", "Rewrites every affected Parquet file without the deleted rows", "Moves the deleted rows into a separate folder for VACUUM to remove", "Marks each affected file as removed and rewrites it at the next read"],
 "With deletion vectors, DELETE writes small files marking which rows are removed and leaves the Parquet files untouched, so it is much cheaper. Readers skip marked rows at query time, and the files are rewritten later by OPTIMIZE, `REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)` or auto-compaction.\n`Rewrites every affected Parquet file`: that is the behavior without deletion vectors.\n`separate folder for VACUUM`: rows aren't moved anywhere; they stay in the files and are marked.\n`rewrites it at the next read`: reads apply the deletion vector on the fly without rewriting files.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/deletion-vectors"],
["P164", 5, "A team enables `delta.enableDeletionVectors` on a table that is also read by an external client whose Delta version does not support deletion vectors. What is the effect?",
 ["The table protocol is upgraded and that client can no longer read the table", "The client sees deleted rows until the next VACUUM runs", "Databricks keeps a compatibility copy of the table without deletion vectors", "Nothing changes for that client until the first OPTIMIZE runs"],
 "Deletion vectors are a Delta table feature, and enabling one upgrades the table protocol. Clients that don't support the feature can no longer read the table at all. On Databricks Runtime 16.4 LTS and above you can drop the feature to restore compatibility.\n`sees deleted rows until VACUUM`: an unsupported client can't read the table at all.\n`compatibility copy`: Databricks doesn't keep a second copy of the table.\n`until the first OPTIMIZE`: the protocol upgrade happens when the feature is enabled, not later.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/deletion-vectors"],

// Objective: caching for repeated reads (the exam guide's "Delta cache"; the docs call it the disk cache)
["P165", 5, "An iterative job on classic compute rereads the same large Delta table many times, and each pass spends most of its time reading from cloud storage. How can the team speed up the repeated reads without changing the code?", ["Use a worker type with local SSDs, where the disk cache is on by default and fills on first read", "Add `CACHE SELECT * FROM` the table at the start of each pass to load it into the disk cache", "Run `OPTIMIZE` on the table between passes so each later read scans fewer, larger files", "Raise `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` so each pass reads the table with more parallel tasks"], "The disk cache stores copies of remote Parquet data, including Delta tables, on workers' local storage, so later reads skip cloud storage. It is on by default for some node types, with SSD worker types recommended, and fills automatically on first read with no code change.\n`CACHE SELECT`: changes the code, and it is ignored on Databricks Runtime 14.2 and above.\n`OPTIMIZE between passes`: fewer files can help a bit, but every pass still reads from cloud storage.\n`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`: controls partitions after a shuffle, not how the table is read from storage.", "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/disk-cache"],
["P166", 5, "How does the disk cache differ from the Apache Spark cache?",
 ["The disk cache fills automatically on first read; the Spark cache needs explicit code", "The Spark cache fills automatically on first read; the disk cache needs explicit code", "The disk cache works for any DataFrame; the Spark cache only for Parquet files", "Both caches need `CACHE TABLE` before the first read of a table"],
 "The disk cache works at the file level: when enabled, Parquet data is cached on workers' local disks automatically the first time it is read. The Spark cache only holds what you ask for with `cache()` or `persist()`, but it works for any DataFrame or RDD.\n`Spark cache fills automatically`: reversed; the Spark cache needs explicit code.\n`disk cache works for any DataFrame`: reversed; the disk cache is for Parquet-based data, and the Spark cache works for any DataFrame.\n`Both need CACHE TABLE`: the disk cache needs no command at all.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/disk-cache"],
["P167", 5, "A cluster has the disk cache enabled and repeatedly reads a Delta table. Another job then runs a MERGE that rewrites some of the table's files. What must the reading cluster do to avoid stale results?",
 ["Nothing, because the cache detects changed files and drops stale entries", "Run `CLEAR CACHE` before every read of the table", "Restart the cluster so that the cached files are evicted", "Toggle `spark.databricks.io.cache.enabled` off and on again"],
 "The disk cache detects when data files are created, deleted, modified or overwritten and updates itself. Delta's MERGE writes new files, so the reading cluster picks up the new version without any manual step.\n`CLEAR CACHE`: not needed for the disk cache; that command clears the Spark cache.\n`Restart the cluster`: unnecessary; stale entries are handled automatically.\n`Toggle spark.databricks.io.cache.enabled`: unnecessary, and turning the cache off doesn't delete data already cached.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/disk-cache"],
["P168", 5, "From a notebook, an engineer wants to check whether the disk cache is turned on for the current cluster. Which configuration should they read with `spark.conf.get`?",
 ["`spark.databricks.io.cache.enabled`", "`spark.databricks.delta.cache.enabled`", "`spark.databricks.io.cache.maxDiskUsage`", "`spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.enabled`"],
 "`spark.databricks.io.cache.enabled` is the switch that turns the disk cache on or off, so reading it tells you whether the cache is active.\n`spark.databricks.delta.cache.enabled`: not the documented setting; the disk cache used to be called the Delta cache, but its switch is `io.cache.enabled`.\n`spark.databricks.io.cache.maxDiskUsage`: sets how much disk each node may use for the cache, not whether it is on.\n`spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.enabled`: relates to Spark's in-memory cache, not the disk cache.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/disk-cache"],
["P169", 5, "To warm up a dashboard, an engineer adds `CACHE SELECT * FROM sales.orders` to a query that runs on a SQL warehouse. What happens?",
 ["The command is ignored and an enhanced disk caching algorithm is used", "The table is loaded into the disk cache of every warehouse cluster", "The table is pinned in memory until the warehouse stops", "The statement fails because it needs classic compute"],
 "On SQL warehouses and Databricks Runtime 14.2 and above, `CACHE SELECT` is ignored and an enhanced disk caching algorithm decides what to cache. The statement runs without error but doesn't warm anything.\n`loaded into every warehouse cluster`: that was the old behavior; the command is now ignored.\n`pinned in memory`: that describes the Spark cache, not the disk cache.\n`fails because it needs classic compute`: it doesn't fail; it is silently ignored.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/disk-cache"],

// Objective: Change Data Feed
["P170", 5, "A downstream team needs row-level changes from the existing Delta table `silver.orders`. Which statement turns on change data feed for it?",
 ["`ALTER TABLE silver.orders SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true)`", "`ALTER TABLE silver.orders SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeLog = true)`", "`ALTER TABLE silver.orders ENABLE CHANGE DATA FEED`", "`SET spark.databricks.delta.cdf.enabled = true` before each write"],
 "Change data feed is a table property: set `delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true` at CREATE TABLE or with `ALTER TABLE ... SET TBLPROPERTIES`. Changes are recorded only from that point on.\n`delta.enableChangeLog`: not a Delta property.\n`ENABLE CHANGE DATA FEED`: not valid SQL syntax.\n`spark.databricks.delta.cdf.enabled`: not a real setting, and CDF is enabled on the table, not per write.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/delta-change-data-feed"],
["P171", 5, "A job reads the change data feed of `silver.customers` and should apply only the new values of inserted and updated rows. Deletes and before-update values are handled elsewhere. Which filter fits?",
 ["`_change_type IN ('insert', 'update_postimage')`", "`_change_type IN ('insert', 'update_preimage')`", "`_change_type IN ('insert', 'update')`", "`_change_type <> 'delete'`"],
 "`_change_type` takes four values: `insert`, `update_preimage` (row before the update), `update_postimage` (row after) and `delete`. New values of inserted and updated rows are `insert` plus `update_postimage`.\n`update_preimage`: that is the old value before the update, which this job doesn't want.\n`'update'`: not a value of `_change_type`, so updates would be missed.\n`<> 'delete'`: also returns the preimages.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/delta-change-data-feed"],
["P172", 5, "An engineer runs `SELECT * FROM table_changes('silver.orders', 5, 10)`. Which table versions are included?",
 ["Versions 5 through 10, both included", "Versions 5 through 9, excluding 10", "Versions 6 through 10, excluding 5", "Only the net change between 5 and 10"],
 "Change data feed reads include both the start and end versions, so this returns row-level changes from versions 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10. If you leave out the end version, the read goes up to the latest version.\n`excluding 10`: the end version is inclusive.\n`excluding 5`: the start version is inclusive too.\n`net change`: CDF returns each row change with its `_commit_version`, not a netted summary.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/delta-change-data-feed"],
["P173", 5, "A new Structured Streaming query reads `spark.readStream.option('readChangeFeed', 'true').table('silver.orders')` with no starting version. What does it return first?",
 ["The current table snapshot as insert records, then later changes", "Only changes committed after the stream was started", "Every change since change data feed was enabled", "An error, because a starting version is required"],
 "A new change data feed stream with no starting version first returns the table's current snapshot as `insert` records, then streams later changes. Set `startingVersion` if the target already holds that data. Only batch CDF reads require a starting version.\n`Only changes after start`: you get that with `startingVersion`, not by default.\n`Every change since CDF was enabled`: the stream starts from a snapshot, not the full history.\n`An error`: streams don't require a starting version; batch reads do.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/delta-change-data-feed"],
["P174", 5, "An audit team plans to use a table's change data feed as the permanent history of every change since the table was created. Why won't this work?",
 ["Change records follow table retention, and VACUUM removes change data files", "Change data feed records inserts only, not updates or deletes", "Change data feed keeps changes only for the last 10 versions", "Change data feed can't be read again after OPTIMIZE runs"],
 "Change data files follow the table's retention settings, and VACUUM deletes them like other old files. CDF is for moving changes downstream, not a permanent archive; to keep history, write the changes to a target table.\n`inserts only`: CDF records inserts, updates (pre and post images) and deletes.\n`last 10 versions`: there's no fixed version count; retention is time based.\n`can't be read after OPTIMIZE`: OPTIMIZE doesn't stop CDF reads; it just doesn't produce change records.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/delta-change-data-feed"],

// Objective: query profile
["P175", 5, "An analyst wants the query profile of a dashboard query, but Query History shows no profile because the result came from the query cache. What is a simple way to get a profile?",
 ["Rerun the query with a trivial change, such as a different LIMIT", "Turn on verbose mode from the profile's kebab menu", "Ask for CAN MANAGE on the SQL warehouse", "Import the profile JSON from the dashboard settings"],
 "A result served from the query cache never ran on the warehouse, so there is no execution to profile. A trivial change, such as altering or removing the LIMIT, misses the cache and forces a real run that produces a profile.\n`verbose mode`: changes how much an existing profile shows; there's no profile to change.\n`CAN MANAGE`: permissions don't create a profile for a cached result.\n`Import the profile JSON from dashboard settings`: dashboards don't store profiles to import.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/queries/query-profile"],
["P176", 5, "A table is liquid clustered on `customer_id`. A slow query filters only on `order_date`, and its scan reads most of the table's bytes. Which query performance insight describes this?",
 ["COVERAGE_FILTER_KEYS_CLUSTERING", "DATA_SKEW", "EXPLODING_JOIN", "WIDE_PROJECTION"],
 "COVERAGE_FILTER_KEYS_CLUSTERING means the scan doesn't filter on the table's clustering keys, so data skipping can't prune files and it reads more than needed. Filtering on `customer_id`, or reviewing the clustering keys, reduces bytes read.\n`DATA_SKEW`: about uneven work across tasks, not missing clustering-key filters.\n`EXPLODING_JOIN`: about a join producing far more rows than its inputs; this query is a scan problem.\n`WIDE_PROJECTION`: not about which columns the filter uses; the issue here is the filter missing the clustering key.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/queries/performance-insights"],
["P177", 5, "In a query profile, a join operator outputs far more rows than it reads from its inputs and dominates time spent. What is the recommended fix for this insight?",
 ["Refine the join condition or reduce input rows on both sides", "Use a larger SQL warehouse so the join does not spill", "Enable predictive optimization on both joined tables", "Raise the warehouse's maximum number of clusters"],
 "An operator that outputs far more rows than it reads is the EXPLODING_JOIN insight, usually a loose or missing join condition. Work out which results you need, then tighten the join condition or reduce input rows on both sides.\n`larger SQL warehouse`: more compute doesn't stop the join producing too many rows.\n`predictive optimization`: maintains table layout; it can't fix a join condition.\n`maximum number of clusters`: scaling out helps with many concurrent queries, not one bad join.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/queries/performance-insights"],
["P178", 5, "A query profile shows the COVERAGE_STATS_DELTA insight for a filter on `region`, the 40th column of a Unity Catalog external Delta table. What fixes the data-skipping gap?",
 ["Add `region` to `delta.dataSkippingStatsColumns`, then run `ANALYZE TABLE ... COMPUTE DELTA STATISTICS`", "Set `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` to auto and rerun the query", "Run `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY (region)` without collecting statistics", "Set `delta.dataSkippingNumIndexedCols` to 0 and rerun the query"],
 "Delta collects data-skipping stats on the first 32 columns by default, so the 40th column has none. `delta.dataSkippingStatsColumns` adds `region` for future writes, and `ANALYZE TABLE ... COMPUTE DELTA STATISTICS` recomputes stats for data already written.\n`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`: affects shuffles, not file statistics.\n`ZORDER BY (region)` without stats: data skipping needs stats on `region`, which still wouldn't exist.\n`dataSkippingNumIndexedCols` to 0: turns off stats collection, making skipping worse.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/data-skipping"],
["P179", 5, "A query profile shows a sort-merge join with a shuffle on both sides, but at runtime one side turns out to be small. With default settings, how can Databricks change this join?",
 ["AQE switches it to a broadcast hash join if that side is under 30MB", "AQE switches it to a shuffle hash join if that side is under 256MB", "Photon switches it to a nested loop join if that side is under 64MB", "Predictive optimization rewrites the join after the query finishes"],
 "Adaptive query execution, on by default, re-plans using real sizes as stages finish. If one side of a sort-merge join turns out to be under `spark.databricks.adaptive.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` (default 30MB), AQE switches to a broadcast hash join and avoids the shuffle.\n`shuffle hash join, 256MB`: the documented runtime conversion for a small side is to broadcast hash join, with a 30MB default threshold.\n`Photon, nested loop join`: nested loop joins aren't a speed-up, and this isn't how runtime join changes work.\n`Predictive optimization rewrites the join`: it maintains tables; it never changes queries.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/aqe"],

// Objective: liquid clustering vs partitioning / ZORDER
["P180", 5, "A new Delta table will hold about 600 GB and is filtered on `event_date` and `device_id`. Which layout follows Databricks guidance?",
 ["No partitioning; liquid clustering on event_date and device_id", "Partition by event_date and Z-order by device_id", "Partition by device_id and Z-order by event_date", "Partition by event_date and cluster by device_id"],
 "Databricks advises not partitioning tables under about 1 TB, since partitions create many small files, and recommends liquid clustering for new tables. A 600 GB table filtered on two columns should be liquid clustered on both.\n`Partition by event_date, Z-order by device_id`: partitioning isn't advised at this size, and liquid clustering replaces this pattern.\n`Partition by device_id`: a high-cardinality partition column produces huge numbers of tiny files.\n`Partition and cluster`: liquid clustering can't be combined with partitioning.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/partitions"],
["P181", 5, "A table is partitioned by `region` and Z-ordered by `customer_id`. When moving it to liquid clustering, which clustering keys does Databricks recommend?",
 ["Both `region` and `customer_id`", "Only `customer_id`", "Only `region`", "A hashed bucket column built from `customer_id`"],
 "When a table used both partitioning and Z-order, Databricks recommends making both the partition columns and the ZORDER BY columns the liquid clustering keys. That keeps data skipping for every filter the old layout served.\n`Only customer_id`: filters on `region` would lose the pruning partitioning gave them.\n`Only region`: loses the skipping Z-order gave on `customer_id`.\n`hashed bucket column`: not the guidance; liquid clustering uses the real columns.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P182", 5, "A legacy table is partitioned by a generated `event_day` column derived from the `event_ts` timestamp. When converting it to liquid clustering, which key should be used?",
 ["`event_ts`, without creating the generated column", "`event_day`, keeping the generated column", "Both `event_day` and `event_ts`", "A new generated `event_hour` column"],
 "When a partition column was generated to lower cardinality, such as a date from a timestamp, use the original column as the clustering key. Liquid clustering handles high-cardinality columns, so the generated column isn't needed.\n`event_day`: the generated column was a partitioning workaround that clustering doesn't need.\n`Both`: redundant; `event_ts` already covers `event_day` filters.\n`event_hour`: still a generated column, which the guidance says not to create.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P183", 5, "On a 20 TB liquid clustered table, an engineer runs `ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (country, product_id)` to replace the old keys. What happens to the existing data?",
 ["It stays as is until `OPTIMIZE t FULL` reclusters it", "It is rewritten right away by the ALTER TABLE statement", "It is rewritten by the next VACUUM on the table", "It can't be read until the next OPTIMIZE finishes"],
 "Changing clustering keys only affects later writes and OPTIMIZE runs; existing data isn't rewritten. `OPTIMIZE t FULL` reclusters all existing data with the new keys, which can take hours on a large table.\n`rewritten right away`: ALTER TABLE only changes metadata.\n`rewritten by VACUUM`: VACUUM deletes old unreferenced files; it never reclusters.\n`can't be read`: the table stays fully readable throughout.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],
["P184", 5, "A large Unity Catalog managed Delta table is partitioned by `sale_date`. The team wants to move it to liquid clustering in place on Databricks Runtime 18.1 and let predictive optimization adapt the keys over time. Which statement fits?",
 ["`ALTER TABLE t REPLACE PARTITIONED BY WITH CLUSTER BY AUTO`", "`ALTER TABLE t DROP PARTITIONED BY ADD CLUSTER BY AUTO`", "`ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY AUTO PARTITIONED BY NONE`", "`OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY AUTO`"],
 "`ALTER TABLE ... REPLACE PARTITIONED BY WITH CLUSTER BY AUTO` converts a partitioned table in place. It starts from the current partition columns as keys and lets predictive optimization adapt them. It needs Databricks Runtime 18.1+ and a Unity Catalog managed table.\n`DROP PARTITIONED BY ADD CLUSTER BY AUTO`: not valid syntax.\n`CLUSTER BY AUTO PARTITIONED BY NONE`: not valid syntax either.\n`OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY AUTO`: there's no automatic Z-order, and Z-order isn't liquid clustering.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta/clustering"],

// ---------- Section 6: Ensuring Data Security and Compliance ----------
// Objective: least-privilege ACLs
["P185", 6, "The `analysts` group needs to query only the table `prod.sales.orders`. Which set of grants follows least privilege?",
 ["SELECT on the table, USE SCHEMA on `prod.sales`, USE CATALOG on `prod`", "SELECT on the table only, with no grants on the parent objects", "ALL PRIVILEGES on the schema `prod.sales` and USE CATALOG on `prod`", "USE CATALOG and SELECT on the whole `prod` catalog"],
 "Reading a table needs SELECT on the table plus USE CATALOG on its catalog and USE SCHEMA on its schema; the USE privileges only let you reach the object. Granting exactly these gives access to one table.\n`SELECT on the table only`: without USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA the group can't reach it.\n`ALL PRIVILEGES on the schema`: grants every privilege on every table in it, far beyond one read.\n`SELECT on the whole catalog`: inherited by every current and future table in `prod`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges/privileges"],
["P186", 6, "A platform engineer must investigate slow queries that other users ran on a SQL warehouse, including their query profiles, without being able to manage or change the warehouse. Which warehouse permission fits?",
 ["CAN MONITOR", "CAN USE", "CAN MANAGE", "IS OWNER"],
 "CAN MONITOR on a SQL warehouse lets you see its queries and their profiles, including other users', without rights to edit or manage the warehouse. It is the lowest level that fits.\n`CAN USE`: lets you run queries on the warehouse but not see other users' profiles.\n`CAN MANAGE`: also lets them change, start and stop the warehouse, which isn't wanted.\n`IS OWNER`: full control of the warehouse, even broader.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/queries/query-profile"],
["P187", 6, "A data steward is granted MANAGE on a table so they can administer its grants. When they query the table, they get a permission error. Why?",
 ["MANAGE doesn't grant SELECT; they must grant it to themselves explicitly", "MANAGE applies only to catalogs and schemas, not to tables", "MANAGE takes effect only after ownership is transferred to them", "MANAGE gives data access only on external tables"],
 "MANAGE lets a user administer an object (grant, revoke, change owner, drop) but doesn't give them every privilege. To read the data they must grant themselves SELECT, and still need USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA on the parents.\n`only catalogs and schemas`: MANAGE can be granted on tables too.\n`only after ownership is transferred`: MANAGE works without ownership.\n`only on external tables`: MANAGE never grants data access, whatever the table type.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges/privileges"],

// Objective: ABAC with governed tags
["P188", 6, "A governance team writes an ABAC row filter policy on a catalog. The UDF must receive whichever column in each table carries the governed tag `region`. Which clauses do this?",
 ["`MATCH COLUMNS has_tag('region') AS rgn` with `USING COLUMNS (rgn)`", "`WHEN has_tag('region')` with `ON COLUMN region`", "`FOR COLUMNS TAGGED 'region'` with `USING (region)`", "`MATCH TABLES has_tag('region')` with `ON (rgn)`"],
 "In an ABAC policy, `MATCH COLUMNS has_tag('region') AS rgn` finds the column in each table carrying the governed tag and names it `rgn`. `USING COLUMNS (rgn)` then passes that column to the row filter UDF. Policies must use governed tags.\n`WHEN ... ON COLUMN`: `WHEN` tests table-level tags, and `ON COLUMN` is how a column mask policy picks the masked column.\n`FOR COLUMNS TAGGED`: not ABAC policy syntax.\n`MATCH TABLES`: not valid; columns are matched with `MATCH COLUMNS` and table tags with `WHEN`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/policies"],
["P189", 6, "After an ABAC policy is attached to a schema, a Lakeflow Job on standard compute with Databricks Runtime 15.4 LTS can no longer read its tables. What resolves this?",
 ["Move the job to Runtime 16.4+ or serverless, or add its principal to the policy's EXCEPT clause", "Grant the job's principal MANAGE on each table covered by the policy", "Switch the job to dedicated access mode on the same runtime", "Remove the governed tags from the tables while the job runs"],
 "ABAC-secured tables can only be read from standard or dedicated compute on Databricks Runtime 16.4 or above, or serverless. Upgrade the job's compute, or if it must stay old, scope the policy and add the job's principal to its EXCEPT clause.\n`MANAGE on each table`: the block comes from the runtime version, not missing privileges.\n`dedicated access mode on the same runtime`: dedicated compute below 16.4 can't read these tables either.\n`Remove the governed tags`: switches governance off for everyone, not a fix.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/requirements"],
["P190", 6, "Two ABAC column mask policies on the same schema both match the `email` column for the same user, and they call different masking UDFs. What happens when that user queries the table?",
 ["Access is blocked and the query returns an error", "The more restrictive of the two masks is applied", "The policy created most recently takes precedence", "Both masks are applied one after the other"],
 "For a given user and column, only one distinct column mask may resolve. If two policies apply different UDFs, Databricks doesn't pick one; it blocks access with an error. Matches are allowed only when they resolve to the same UDF and arguments.\n`more restrictive mask applied`: Databricks doesn't judge which mask is stricter.\n`most recent policy wins`: creation order doesn't decide conflicts.\n`both masks applied in sequence`: masks don't chain.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/requirements"],

// Objective: anonymization and pseudonymization
["P191", 6, "Analysts must join two tables on customer email but must never see real email addresses. Which column mask design supports the join?",
 ["A DETERMINISTIC UDF returning `SHA2(CONCAT(val, CAST(version AS STRING)), 256)`", "A UDF that returns the constant `'***'` for every non-admin user", "A UDF that returns `uuid()` in place of each email value", "A UDF that returns NULL for every user outside the admins group"],
 "A deterministic hash always maps the same email to the same output, so two masked columns still match in a join while real addresses stay hidden. Including a version value through USING COLUMNS lets you rotate the hash.\n`constant '***'`: every row gets the same value, so the join matches everything with everything.\n`uuid()`: a new random value each time, so the same email never matches.\n`NULL`: NULLs never match in a join, so nothing joins.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/common-patterns"],
["P192", 6, "A column mask on a DOUBLE `salary` column returns the exact value to HR, `ROUND(salary / 1000) * 1000` to managers, and should hide it from everyone else. Which statement about this mask is correct?",
 ["Every branch must return a value castable to DOUBLE, so `'CONFIDENTIAL'` would fail", "Masks always return STRING, so the salary is cast to STRING automatically", "Any return type works because masks are applied after the query finishes", "Rounding isn't allowed in masks; only constants and NULL are allowed"],
 "A column mask's return value must match, or be castable to, the column's type, here DOUBLE. Rounding to the nearest thousand is a valid way to generalize, and a fallback like `0.0` or NULL keeps the type valid, but the string `'CONFIDENTIAL'` can't be cast to DOUBLE.\n`always return STRING`: masks return the column's type, not STRING.\n`applied after the query finishes`: masks apply when the column is read, so the type matters for the rest of the query.\n`Rounding isn't allowed`: masks can run any expression, including rounding.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/common-patterns"],
["P193", 6, "A customer makes a GDPR right-to-be-forgotten request. A colleague suggests applying a hashing column mask to their PII instead of deleting it. What does Databricks guidance say?",
 ["Delete the data, since complete erasure is the safest option", "Masking is enough, since masked values are never stored in files", "Hashing is enough if it uses SHA-256 with a version argument", "Masking is enough if time travel is disabled on the table"],
 "Databricks lists pseudonymization and masking as privacy techniques, but for erasure requests it calls complete deletion the safest option. Masking or hashing still leaves the original values stored and carries a reidentification risk.\n`masked values are never stored`: the reverse; masks change what queries return, and the raw values stay in the files.\n`SHA-256 with a version`: a strong hash is still pseudonymization, not erasure.\n`time travel disabled`: the current files still hold the raw data.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/security/privacy/gdpr-delta"],

// Objective: compliant PII pipeline across batch and streaming
["P194", 6, "A company wants PII columns in new tables, including streaming tables loaded by Lakeflow pipelines, to be detected and masked across a catalog without manual work per table. Which approach fits?",
 ["Data Classification with auto-tagging, plus an ABAC mask policy matching the `class.*` tags", "Table-level masks added by each owner after a monthly manual review", "A dynamic view per table that hashes every string column", "Change data feed with a filter that drops rows containing PII"],
 "Data Classification scans tables, streaming tables and materialized views across a catalog, detects sensitive data and applies governed `class.*` tags. An ABAC mask policy that matches those tags then masks every tagged column automatically, including in new tables.\n`manual review`: the opposite of no manual work, and new tables stay exposed until reviewed.\n`dynamic view per table`: per-table work, and hashing every string column hides non-PII too.\n`change data feed`: tracks row changes; it doesn't detect or mask PII.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/data-classification"],
["P195", 6, "Data Classification is enabled on a catalog. Where are its results stored, and how soon are newly created tables usually scanned?",
 ["`system.data_classification.results`; new tables are usually scanned within 24 hours", "`system.data_classification.results`; new tables are scanned as part of each write", "`system.access.pii_detections`; new tables are usually scanned within 24 hours", "`system.access.pii_detections`; new tables are scanned only on manual full scans"],
 "Data Classification writes its results, including sample values, to `system.data_classification.results`. Scanning is incremental, and new tables and columns are usually scanned within 24 hours.\n`scanned as part of each write`: scanning runs in the background, not inside writes.\n`system.access.pii_detections`: not a system table.\n`only on manual full scans`: not a real table, and new tables are scanned automatically.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/data-classification"],
["P196", 6, "A Lakeflow pipeline builds a materialized view from a source table protected by an ABAC column mask. The pipeline's run-as identity is subject to that mask. What is the result, and the recommended fix?",
 ["The view stores masked data permanently; add the run-as identity to EXCEPT on source policies", "The view stores unmasked data; add a column mask to the view as well", "The refresh fails; grant the run-as identity MANAGE on the source table", "The view stores unmasked data; run the pipeline on dedicated compute"],
 "A materialized view refresh reads the source as the pipeline owner or run-as identity. If a mask applies to that identity, the masked values are what get stored, permanently. Adding the identity to the EXCEPT clause of the source policies lets the refresh see real data.\n`stores unmasked data; add a mask on the view`: the refresh sees masked data, so the view isn't unmasked.\n`refresh fails; grant MANAGE`: the refresh doesn't fail, and MANAGE doesn't exempt anyone from a mask.\n`dedicated compute`: the compute type doesn't change whose identity the mask is evaluated for.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/requirements"],
["P197", 6, "A streaming table in a Lakeflow pipeline has a column mask that calls `is_account_group_member('hr')`. How is that function evaluated?",
 ["As the pipeline owner during refresh, and as the querying user at query time", "As the querying user both during refresh and at query time", "As the pipeline owner both during refresh and at query time", "As the workspace admin during refresh, and as the owner at query time"],
 "Row filter and column mask functions on pipeline tables run with the pipeline owner's rights while the pipeline refreshes. When someone queries the table, user-context functions like `is_account_group_member` are evaluated for that querying user.\n`querying user during refresh too`: no user is querying during a refresh; it runs as the owner.\n`pipeline owner at query time too`: then every reader would get the owner's view.\n`workspace admin during refresh`: refreshes run as the pipeline owner, not an admin.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/unity-catalog"],

// Objective: data purging for retention and erasure
["P198", 6, "A Delta table with deletion vectors enabled holds a customer's PII that must be erased physically. Which sequence removes it from storage?",
 ["DELETE the rows, run `REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)`, then VACUUM", "DELETE the rows, then run VACUUM with default settings only", "DELETE the rows, then run `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY (customer_id)`", "Run `REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)` first, then DELETE the rows"],
 "With deletion vectors, DELETE only marks rows as removed; the data stays in the Parquet files. `REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)` rewrites the files without those rows, and VACUUM, after the retention period, deletes the old files that still hold the data.\n`VACUUM with default settings only`: the files with the deleted rows are still current, so VACUUM keeps them.\n`OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY`: only rewrites files it chooses to compact, and old files remain until VACUUM.\n`REORG first, then DELETE`: there is nothing to purge before the delete.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/security/privacy/gdpr-delta"],
["P199", 6, "A streaming table reads from a Delta source. To meet an erasure request, user rows are deleted from both the source and the streaming table with DML. How should the streaming read change so it keeps running without a full refresh?",
 ["Add `.option('skipChangeCommits', 'true')` to the streaming read", "Add `.option('readChangeFeed', 'true')` to the streaming read", "Add `.option('ignoreDeletes', 'false')` to the streaming read", "Set `startingVersion` to the version of the delete commit"],
 "A streaming read from Delta expects append-only input, so a commit that updates or deletes rows makes it fail. `skipChangeCommits` tells the stream to ignore those commits and keep reading new inserts, avoiding a full refresh that could lose data past upstream retention.\n`readChangeFeed`: switches to reading change records, including deletes, which changes what the stream outputs.\n`ignoreDeletes = false`: that is the default, so nothing changes.\n`startingVersion`: only applies to a new stream, and later change commits would still break it.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/security/privacy/gdpr-delta"],
["P200", 6, "A team deletes user records from a Delta table and runs `VACUUM` with default settings. Which files does VACUUM remove?",
 ["Files no longer referenced by the table that were removed over 7 days ago", "All files not referenced by the current version, regardless of age", "Files no longer referenced by the table that were removed over 30 days ago", "Only transaction log entries that are older than 30 days"],
 "VACUUM deletes data files the table no longer references once they have been removed for longer than the retention threshold, 7 days by default via `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration`. After that you can't time travel to versions older than the retention period.\n`regardless of age`: the threshold protects recent versions and running readers.\n`over 30 days`: 30 days is the default of `delta.logRetentionDuration`, which keeps transaction log history, not data files.\n`transaction log entries`: VACUUM removes data files; log clean-up happens separately at checkpoints.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/delta-vacuum"]
);


PBANK.push(
// ---------- Added 2026-11-25: streaming ----------
["P201", 1, "A team runs a Lakeflow pipeline but plans to rewrite one flow as a notebook Structured Streaming job, because each micro-batch must run a custom `MERGE` into an external Delta table and then call a REST API. What should they consider first?",
 ["A `@dp.foreach_batch_sink` fed by an `@dp.append_flow` runs custom per-batch Python inside the pipeline", "A `dp.create_sink()` Delta sink with `MERGE` options set runs the custom logic per batch", "An `AUTO CDC` flow can target the external table and call the API from its `sequence_by` expression", "A materialized view over the source can run the `MERGE` and API call on each refresh"],
 "Lakeflow pipelines have a ForEachBatch sink: define it with `@dp.foreach_batch_sink`, feed it with an `@dp.append_flow`, and each micro-batch runs your own Python, such as a custom merge or a call to several destinations. You don't need to leave the pipeline for this.\n`dp.create_sink()`: creates a built-in sink with a fixed format (Kafka or Delta). It writes rows; it can't run arbitrary per-batch code.\n`AUTO CDC`: can only target streaming tables, and `sequence_by` just orders change events.\n`materialized view`: is a batch flow defined by its query; it has no hook for custom per-batch side effects.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/for-each-batch"],
["P202", 1, "A data team maintains 25 interdependent bronze, silver and gold streaming notebooks, each a Lakeflow Jobs task. They spend a lot of time ordering tasks by hand and writing retry logic. Which change best reduces this operational work?",
 ["Move the tables into a Lakeflow pipeline, which infers the dependency order and retries at task, flow and pipeline level", "Keep the notebooks and switch every stream to `trigger(availableNow=True)` so tasks finish in order", "Keep the notebooks and point all streams at one shared checkpoint so they recover together", "Keep the notebooks and set `spark.sql.streaming.multipleWatermarkPolicy` to `max` across the job"],
 "Pipelines work out the dependency order from the table definitions, run steps with maximum parallelism, and retry at three levels: Spark task, flow and whole pipeline. That removes the hand-built ordering and retry logic.\n`availableNow`: changes how each stream is triggered (process what's there, then stop). It does nothing about task ordering or retries.\n`one shared checkpoint`: each query needs its own checkpoint directory; sharing one breaks recovery.\n`multipleWatermarkPolicy`: sets how one query combines several watermarks, and `max` drops data from slower streams. Unrelated to orchestration.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/"],
["P203", 1, "A card fraud model must score each transaction from Kafka and respond within tens of milliseconds. The current micro-batch stream on a `processingTime` trigger takes several seconds end to end. What fits this requirement?",
 ["Real-time mode for Structured Streaming, with dedicated compute that has enough task slots", "The `continuous` trigger, which Databricks recommends for millisecond-level latency", "A materialized view on serverless with incremental refresh, run on a tight schedule", "A `processingTime` trigger of `0 seconds` so that micro-batches start back to back"],
 "Real-time mode is a Structured Streaming trigger built for sub-second latency, down to a few milliseconds. It needs dedicated compute with at least as many task slots as the tasks of all stages in a batch.\n`continuous` trigger: an experimental Spark OSS feature that Databricks doesn't support; the docs point you to real-time mode instead.\n`materialized view`: refresh latency is seconds to minutes, so it isn't built for low-latency use.\n`processingTime 0 seconds`: this is the default trigger. It's still micro-batch, which is the multi-second behaviour they already have.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/real-time"],
["P204", 1, "A streaming aggregation writes running counts per device to a Unity Catalog managed table using `.outputMode('update')`. The query fails. What is the cause?",
 ["Delta Lake supports the append and complete output modes but not update mode", "Update mode needs a watermark, and without one Delta rejects the write", "Update mode is only allowed for stream-stream joins, not for aggregations", "Managed tables accept only complete mode for any query with an aggregation"],
 "The output mode has to be one the sink supports. Delta Lake, which backs every Unity Catalog managed table, supports append and complete but not update, so the write fails.\n`needs a watermark`: update mode writes rows as they're calculated and doesn't need a watermark. The failure is the sink, not the missing watermark.\n`only for stream-stream joins`: it's the other way round. Joins support only append, while aggregations can use update.\n`only complete mode`: managed tables also accept append, for example an aggregation with a watermark.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/output-mode"],
["P205", 1, "An inner stream-stream join of `impressions` and `clicks` sets a watermark on both inputs and joins only on `adId = clickAdId`. State keeps growing for days. What else is needed so Spark can drop old state?",
 ["A time-range join condition, such as `clickTime BETWEEN impressionTime AND impressionTime + INTERVAL 1 HOUR`", "Switching the query to complete output mode so that the join state is rewritten each trigger", "Calling `dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark` on both inputs before the join is applied", "Setting `spark.sql.streaming.multipleWatermarkPolicy` to `max` so the faster stream wins"],
 "Watermarks say how late each input can be, but Spark also needs an event-time constraint linking the two sides, such as a time-range condition or a join on event-time windows. Without it, a row could always match something in the future, so state is never dropped.\n`complete mode`: stream-stream joins support only append mode.\n`dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark`: removes duplicate input rows. It doesn't tell the join when old rows can no longer match.\n`multipleWatermarkPolicy max`: only changes which watermark is global, and drops data from the slower stream. The missing time bound is still missing.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html"],
["P206", 1, "A producer retries failed sends, so the same `event_id` can arrive twice, and each retry is stamped with a new `event_time` up to 30 minutes later. Which deduplication keeps state bounded and removes these duplicates?",
 ["`withWatermark('event_time', '30 minutes').dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark(['event_id'])`", "`withWatermark('event_time', '30 minutes').dropDuplicates(['event_id', 'event_time'])`", "`dropDuplicates(['event_id'])` on the stream with no watermark defined on `event_time`", "`withWatermark('event_time', '30 minutes').distinct()` applied before the write to Delta"],
 "`dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark` deduplicates on the ID alone and keeps each ID in state for the watermark delay. That catches duplicates even when their event times differ, and the watermark keeps state bounded.\n`dropDuplicates(['event_id', 'event_time'])`: each retry has a new `event_time`, so it counts as a different key and the duplicate survives.\n`dropDuplicates(['event_id'])` with no watermark: removes the duplicates but keeps every ID in state forever.\n`distinct()`: compares all columns, and retries differ in `event_time`, so they aren't removed.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html"],
["P207", 1, "An engineer must restart a production Kafka-to-Delta stream from its existing checkpoint after a code change. Which change is NOT allowed and would need a new checkpoint?",
 ["Adding a second column to the existing `dropDuplicates` call", "Adding a new `filter` before the projection on the stream", "Changing `maxOffsetsPerTrigger` on the Kafka source", "Changing the target topic of a Kafka output sink"],
 "State is checkpointed with a fixed schema, so you can't add, remove or change a stateful operator between restarts. Adding a column to `dropDuplicates` changes the state schema and needs a new checkpoint.\n`Adding a filter`: adding or removing filters is an allowed change.\n`maxOffsetsPerTrigger`: adding, removing or changing a rate limit on the source is allowed.\n`target topic of a Kafka sink`: changing the output topic is allowed. The new topic just gets data from that point on.",
 "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html"],
["P208", 1, "A stateful aggregation on Databricks Runtime 18 LTS with RocksDB was started with 50 shuffle partitions and is now under-parallelised. The engineer raised `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` to 400 and restarted, but nothing changed. How can parallelism be raised without losing state?",
 ["Set `spark.sql.streaming.stateStore.partitions` to 400 and restart the query on the same checkpoint", "Delete the checkpoint's offsets folder so the new shuffle partition count is picked up", "Set `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` to `auto` so that AQE resizes the existing state", "Start the query with a new checkpoint location, which keeps the existing aggregation state"],
 "The number of state partitions is fixed when the checkpoint is created. Queries with an existing checkpoint ignore a new `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`. On Runtime 18 LTS+ with RocksDB, set `spark.sql.streaming.stateStore.partitions` and restart: state is repartitioned and kept.\n`Delete the offsets folder`: the documented options are state repartitioning or a new checkpoint, not editing checkpoint files.\n`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = auto`: still a shuffle-partitions change, which existing checkpoints ignore.\n`new checkpoint location`: this does apply the new count, but the query restarts with no state, so the state is lost.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/state-repartitioning"],
["P209", 1, "One stream uses `foreachBatch` to write each micro-batch to three different Delta tables. Batch latency keeps rising as volume grows. What does Databricks recommend?",
 ["Use separate streaming writers, one per target table, each with its own checkpoint", "Keep `foreachBatch` and cache the batch DataFrame so the three writes run in parallel", "Keep `foreachBatch` and switch the stream to `trigger(availableNow=True)` to batch writes", "Use one streaming writer in complete mode so that all three tables are rewritten together"],
 "`foreachBatch` runs the writes in each micro-batch one after another, so three targets mean three serial writes per batch. Databricks recommends a separate streaming writer per target, each with its own checkpoint, for parallelism and throughput.\n`cache the batch`: avoids recomputing the input, but the three writes still run in series.\n`availableNow`: changes when the stream runs (process what's there, then stop). Writes inside each batch are still serial.\n`complete mode`: one writer has one sink, so it can't feed three tables. Complete mode also only works with aggregations.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/foreach"],
["P210", 1, "A Lakeflow pipeline writes to an external table through a ForEachBatch sink. After an engineer runs a full refresh, the external table contains every record twice. Why?",
 ["A full refresh resets the flow's checkpoint, so batch IDs restart at 0 and the sink's external data is not cleaned up", "A full refresh replays only the last batch, and ForEachBatch sinks have no idempotent write option", "ForEachBatch sinks keep their checkpoint on full refresh but the target table's history is reset", "A full refresh switches ForEachBatch sinks to complete mode, which appends the whole result again"],
 "On a full refresh, the flow's checkpoint is reset and batch IDs start again at 0. The pipeline doesn't know what your sink code wrote externally, so it can't clean it up. Truncate or drop the external target yourself before re-running.\n`replays only the last batch`: a full refresh reprocesses all source records. Idempotent Delta writes are possible with `txnAppId` and `txnVersion`.\n`keeps their checkpoint`: the docs say the checkpoint directory is reset.\n`complete mode`: pipelines expose only append and update flows, and the duplicates come from the reset, not a mode switch.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/for-each-batch"],
["P211", 1, "A streaming table `all_orders` is defined as a `UNION` of Kafka topics for three regions. A fourth region is launching, and history from the existing regions must not be reprocessed. How should the new topic be added?",
 ["Add an `@dp.append_flow` that reads the new topic and targets `all_orders`", "Add the topic to the `UNION` and run a selective refresh of `all_orders` only", "Redefine `all_orders` as a materialized view that reads all four topics", "Add an `AUTO CDC` flow for the new topic that targets `all_orders`"],
 "Append flows let several streaming sources write into one streaming table. Each flow has its own checkpoint, so a new source starts reading without touching what's already in the table. The docs recommend this over `UNION` for adding sources without a full refresh.\n`UNION plus selective refresh`: selective refresh only picks which tables update. Changing the defining `UNION` is the change that append flows are meant to avoid.\n`materialized view`: replaces the streaming table and recomputes from the sources, which is the reprocessing they want to avoid.\n`AUTO CDC flow`: for change events, and a streaming table targeted by AUTO CDC accepts only other AUTO CDC flows.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/flow-examples"],
["P212", 1, "During a tidy-up, an engineer renames an `@dp.append_flow` from `orders_eu` to `eu_orders_flow` without changing its query. What happens on the next pipeline update?",
 ["The renamed flow is treated as a new flow with no checkpoint, so it reads its source again from the start", "The checkpoint follows the flow by its target table, so the flow resumes where it stopped", "The pipeline fails validation because flow names cannot be changed after the first update", "The pipeline triggers a full refresh of the target table to rebuild the flow's checkpoint"],
 "A flow's name identifies its streaming checkpoint. Rename it and the old checkpoint doesn't carry over: the renamed flow is a new flow, so it reads its source from the beginning.\n`resumes where it stopped`: checkpoints are tracked per flow, not per target table.\n`fails validation`: renaming isn't blocked. The rule is that you can't reuse a flow name with a different definition.\n`triggers a full refresh`: nothing clears the target table. The new flow simply starts with no checkpoint, which is how duplicates can appear.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/flow-examples"],
["P213", 1, "A bronze streaming table ingests new files with Auto Loader. Five years of archived files in another folder must be loaded into the same table one time, without a full refresh. Which approach fits?",
 ["Add an append flow with `once=True` (SQL `INSERT INTO ONCE`) that reads the archive folder", "Set `cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles` to `true` on the existing flow and run an update", "Run a full refresh of the streaming table after adding the archive path to its source", "Create a materialized view over the archive folder and union it into the streaming table"],
 "An append flow with `once=True` (SQL `INSERT INTO ONCE`) runs a one-time batch backfill into the streaming table and then goes idle. The incremental Auto Loader flow keeps running. A full refresh would run the backfill again, so plan for possible duplicates.\n`includeExistingFiles`: only read the first time a stream starts. Changing it later does nothing.\n`full refresh`: reprocesses every source and clears the table, which is what they want to avoid.\n`materialized view unioned in`: streaming tables accept append and AUTO CDC flows, not materialized view flows.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/flows-backfill"],
["P214", 1, "A streaming table reads a Kafka topic that has 7 days of retention and holds two years of history. An engineer proposes a full refresh to apply a new transformation to all rows. What is the main risk?",
 ["Records no longer present in Kafka are dropped from the table, losing everything older than 7 days", "The table is rebuilt from its Delta history, so the new transformation applies only to new rows", "Kafka offsets are kept, so the refresh reads nothing and the table is left empty until new data", "The refresh fails because full refresh is not supported for streaming tables with Kafka sources"],
 "A full refresh clears the streaming table and its checkpoints, then reprocesses the source from scratch. Anything Kafka no longer holds can't be read again, so only about the last 7 days come back. Use `pipelines.reset.allowed = false` to protect tables like this.\n`rebuilt from Delta history`: a full refresh rebuilds from the source, not from the table's own history.\n`Kafka offsets are kept`: checkpoints, including offsets, are cleared, so the refresh reads whatever Kafka still retains.\n`fails`: full refresh is supported for these tables. The danger is that it succeeds and loses data.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/updates"],
["P215", 1, "A gold table sums revenue per customer from `silver.orders`. Orders in silver are regularly corrected with `UPDATE`s and removed with `DELETE`s, and the totals must reflect those changes. Which dataset type should the gold table be?",
 ["A materialized view, which reflects updates and deletes in its source", "A streaming table reading `silver.orders` with `skipChangeCommits`", "A streaming table with an append flow from `silver.orders`", "A temporary view that is evaluated only within the pipeline"],
 "A materialized view gives the correct result as of each refresh, so updates and deletes in `silver.orders` show up in the totals. Databricks recommends it when the source changes rather than only growing.\n`skipChangeCommits`: makes the stream ignore the update and delete commits, so corrections never reach the totals.\n`append flow`: still a streaming table, which expects an append-only source and processes each row once.\n`temporary view`: isn't persisted, so other jobs and queries can't read it as a gold table.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/"],
["P216", 1, "A large materialized view on a serverless pipeline must only ever refresh incrementally. If a query change makes it non-incrementalizable, the team wants the refresh to fail rather than silently run an expensive full recompute. What should they set?",
 ["`REFRESH POLICY INCREMENTAL STRICT`", "`REFRESH POLICY INCREMENTAL`", "`REFRESH POLICY AUTO`", "`pipelines.reset.allowed = false`"],
 "`INCREMENTAL STRICT` makes incremental refresh mandatory. If the plan can't be incrementalized, the create or refresh fails instead of quietly doing a full recompute.\n`INCREMENTAL`: prefers incremental refresh but falls back to a full refresh when the plan no longer supports it, which is exactly the quiet cost they want to avoid.\n`AUTO`: the default, where Databricks picks the cheaper of incremental and full refresh.\n`pipelines.reset.allowed = false`: blocks user-requested full refreshes. It doesn't control how an ordinary refresh is computed.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/incremental-refresh"],
["P217", 1, "Auto Loader runs with `cloudFiles.cleanSource` set to `MOVE`, and `cloudFiles.cleanSource.moveDestination` points to an `archive/` folder inside the source directory. What will happen?",
 ["Archived files are ingested again, because the destination must not be a child of the source directory", "Files are moved as soon as each batch commits, ignoring the retention duration for `MOVE` mode", "The stream fails at start because `MOVE` requires the destination to be in a different bucket", "Files are moved, and Auto Loader skips the `archive/` folder because it already processed them"],
 "The move destination must not be inside the source directory. Auto Loader still watches that path, so archived files look like new files and get ingested again. Use a folder outside the source, but in the same external location or volume.\n`ignoring the retention duration`: files become candidates only after `cleanSource.retentionDuration`, and the earliest is two runs later. `MOVE` just has no minimum duration.\n`different bucket`: it's the opposite. Cross-bucket moves aren't supported, and the destination must be in the same location.\n`skips the archive folder`: it doesn't, which is why the docs forbid a child path.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/clean-source"],
["P218", 1, "An Auto Loader stream uses file events on its external location and runs with `availableNow` only once a month. Each run starts with a slow, costly listing of the whole directory. What should the team change?",
 ["Run the stream at least every 7 days so that it can keep reading from file events", "Set `cloudFiles.backfillInterval` to `1 day` so the listing is spread across the month", "Switch to directory listing mode, which avoids a full listing on each monthly run", "Set `cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles` to `false` so earlier files are not listed again"],
 "With file events, Auto Loader reads new-file information from the event service between runs. If the stream goes more than 7 days without running, it falls back to a full directory listing, so it should run at least weekly.\n`backfillInterval`: Databricks says not to set it with file events. It's for completeness with classic file notification mode.\n`directory listing mode`: lists the input directory on each run, so it doesn't avoid the listing cost.\n`includeExistingFiles = false`: only read when a stream first starts, so it has no effect on an existing stream.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/production"],
// ---------- Added 2026-11-25: ingest_monitor ----------
["P219", 2, "A Kafka stream has run for a month with `startingOffsets` set to `latest`. The team now wants to backfill from the start of the topic, so they change the option to `earliest` and restart the query with the same checkpoint. What happens?",
 ["The query resumes from the offsets in its checkpoint and the new value is ignored", "The query rereads every partition from the earliest retained offset and appends duplicates", "The query fails at startup because the option conflicts with the stored checkpoint offsets", "The query rereads only partitions whose earliest offset is older than the checkpoint"],
 "`startingOffsets` is read only when a query starts with a new checkpoint. A resumed query always continues from the offsets stored in its checkpoint, so a backfill needs a new checkpoint (and a target that can absorb the replay).\n`rereads every partition from the earliest`: that is what a brand-new query with `earliest` does; this one resumes from its checkpoint.\n`fails at startup`: the changed value isn't validated against the checkpoint, it is simply ignored.\n`rereads only partitions`: the only automatic earliest read is for partitions added while a query runs, not for existing ones.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/spark/api-options"],
["P220", 2, "A Kafka ingestion stream was stopped for two weeks. On restart it fails because the topic's retention has already removed offsets it had not read. The business accepts the gap and wants the stream running again from what is still available. What should the engineer do?",
 ["Set `failOnDataLoss` to `false` so the query skips the missing data and continues", "Set `startingOffsets` to `earliest` so the query restarts from the oldest offset", "Set `maxOffsetsPerTrigger` so the query reads the remaining backlog in smaller batches", "Set `kafka.group.id` to a new value so the broker assigns fresh committed offsets"],
 "`failOnDataLoss` defaults to `true`, so the query fails when data might have been lost, for example through offset truncation. Setting it to `false` tells the reader to skip the missing data and carry on.\n`startingOffsets` set to `earliest`: a resumed query always uses its checkpoint, so this option is ignored on restart.\n`maxOffsetsPerTrigger`: caps micro-batch size; it does nothing about offsets that no longer exist.\n`kafka.group.id`: the consumer group ID doesn't decide where a resumed query reads from; the checkpoint does, and sharing group IDs can make queries interfere.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/spark/api-options"],
["P221", 2, "A new Kafka stream starts against a topic holding several days of backlog. The first micro-batch tries to read all of it and the cluster runs out of memory. Which reader option caps how much each micro-batch reads?",
 ["`maxOffsetsPerTrigger`, which is spread proportionally across topic partitions", "`minOffsetsPerTrigger`, which waits until a set number of offsets is available", "`maxFilesPerTrigger`, which limits how many new files each micro-batch reads", "`maxTriggerDelay`, which limits how long a single micro-batch is allowed to run"],
 "`maxOffsetsPerTrigger` sets the most offsets one trigger may process, split proportionally across the topic's partitions, so a large backlog is worked through in bounded micro-batches.\n`minOffsetsPerTrigger`: sets a minimum to wait for before triggering, the opposite of a cap.\n`maxFilesPerTrigger`: limits files per batch for file and Delta sources; Kafka has no files.\n`maxTriggerDelay`: the longest wait for `minOffsetsPerTrigger` to accumulate, not a limit on batch size or run time.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/spark/api-options"],
["P222", 2, "A platform team must ingest two sources into Unity Catalog streaming tables with as little hand-written streaming code as possible: an Apache Kafka cluster and an Amazon Kinesis stream. Which approach fits?",
 ["Lakeflow Connect's managed connector for Kafka; Structured Streaming or a Lakeflow pipeline for Kinesis", "Lakeflow Connect's managed connectors for both, since both are message bus sources", "Structured Streaming for Kafka, since managed connectors only cover databases and SaaS apps", "Auto Loader for both, reading the topic and the stream through their cloud storage paths"],
 "Lakeflow Connect has managed streaming connectors for Kafka and RabbitMQ that need no streaming code. Sources without one, such as Kinesis, Pub/Sub and Pulsar, are read with Structured Streaming or a Lakeflow pipeline.\n`managed connectors for both`: there is no managed Kinesis connector.\n`Structured Streaming for Kafka`: managed connectors are not limited to databases and SaaS apps; Kafka has one. Direct Structured Streaming suits Kafka when you need custom offset handling or per-batch logic.\n`Auto Loader for both`: Auto Loader ingests files from cloud storage; it does not read from a topic or a stream.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/streaming-overview"],
["P223", 2, "A gold stream reads `silver.customers` with `spark.readStream.table(...)`. The silver job has started using `MERGE`, so rows are now updated and deleted, and the gold stream fails. The gold layer must reflect those updates and deletes. What should the team do?",
 ["Enable change data feed on the silver table and stream its row-level changes", "Set `skipChangeCommits` to `true` so the stream keeps running past the commits", "Set `ignoreChanges` to `true` so rewritten files are re-emitted downstream", "Restart the gold stream after each `MERGE` so it reloads the current snapshot"],
 "A Delta streaming source accepts only appends and throws when existing rows are updated or deleted. To carry inserts, updates and deletes downstream, stream the table's change data feed and apply those row-level changes.\n`skipChangeCommits`: keeps the stream running by ignoring commits that modify rows, so updates and deletes never reach gold.\n`ignoreChanges`: legacy option that re-emits rewritten files with duplicates and does not propagate deletes.\n`Restart the gold stream`: it resumes from the checkpoint and hits the same non-append commit; restarting doesn't turn a MERGE into changes it can apply.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/delta-lake"],
["P224", 2, "A stateful streaming aggregation with a watermark is started against a Delta table that already holds a year of events. Some older events are silently dropped during the first run, although the same data processes correctly when it arrives incrementally. Which option addresses this?",
 ["`withEventTimeOrder`, so the initial snapshot is processed in event-time buckets", "`startingTimestamp`, so the stream skips commits made before the watermark", "`maxBytesPerTrigger`, so the initial snapshot is split into smaller micro-batches", "`skipChangeCommits`, so earlier rewrites of the table are left out of the snapshot"],
 "By default the initial snapshot of a Delta source is processed in file modification order, which can move the watermark ahead and drop older events as late. `withEventTimeOrder` splits the snapshot into event-time buckets so it is processed in time order.\n`startingTimestamp`: skips commits before the timestamp, which throws away the year of history instead of processing it correctly.\n`maxBytesPerTrigger`: makes batches smaller but doesn't change the order files are read in.\n`skipChangeCommits`: ignores commits that modify rows; it has nothing to do with event-time order.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/delta-lake"],
["P225", 2, "An engineer reads CSV files with `mode` set to `DROPMALFORMED` and `columnNameOfCorruptRecord` set to `_corrupt_record`, planning to review bad rows later. The `_corrupt_record` column is always empty. Why?",
 ["Under `DROPMALFORMED` the malformed records are dropped, so the column stays empty", "The column fills only when `rescuedDataColumn` is also set on the same reader", "The column fills only when `badRecordsPath` points to a writable storage path", "Under `DROPMALFORMED` the bad rows are written to `_rescued_data` instead"],
 "`DROPMALFORMED` removes malformed rows from the output, so there is nothing left to put in the corrupt record column; the docs say it stays empty in this mode. Use `PERMISSIVE` (the default) to keep bad rows in `_corrupt_record`.\n`rescuedDataColumn`: a separate column for type and schema mismatches; it doesn't bring back dropped rows.\n`badRecordsPath`: an independent way to write bad record details to files; it doesn't fill the corrupt record column.\n`_rescued_data`: dropped rows go nowhere under `DROPMALFORMED`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/spark/api-options"],
["P226", 2, "A new Kinesis stream is being set up to load everything the stream still retains, not just records that arrive after the query starts. Which reader setting is needed?",
 ["Set `initialPosition` to `trim_horizon` (an alias for `earliest`)", "Leave `initialPosition` unset, since it defaults to the oldest record", "Set `startingOffsets` to `earliest`, as with the Kafka reader", "Set `failOnDataLoss` to `false` so expired shards are read first"],
 "Kinesis `initialPosition` defaults to `latest`, so a new query reads only records arriving after it starts. Setting `trim_horizon` (alias `earliest`) starts from the oldest record the stream still retains; `at_timestamp` is the other option.\n`Leave initialPosition unset`: the default is `latest`, not the oldest record.\n`startingOffsets`: that is the Kafka reader's option, not a Kinesis one.\n`failOnDataLoss`: controls whether the query fails when data may be lost; it doesn't set the start position.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/spark/api-options"],
["P227", 4, "A job's ingestion task is configured with retries. On-call wants an email every time an attempt of that task fails, not only when the whole run finally fails. Where should the failure notification be configured?",
 ["On the task, because job-level notifications are not sent when failed tasks are retried", "On the job, because job-level failure notifications fire once for each failed attempt", "On a SQL alert that polls `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` every few minutes", "On the job with 'Mute notifications until the last retry' turned on for the task"],
 "Job-level notifications aren't sent when failed tasks are retried, so the only built-in way to hear about each failed attempt is a failure notification on the task itself.\n`On the job`: job-level failure notifications don't fire for retried task failures.\n`SQL alert` on `job_run_timeline`: a workaround with its own warehouse, query and schedule to maintain, when a task notification already does this.\n`Mute notifications until the last retry`: does the opposite, hiding task alerts until the final retry.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/notifications"],
["P228", 4, "A job has 'Mute notifications for skipped runs' turned on in its job-level notification settings, but the team still receives emails when runs are skipped. What is the likely cause?",
 ["Notifications set on individual tasks are not filtered by the job-level mute setting", "Skipped runs are treated as failures, which the job-level mute setting never filters", "The mute setting only applies after the job's maximum concurrent runs is raised above 1", "The mute setting only applies to Slack and webhook destinations, not to email addresses"],
 "Muting skipped runs at the job level filters only job-level notifications. Notifications configured on individual tasks are not affected, so they must be muted in each task's notification settings too.\n`Skipped runs are treated as failures`: skipped is its own run outcome with its own mute setting.\n`maximum concurrent runs`: the mute setting has no such dependency.\n`only applies to Slack and webhook`: muting covers notifications regardless of destination, email included.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/notifications"],
["P229", 4, "Anomaly detection is enabled on the `sales` schema, which contains managed tables, standard views and foreign tables from a federated PostgreSQL catalog. Which objects get freshness and completeness checks?",
 ["Only the tables; views and foreign tables are not supported", "All three, since every object in the schema is scanned", "The tables and views, but not the foreign tables", "Only tables with a `MANAGE` grant for the monitoring user"],
 "Anomaly detection monitors the tables in a schema but does not support views or foreign tables, so only the managed tables get freshness and completeness checks.\n`All three`: views and foreign tables are explicitly unsupported.\n`The tables and views`: views are unsupported as well as foreign tables.\n`MANAGE grant`: `MANAGE` on the schema or catalog is needed to enable anomaly detection; it doesn't decide which tables are scanned.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/data-quality-monitoring/anomaly-detection/"],
["P230", 4, "A data steward wants a dashboard of stale and incomplete tables built on anomaly detection results, but queries against `system.data_quality_monitoring.table_results` fail with a permission error. What explains this?",
 ["By default only account admins can access that table, so an admin must grant access", "The table is written only after the steward enables data profiling on each table", "Results are kept in each schema's event log, so the system table stays empty", "The steward needs `MANAGE` on the schema to read rows for the tables in it"],
 "Anomaly detection writes scan results to `system.data_quality_monitoring.table_results`, and by default only account admins can read that table. An admin has to grant access before others can query it or use the dashboard template.\n`data profiling`: a separate feature (formerly Lakehouse Monitoring); anomaly detection results don't depend on it.\n`each schema's event log`: event logs belong to Lakeflow pipelines; anomaly results go to the system table.\n`MANAGE on the schema`: needed to enable anomaly detection and set alerts, not to read the system table.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/data-quality-monitoring/anomaly-detection/results"],
["P231", 4, "An account admin creates a budget filtered to the custom tag `team = marketing`, with a monthly threshold of $10,000 and an email recipient. Which behaviour should the marketing team expect?",
 ["An email after tagged spend at list price passes $10,000, possibly up to a day late, while compute keeps running", "An email as soon as spend passes $10,000 after discounts, and new tagged clusters cannot start", "An email after spend passes $10,000 across the workspace, with tagged jobs paused until next month", "An email when the forecast for tagged spend passes $10,000, using negotiated prices from billing"],
 "A budget filtered by tags tracks only usage carrying those tags, is calculated at list price with no credits or negotiated discounts, and its emails can lag usage by up to 24 hours. Except for Unity Gateway and Genie, budgets only monitor spend.\n`after discounts` and `cannot start`: budgets ignore discounts and don't block compute.\n`across the workspace` with `paused`: the tag filter limits it to tagged usage, and nothing is paused.\n`forecast` with `negotiated prices`: thresholds compare actual monthly spend, at list price.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/account-settings/budgets"],
["P232", 4, "An on-call script needs the runs of job `123` that are still in progress, so it can check whether a backfill is already under way before starting another. Which CLI command returns exactly that?",
 ["`databricks jobs list-runs --job-id 123 --active-only`", "`databricks jobs list-runs --job-id 123 --completed-only`", "`databricks jobs get-run 123 --include-history`", "`databricks jobs list-runs --job-id 123 --run-type JOB_RUN`"],
 "`list-runs` with `--job-id` filters to one job, and `--active-only` keeps only runs still in progress; results come back newest first.\n`--completed-only`: returns finished runs, the opposite of what's needed.\n`get-run` with `--include-history`: takes a run ID, not a job ID, and returns one run's details plus repair history.\n`--run-type JOB_RUN`: filters on how the run was created (job, submit or workflow run), not on whether it is still running.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/reference/jobs-commands"]
);
